    <encodingDesc>
      <classDecl>
        <taxonomy xml:id="tiers">
          <desc>The tiers: how far a file goes, from a minimal file to the full encoding. Every element, attribute
            and rule of the schema belongs to one tier, named by <att>corresp</att> on its specification, or, for an
            element taken from TEI unchanged, by the table of elements beside the specification.</desc>
          <category xml:id="tier-required">
            <catDesc><term>required</term>: what every file has. A file without it fails the checks: the import
              refuses a file that fails the RelaxNG, and reports the Schematron's errors, such as a formal title
              without the document's code.</catDesc>
          </category>
          <category xml:id="tier-common">
            <catDesc><term>common</term>: what most encoders add, as most of the project's coded files
              do.</catDesc>
          </category>
          <category xml:id="tier-full">
            <catDesc><term>full</term>: every other claim about the text, its source or the world, at full depth,
              signed and with its evidence.</catDesc>
          </category>
          <category xml:id="tier-export-only">
            <catDesc><term>export only</term>: the application's own state, which only its exports write, so that
              anyone can read them.</catDesc>
          </category>
        </taxonomy>
      </classDecl>
    </encodingDesc>
  </teiHeader>
  <text>
    <front>
      <!-- The title page of the HTML and the PDF. Its version and date are the release's: the edition's above, and
           the date of its section in the changelog. -->
      <titlePage>
        <docTitle>
          <titlePart type="main">Encoding Early Medieval Canon Law</titlePart>
          <titlePart type="sub">The SOLEMNE Guidelines for TEI</titlePart>
        </docTitle>
        <docAuthor>Gleb Schmidt</docAuthor>
        <docAuthor>Friederike Voit</docAuthor>
        <docAuthor>Sven Meeder</docAuthor>
        <docEdition>Version 0.3.0</docEdition>
        <docDate when="2026-11-05">5 November 2026</docDate>
        <docImprint>SOLEMNE: The Social Life of Early Medieval Normative Texts, Radboud University, Nijmegen; funded by
          the European Research Council, grant 101087979. The guide is under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
          licence; the schema made from it, under the MIT licence.</docImprint>
      </titlePage>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xml:id="introduction">
  <head>About this guide</head>
  <p>This is SOLEMNE's encoding guide: how to encode the texts of early medieval canon law in TEI, so that the
    project's application can import them, and how to read what the application exports. It is also the project's
    TEI schema: one ODD holds the rules and explains them, and the schema that checks files is generated from it. A
    change to a rule is made here, in the prose and the specification together.</p>
  <p>It is written for someone who has never seen the project's corpus: they can encode a text from scratch, as far
    as they can reasonably go, check it, and hand it to the import; and anyone can read what an export holds. SOLEMNE,
    The Social Life of Early Medieval Normative Texts, is a project funded by the European Research Council (grant
    101087979) at Radboud University.</p>
  <p>Its ten parts go from what a file describes to what an export holds: the file and its header, the text, what is
    marked in it, records, relations, dates, manuscripts, editions and literature, and exports. The appendices list
    every element and rule, the code tables and the vocabularies' seeds, older encodings, the import formats' tables,
    and the changes from one version to the next.</p>
  <p>Part VIII's description of manuscripts is modelled on the Bodleian Libraries' <title>TEI P5 Customization and
      Encoding Guidelines for Manuscript Description</title> (Bodleian Library, Oxford, version 1.0, 2020; the
    guidelines for medieval manuscripts by James Freeman and Matthew Holford;
    <ref target="https://github.com/msDesc/consolidated-tei-schema">github.com/msDesc/consolidated-tei-schema</ref>),
    under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 licence. Where this guide quotes them word for word, the quotation is
    introduced as "Oxford encoding guide:".</p>

  <div xml:id="intro-checks">
    <head>Must and should, and the checks</head>
    <p>The guide says <hi>must</hi> for what the checks require, and <hi>should</hi> for what they report or what
      the guide recommends. The schema checks a file twice:</p>
    <list>
      <item><hi>the RelaxNG</hi>: the structure, the elements and attributes the schema allows, the closed lists, the
        forms of pointers and numbers. A file that fails it fails a <hi>must</hi>, and the import refuses it;</item>
      <item><hi>the Schematron</hi>: the rules that the structure can't express, each with its name. An
        <hi>error</hi> is a <hi>must</hi>; a <hi>warning</hi> is a <hi>should</hi>. The import reports both, and
        imports the file.</item>
    </list>
    <p>An export passes both, without a Schematron error. Appendix B lists every rule, with its check, its severity
      and its tier; each part shows a counter-example of each of its rules, a fragment that breaks it.</p>
    <p>To check a file, install the package (its README says how) and run <code>solemne-tei validate
        file.xml</code>: it prints each problem with its line, its check, its severity, its rule and its message;
      <code>--strict</code> makes a Schematron error fail too, as an export must. In Oxygen, a file made from one of
      the templates is checked as it is typed: the template declares the schema by two <code>xml-model</code>
      processing instructions, one for the RelaxNG, with the Schematron inside it, and one for the Schematron, for
      other tools:</p>
    <eg>&lt;?xml-model href="https://solemne-erc.github.io/solemne-tei/schema/0.3.0/solemne.rng"
  type="application/xml" schematypens="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0"?&gt;
&lt;?xml-model href="https://solemne-erc.github.io/solemne-tei/schema/0.3.0/solemne.sch"
  type="application/xml" schematypens="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron"?&gt;</eg>
    <p>The schema is published at its version's address once the release is published; until then, a template is
      checked with <code>solemne-tei validate</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="intro-tiers">
    <head>The boundary and the tiers</head>
    <p>A file may state any scholarly claim about the text, its source or the world, at any depth, signed and with
      its evidence, including pointers to the application's records by permalink, which the import takes as given.
      Facts that tools establish better, codes, the bounds of a date read from its wording, page breaks joined to
      images, the identifiers of a text's levels, a file may give too, and they're checked strictly. The
      application's own state, its nodes' ids, its text versions, change sets, releases, review states and the data
      it computes, only its exports write, and the guide documents it so that anyone can read an export.</p>
    <p>So every element, attribute and rule of the schema has a tier:</p>
    <list>
      <item><hi>required</hi>: what every file has; without it, a file fails the checks;</item>
      <item><hi>common</hi>: what most encoders add, as most of the project's coded files do;</item>
      <item><hi>full</hi>: every other claim, at full depth;</item>
      <item><hi>export only</hi>: the application's state, which its exports write.</item>
    </list>
    <p>Only the required tier is required, so a minimal file, a rich one and an export pass the same checks. Each part
      goes from the minimum to the full form, and says each form's tier; appendices A and B give every element's,
      attribute's and rule's.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="intro-templates">
    <head>The templates</head>
    <p>The templates, in the guide's folder <code>templates</code>, are whole files to start from, each a short real
      text of the corpus, and each checked with the guide's examples:</p>
    <list>
      <item><code>minimal.xml</code>: only the required tier;</item>
      <item><code>rich.xml</code>: every common form for a text, and some of the full ones;</item>
      <item><code>council-edition.xml</code>: a council, from an edition;</item>
      <item><code>collection-edition.xml</code>: a collection, from an edition;</item>
      <item><code>decretal.xml</code>: a decretal;</item>
      <item><code>penitential.xml</code>: a penitential;</item>
      <item><code>manuscript-collection.xml</code>: a collection in one manuscript, with the manuscript's
        description;</item>
      <item><code>reconstruction.xml</code>: a reconstruction, with the witnesses it was made from;</item>
      <item><code>export.xml</code>: an export, as the application writes it, with what it adds to an imported
        file.</item>
    </list>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="intro-principles">
    <head>Principles</head>
    <list type="ordered">
      <item><hi>The model decides.</hi> Where the application's model has a concept, the schema has one form for it;
        where it has none, the schema doesn't invite one: one element or attribute for each thing, one attribute for
        pointing to a record, one form of date.</item>
      <item><hi>One form in and out.</hi> What an export writes, an import reads: an exported file imports again
        without loss. For each form, the guide says what the import makes of it and what an export writes.</item>
      <item><hi>TEI as it is.</hi> The guide restricts TEI and documents it, and adds to it only where TEI has no way
        to say something, saying so: so far, a type on <gi>segmentation</gi>, and a hand's execution on
        <gi>handNote</gi>.</item>
      <item><hi>Closed where the model is fixed, seeded where it grows.</hi> The types the application's code relies
        on are closed lists, which the RelaxNG checks; the vocabularies that grow in the application start from the
        guide's seeds.</item>
      <item><hi>Others' TEI through their own schemas.</hi> The Bodleian's descriptions, SCTA's Gratian, the files
        of the Patrologia Latina and the transcriptions of handwritten text recognition are checked against their
        own schemas and converted, never let in by widening this one.</item>
      <item><hi>Versioned.</hi> Every release says what changed, and whether files that passed may now fail: a major
        version where they may, a minor one for additions, a patch for the prose.</item>
    </list>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="intro-paths">
    <head>Two ways through the guide</head>
    <p><hi>A first file.</hi> To encode a text from scratch, read in this order, starting from the minimal
      template:</p>
    <list type="ordered">
      <item><ptr target="#documents-and-nodes"/> and <ptr target="#levels"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#header-title"/>, <ptr target="#header-categories"/> and <ptr target="#header-codes"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#header-publication"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#text-divisions"/>, <ptr target="#text-headings"/> and <ptr target="#text-numbers"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#transcription-sources"/>, <ptr target="#transcription-page-breaks"/> and <ptr target="#transcription-gaps"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#marked-names"/>, <ptr target="#marked-statements"/>, <ptr target="#marked-pointers"/> and
        <ptr target="#marked-notes"/>, as far as the file goes;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#header-curation"/>; then check the file (<ptr target="#intro-checks"/>).</item>
    </list>
    <p><hi>Reading an export.</hi> To read what the application writes:</p>
    <list type="ordered">
      <item><ptr target="#what-a-file-describes"/>, all of it;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#header-title"/>, for the levels' permalinks, and <ptr target="#header-revisions"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#text"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#marked-in-the-text"/>, with <ptr target="#marked-fragments"/> and <ptr target="#marked-anchors"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#records"/>, <ptr target="#relations"/>, <ptr target="#dates"/> and <ptr target="#editions-and-literature"/>;</item>
      <item><ptr target="#exports"/>, with the template <code>export.xml</code>.</item>
    </list>
  </div>
</div>
    </front>
    <body>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xml:id="what-a-file-describes" n="I">
  <head>What a file describes</head>
  <p>This part says what a file describes, in the terms the rest of the guide uses: documents and their nodes, the
    levels of a text, codes, ids, citations and permalinks, annotations, records and relations. They're the terms of
    the application the files are made for, SOLEMNE's, which imports them and exports them again. A file uses them
    without having to name them.</p>
  <p>Every bearer of the texts of early medieval canon law is described in the same way: a council as an edition
    prints it, a collection as a manuscript gives it, a collection as scholars have reconstructed it. Each keeps its
    own structure and its own text. How they relate, that one collection copies another or that a chapter quotes a
    council, is evidence, stated with its grounds by scholars and found by the application's tools; it isn't the
    frame a file is written in.</p>

  <div xml:id="documents-and-nodes">
    <head>Documents and nodes</head>
    <p>A file describes one document. A document is a tree of nodes, whose root stands for the document itself: the
      file's <gi>body</gi>. There are three kinds of node:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a <term>division</term>, which has a type and holds other nodes, never text;</item>
      <item>a <term>heading</term>, which has a type and a text of its own;</item>
      <item>a <term>paragraph</term>, which holds text.</item>
    </list>
    <p>A division holds headings, and either paragraphs or divisions, never both; its headings stand where the
      source puts them, usually first. A node knows its parent, its place among its siblings, its kind, its type, its
      number (a division's as a value, 49 for "XLVIIII"; the heading keeps what the source writes), its citation and
      its text. <ptr target="#text"/> gives the forms.</p>
    <p>The text of a division that holds no divisions is one unit of reception: what a reader, a search or the
      detection of text reuse takes as one piece. Where a source sets several units side by side without headings,
      each is a division of the type <val>unit</val>. Where an encoder didn't mark the units, the application infers
      them, and the file says so (<ptr target="#header-inferred-units"/>).</p>
    <p>Each version of a node's text has two statuses: its curation, how far it has been checked (<val>high</val>,
      <val>medium</val>, <val>low</val>, <val>unknown</val>), which a file may state (<ptr target="#header-curation"/>); and its completeness, which the application counts from its gaps.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="levels">
    <head>The levels of a text</head>
    <p>A text has levels, after SCTA's model:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a <term>work</term>: a council's canons, a decretal, a collection;</item>
      <item>its <term>versions</term>: a translation, a recension, one of them canonical, each with its
        <term>canons</term>, the units by which it is cited, without text of their own;</item>
      <item>its <term>witnesses</term>: a copy in a manuscript, or an edition in a publication;</item>
      <item>their <term>transcriptions</term>: texts with structure.</item>
    </list>
    <p>A file is the transcription of one witness: of a manuscript's copy, or of an edition. Which version is
      canonical is a default, not a fact. A witness keeps its own structure, which the application maps to its
      version's canons. A manuscript and a publication are records (<ptr target="#records"/>); a witness is "in"
      one of them, at its folios or its pages.</p>
    <p>The Council of Nicaea is a work, <code>C.NIC-0325</code>; its canonical version takes the same code, and its
      third canon is <code>C.NIC-0325_3</code>; the first Dionysiana's translation is another version,
      <code>C.NIC-0325-vDIO1</code>; its edition by Turner is a witness, and the file that transcribes it, of the same
      code, its transcription. A file doesn't encode these levels: its header gives its code, and an export adds the
      permalinks of the levels it belongs to, in its formal title. The application's domain isn't fixed yet; the
      example uses <code>example.org</code>:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <title type="formal">
        <idno>C.NIC-0325-vDIO1</idno>
        <idno type="category">C</idno>
        <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        <idno type="work">https://example.org/id/work/C.NIC-0325</idno>
        <idno type="version">https://example.org/id/version/C.NIC-0325-vDIO1</idno>
        <idno type="witness">https://example.org/id/witness/C.NIC-0325-vDIO1</idno>
        <idno type="transcription">https://example.org/id/transcription/C.NIC-0325-vDIO1</idno>
      </title>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="codes-and-ids">
    <head>Codes and ids</head>
    <p>A document's id is its code (<ptr target="#header-codes"/>), read with its kind: a work takes the code its
      texts share; its canonical version the work's code, and its canons the citations that source notes use
      (<code>C.NIC-0325_3</code>); other versions a suffix (<code>C.NIC-0325-vDIO1</code>); a witness its
      transcription's code. A text of the Patrologia Latina takes <code>.ed-PL</code> and the volume
      (<code>COLL.Burch.ed-PL140</code>), SCTA's Gratian <code>COLL.Grat.ed-SCTA</code>. Codes are valid XML ids and
      identifiers of the Distributed Text Services: a letter first, then letters, digits, <code>.</code> and
      <code>-</code>; <code>_</code> only before a citation. A manuscript's short code is made from its full
      shelfmark: <code>Mun-BSB-4592</code>. A record's id is its kind and a number: <code>PERS.1234</code>.</p>
    <p>A file's own <att>xml:id</att>s are its encoder's, for the pointers inside it: fragments, declarations,
      records listed in the file. The import gives each node an id of its own, for good, which an export writes as
      the node's <att>xml:id</att>. Other projects' ids are kept beside the project's, as external ids (<ptr target="#header-external-ids"/>).</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="citations-and-permalinks">
    <head>Citations and permalinks</head>
    <p>A node's citation is the path of steps from the root to it, joined by dots:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a chapter or a register item by its number, as a value (<code>49</code>); one without a number takes the
        number before it and a letter (<code>11a</code>), and a number repeated takes the next letter;</item>
      <item>a section by its own number or name, or its position;</item>
      <item>the other divisions by a prefix and their position: <code>pr</code> for a preface, <code>cl</code> for a
        closer, <code>sub</code> for subscriptions, <code>r</code> for a register, <code>u</code> for a unit
        (<code>u1</code>).</item>
    </list>
    <p>So the forty-ninth chapter of a collection's second book is <code>2.49</code>, and a pointer to it, the
      document's code and the citation (<code>COLL.Dach.ms-Brn-Burg-425_2.49</code>; <ptr target="#marked-pointers"/>). Readers see the document's own terms: "lib. 2 c. 49", "C. 16 q. 6 c. 2". A
      node keeps its id for good; its citation follows its place.</p>
    <p>A permalink is <code>https://</code>, the application's domain, <code>/id/</code>, the kind and the id: a
      record, a document, a node, a level. A text as it stood at a time adds <code>@</code> and its version
      (<code>…@t3</code>); a release is named by its name. Inside the project only the project's ids are used.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="annotations">
    <head>Annotations</head>
    <p>A node's text is plain text. Everything marked in it is an annotation, anchored to a span of the text, a
      point, the whole node, or other annotations; spans may nest and overlap. Each kind of annotation is a layer,
      which says what an annotation of that kind keeps, and how it's written to TEI. The layers, and the forms a file
      marks them by (<ptr target="#marked-in-the-text"/>, <ptr target="#text-transcription"/>):</p>
    <table xml:id="layers">
      <head>The layers of annotation</head>
      <row role="label">
        <cell>Layer</cell>
        <cell>Marked by</cell>
        <cell>Anchored to</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>person, place, event</cell>
        <cell><gi>persName</gi>, <gi>placeName</gi>, <gi>eventName</gi></cell>
        <cell>a span</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>work named</cell>
        <cell><gi>bibl</gi></cell>
        <cell>a span</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>statement, reference, quotation</cell>
        <cell><gi>cit</gi>, <gi>ref</gi>, <gi>quote</gi></cell>
        <cell>its reference and its quotation; a span each</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>formula</cell>
        <cell><gi>seg</gi> typed <val>formula</val></cell>
        <cell>a span</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>date</cell>
        <cell><gi>date</gi></cell>
        <cell>a span</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>attribution</cell>
        <cell>an inscription; a statement's reference</cell>
        <cell>the inscription; the reference</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>source note, parallel, editorial note</cell>
        <cell><gi>note</gi> typed <val>source</val>, <val>parallel</val>, <val>editorial</val>; <gi>sic</gi></cell>
        <cell>a point, or the node</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>scribal change</cell>
        <cell><gi>add</gi>, <gi>del</gi>, <gi>subst</gi>, <gi>seg</gi> typed <val>corr</val></cell>
        <cell>a span; a point for a deletion</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>unclear, gap</cell>
        <cell><gi>unclear</gi>, <gi>gap</gi></cell>
        <cell>a span; a point</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>page break</cell>
        <cell><gi>pb</gi></cell>
        <cell>a point</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>language</cell>
        <cell><gi>foreign</gi></cell>
        <cell>a span</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>editor's addition; abbreviation and expansion</cell>
        <cell><gi>supplied</gi>; <gi>choice</gi> with <gi>abbr</gi> and <gi>expan</gi></cell>
        <cell>a span</cell>
      </row>
    </table>
    <p>Where a scribe corrected a word, the corrected reading is the text, and the change is an annotation. A note
      outside a division's paragraphs annotates the division; gaps, page breaks and notes between divisions anchor at
      the start of the next node. An annotation that can't nest where it stands is written as fragments, or by
      anchors and a stand-off annotation (<ptr target="#marked-other"/>).</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-and-relations">
    <head>Records, relations, observations and judgements</head>
    <p>A record describes a person, a place, an institution, an event, a manuscript or a publication. A file points
      to records from its names (<ptr target="#marked-names"/>), and may list them in its standOff (<ptr target="#records"/>). A placeholder becomes a provisional record at import. Every record, text and annotation
      names its source: where it comes from, its id there, a link, the date it was retrieved, and its licence.</p>
    <p>A relation is a typed statement from one thing to another, records, nodes or relations, with a type and its
      reverse, a date, a certainty (certain, probable, possible), a role and a place where its type needs them, its
      evidence, and its author. An <term>observation</term> is what a witness shows: that a chapter's inscription
      names the council of Nicaea, that a source note gives a canon. A <term>judgement</term> holds of a version or
      a work, and names its evidence and its method: that a collection draws on the Hispana. A file may state either,
      signed (<ptr target="#relations"/>); an edition's source notes are kept as observations until the project
      resolves them into relations. A family of texts is a view the application works out from the relations; it is
      never encoded.</p>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="file-and-header" n="II">
  <head>The file and its header</head>
  <p>A file is one document: a TEI root, <gi>TEI</gi>, in TEI's namespace, holding a header, <gi>teiHeader</gi>,
    the text, <gi>text</gi>, and, where the file lists records, relations or annotations, a <gi>standOff</gi> after the
    text. The header says what the document is, who made the file and from what, and how the text was encoded. Its
    skeleton is <hi>required</hi>: the title statement with its formal title, the publication statement, and the
    description of the source. The rest is <hi>common</hi> or <hi>full</hi>, as each section says.</p>
  <p>The root needs no id: the document's code, in the formal title, identifies it. A root's <att>xml:id</att> is
    the encoder's, and the import doesn't read it.</p>

  <div xml:id="header-title">
    <head>The title statement</head>
    <p>The title statement (<gi>titleStmt</gi>) opens with the formal title, <gi>title</gi> typed
      <val>formal</val>, which holds the document's identifiers, once each:</p>
    <list>
      <item>its code, an <gi>idno</gi> without a type (<ptr target="#header-codes"/>);</item>
      <item>its category, an <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>category</val>;</item>
      <item>its type, an <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>type</val>.</item>
    </list>
    <p>Its other titles follow: an <val>alias</val>, the title readers know it by, and titles typed
      <val>other</val>, each naming its source in <att>source</att>. The formal title and its three identifiers are
      <hi>required</hi>: the import needs the code for the document's id. The Schematron checks them as errors, which
      the import reports. An alias is <hi>common</hi>; other titles are <hi>full</hi>. The Council of Mainz, 813:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <titleStmt>
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>C.MAI-0813</idno>
          <idno type="category">C</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
        <title type="alias">Council of Mainz, 813</title>
      </titleStmt>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="title-first-formal">
      <titleStmt>
        <title type="alias">Council of Samplia, 600</title>
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
          <idno type="category">C</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
      </titleStmt>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="title-one-formal">
      <titleStmt>
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
          <idno type="category">C</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
        <title type="formal">Council of Samplia</title>
      </titleStmt>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="formal-title-idnos">
      <title type="formal">
        <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
        <idno type="category">C</idno>
      </title>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="title-type">
      <title type="main">Council of Samplia, 600</title>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="title-alias-words">
      <title type="alias"/>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="title-other-source">
      <title type="other">Synodus Sampliensis</title>
    </egXML>
    <p>Identifiers in the title statement stand in the formal title, and are its code, its category, its type, or,
      in an export, the permalinks of the levels the document belongs to: one each for its <val>work</val>, its
      <val>version</val>, its <val>witness</val> and its <val>transcription</val> (<ptr target="#levels"/>). Those
      are <hi>export only</hi>: the application writes them.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="idno-in-formal-title">
      <title type="alias">Council of Samplia, 600 <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno></title>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="idno-type">
      <title type="formal">
        <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
        <idno type="category">C</idno>
        <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        <idno type="shelfmark">MS 12</idno>
      </title>
    </egXML>

    <div xml:id="header-categories">
      <head>Categories and types</head>
      <p>The category says what kind of text the document is. The application keeps the categories as a vocabulary,
        which may grow; the schema checks them against its list, which these seed:</p>
      <list xml:id="category-list">
        <item><val>SYSCOLL</val>: a collection arranged by subject;</item>
        <item><val>CHRCOLL</val>: a collection arranged chronologically;</item>
        <item><val>C</val>: a council;</item>
        <item><val>D</val>: a decretal;</item>
        <item><val>P</val>: a penitential;</item>
        <item><val>H</val>: a homiliary;</item>
        <item><val>EX</val>: exegesis;</item>
        <item><val>EP</val>: a letter, not a pope's;</item>
        <item><val>LR</val>: Roman law, <foreign xml:lang="la">lex Romana</foreign>;</item>
        <item><val>CPT</val>: a capitulary;</item>
        <item><val>CHA</val>: a charter;</item>
        <item><val>UNK</val>: not known.</item>
      </list>
      <p>The type says what the file transcribes:</p>
      <list>
        <item><val>edition</val>: a text as a scholarly edition prints it;</item>
        <item><val>manuscript</val>: a text as a manuscript gives it, transcribed from it; its source description is
          the manuscript's (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>);</item>
        <item><val>reconstruction</val>: a text that scholars have reconstructed from others, such as a collection
          known from parts of other collections;</item>
        <item><val>hypothesis</val>: any text supposed rather than found, less precise than a reconstruction;</item>
        <item><val>placeholder</val>: a text whose content stands in for another's, such as a manuscript's text taken
          for now from the edition that used it.</item>
      </list>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="category-value">
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>CAN.SAM-0600</idno>
          <idno type="category">CAN</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="document-type-value">
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
          <idno type="category">C</idno>
          <idno type="type">copy</idno>
        </title>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="type-manuscript">
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
          <idno type="category">C</idno>
          <idno type="type">manuscript</idno>
        </title>
      </egXML>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="header-codes">
      <head>The coding rules</head>
      <p>A document's code says what the document is: its category, its title, its date, and, where needed, its
        source. Codes are made by the rules below, from the old Solemne guide, revised. The Schematron checks the
        code's form and its prefix, that a manuscript's code goes with the manuscript's description, and that a file
        named by a code is named by its own; the rest of the rules are the project's practice, explained here and
        not checked. A tool that proposes codes may come later.</p>
      <p>A code has up to three parts, joined by dots:</p>
      <list>
        <item><hi>The category's prefix:</hi> the category itself (<code>C</code>, <code>D</code>, <code>P</code>,
          <code>H</code>, <code>EX</code>, <code>EP</code>, <code>LR</code>, <code>CPT</code>, <code>CHA</code>),
          and <code>COLL</code> for both kinds of collection. A document of an unknown category takes any
          prefix.</item>
        <item><hi>The title part,</hi> its subparts joined by hyphens:
          <list>
            <item>the title: for a council, three capitals for its place (<code>C.MAI</code> for Mainz); for a
              decretal, three for its pope (<code>D.LEO</code>), with <code>Ps</code> before a forged decretal's
              pope (<code>D.PsCAL</code>); for a collection or a penitential, its name or an abbreviation of it
              (<code>COLL.Dach</code>, <code>COLL.400</code>, <code>P.HAL</code>); <code>UNK</code> where the title
              isn't known;</item>
            <item>the date, where wanted: four digits, the year (<code>C.MAI-0813</code>); the latest possible year,
              where the date is a range; <code>9999</code> where the date isn't known (<code>EP.LupEuf-9999</code>);</item>
            <item>a further identifier, where needed: a decretal's number in Jaffé's register, in Kaltenbrunner's
              numbering (<code>D.LEO-0447-414</code>); a version, <code>-v</code> and a name
              (<code>C.NIC-0325-vDIO1</code>, Nicaea in the first Dionysiana; <code>D.LEO-0446-410-v1</code>).</item>
          </list></item>
        <item><hi>The source part,</hi> where the code would otherwise be another document's: <code>ms-</code> and
          the manuscript's short code, for every transcription of a manuscript
          (<code>COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592</code>); <code>ed-</code> and the edition, where the corpus holds two
          editions of one text: the volume of the Patrologia Latina (<code>COLL.Burch.ed-PL140</code>), SCTA's
          Gratian (<code>COLL.Grat.ed-SCTA</code>).</item>
      </list>
      <p>A manuscript's short code joins, by hyphens, its city in three letters, its library's abbreviation and its
        shelfmark: <code>Mun-BSB-4592</code> for Munich, Bayerische Staatsbibliothek, Clm 4592. A code is a valid
        <att>xml:id</att>: ASCII letters and digits joined by dots and hyphens, without spaces, accents or
        parentheses, so a former shelfmark has no place in it. An underscore stands only in a pointer, before a
        citation (<ptr target="#marked-pointers"/>).</p>
      <p>No file of the corpus has the categories <code>LR</code>, <code>CPT</code> or <code>CHA</code> yet. Their
        codes follow the general rule, with their own prefixes; how their title parts are made, the guide will say
        with the first files.</p>
      <p>The Schematron checks the form: the prefix and its dot; the title part, whose subparts, the date among them,
        are joined by hyphens; and any source part after a dot, which begins with a letter. A code with an
        underscore fails, and so does a date joined by a dot (<code>P.SAM.0700</code> for
        <code>P.SAM-0700</code>):</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="code-format">
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>C.SAM_0600</idno>
          <idno type="category">C</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="code-format">
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>P.SAM.0700</idno><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <idno type="category">P</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="code-category">
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
          <idno type="category">D</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="code-manuscript">
        <title type="formal">
          <idno>COLL.Samp.ms-Now-NL-12</idno>
          <idno type="category">SYSCOLL</idno>
          <idno type="type">edition</idno>
        </title>
      </egXML>
      <p>A file named by a code, whatever the category's, is named by its own: <code>C.MAI-0813.xml</code>. A file
        named otherwise isn't checked; one named by another code is reported. Only a whole file can show it:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="code-matches-file-name">
        <!-- file name: C.SAM-0601.xml -->
        <TEI>
          <teiHeader>
            <fileDesc>
              <titleStmt>
                <title type="formal">
                  <idno>C.SAM-0600</idno>
                  <idno type="category">C</idno>
                  <idno type="type">edition</idno>
                </title>
              </titleStmt>
              <publicationStmt>
                <p>An example of the guide.</p>
              </publicationStmt>
              <sourceDesc>
                <p>An excerpt.</p>
              </sourceDesc>
            </fileDesc>
          </teiHeader>
          <text>
            <body/>
          </text>
        </TEI>
      </egXML>
      <p>The import gives the document the code as its id, read with its kind (<ptr target="#codes-and-ids"/>).</p>
      <specGrp xml:id="header-title-specs">
        <!-- The title statement: the formal title first, then aliases and other titles. -->
        <elementSpec ident="titleStmt" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-required">
          <constraintSpec ident="title-first-formal" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The title statement opens with the formal title.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt">
                <sch:assert id="title-first-formal" test="tei:title[1][@type = 'formal']">The title statement opens
                  with a title typed formal.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="title-one-formal" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The title statement has one formal title.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt">
                <sch:assert id="title-one-formal" test="count(tei:title[@type = 'formal']) = 1">The title statement
                  has one formal title.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="title-type" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The title statement's titles are typed formal, alias or other.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title">
                <sch:assert id="title-type" test="@type = ('formal', 'alias', 'other')">A title in the title statement
                  is typed formal, alias or other.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="title-alias-words" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-common">
            <desc>An alias isn't empty.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title">
                <sch:assert id="title-alias-words" test="not(@type = 'alias') or normalize-space(.) != ''">An alias
                  isn't empty.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="title-other-source" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>A title typed other names its source.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title">
                <sch:assert id="title-other-source" role="warning" test="not(@type = 'other') or @source">A title
                  typed other names its source in @source.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <!-- The formal title's identifiers: the document's code, its category and its type; an export's levels. -->
        <elementSpec ident="idno" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-required">
          <constraintSpec ident="formal-title-idnos" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The formal title holds the document's code (an identifier without a type), its category and its
              type, once each; an export adds the permalinks of the levels the file belongs to.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']">
                <sch:assert id="formal-title-idnos" test="count(tei:idno[not(@type)]) = 1 and count(tei:idno[@type =                   'category']) = 1 and count(tei:idno[@type = 'type']) = 1 and (every $level in ('work', 'version',                   'witness', 'transcription') satisfies count(tei:idno[@type = $level]) le 1)">The formal title holds
                  the document's code (an identifier without a type), its category and its type, once each, and at
                  most one permalink for each level the file belongs to.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="idno-in-formal-title" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>An identifier in the title statement stands in the formal title.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title/tei:idno">
                <sch:assert id="idno-in-formal-title" test="parent::tei:title[@type = 'formal']">An identifier in the
                  title statement stands in the formal title.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="idno-type" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>An identifier in the title statement is the code, the category, the type, or a level's
              permalink.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title/tei:idno">
                <sch:assert id="idno-type" test="not(@type) or @type = ('category', 'type', 'work', 'version',                   'witness', 'transcription')">An identifier in the formal title is the code (without a type), the
                  category, the type, or the permalink of a level (work, version, witness, transcription).</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="code-format" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The document's code is a prefix of capitals, a dot, the title part, its subparts joined by hyphens,
              and any source part after a dot, beginning with a letter: a date is joined by a hyphen.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']/tei:idno[not(@type)]">
                <sch:assert id="code-format" test="matches(normalize-space(.),                   '^[A-Z]+\.[A-Za-z0-9]+(-[A-Za-z0-9]+)*(\.[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*(-[A-Za-z0-9]+)*)*$')">The document's
                  code (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>) begins with its category's prefix and a dot, holds
                  only letters, digits, dots and hyphens, and joins its title's parts, the date among them, by
                  hyphens; a part after another dot is a source part, beginning with a letter.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="code-category" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The document's code begins with its category's prefix: COLL for both kinds of collection, the
              category itself for the others; UNK's any.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']/tei:idno[not(@type)]">
                <sch:let name="category" value="normalize-space(../tei:idno[@type = 'category'][1])"/>
                <sch:let name="prefix" value="substring-before(normalize-space(.), '.')"/>
                <sch:assert id="code-category" test="$category = ('', 'UNK') or $prefix = (if                   ($category = ('SYSCOLL', 'CHRCOLL')) then 'COLL' else $category)">The document's code
                  (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>) begins with its category's prefix (<sch:value-of select="if ($category = ('SYSCOLL', 'CHRCOLL')) then 'COLL' else $category"/>, for the category
                  <sch:value-of select="$category"/>).</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="code-manuscript" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>A manuscript's code goes with the manuscript's description.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']/tei:idno[not(@type)]">
                <sch:assert id="code-manuscript" test="not(contains(normalize-space(.), '.ms-')) or                   ancestor::tei:fileDesc/tei:sourceDesc//tei:msDesc">A manuscript's code (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>) goes with the manuscript's description (msDesc) in the
                  sourceDesc.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="code-matches-file-name" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>A file named by a code, any category's, is named by its document's code.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']/tei:idno[not(@type)]">
                <sch:assert id="code-matches-file-name" role="warning" test="not(matches(base-uri(.),                   '/[A-Z]+\.[^/]*\.xml$')) or ends-with(base-uri(.), concat('/',                   encode-for-uri(normalize-space(.)), '.xml'))">A file named by a code is named by its document's code
                  (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>).</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="category-value" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The document's category is one of the categories.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']/tei:idno[@type = 'category']">
                <sch:assert id="category-value" test="normalize-space(.) = ('SYSCOLL', 'CHRCOLL', 'C', 'D', 'P', 'H',                   'EX', 'EP', 'LR', 'CPT', 'CHA', 'UNK')">The document's category (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>) is one of SYSCOLL, CHRCOLL, C, D, P, H, EX, EP, LR, CPT, CHA,
                  UNK.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="document-type-value" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
            <desc>The document's type is one of the types.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']/tei:idno[@type = 'type']">
                <sch:assert id="document-type-value" test="normalize-space(.) = ('edition', 'manuscript',                   'reconstruction', 'hypothesis', 'placeholder')">The document's type (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>) is one of edition, manuscript, reconstruction, hypothesis,
                  placeholder.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="type-manuscript" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>A document typed manuscript has the manuscript's description.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']/tei:idno[@type = 'type']">
                <sch:assert id="type-manuscript" test="not(normalize-space(.) = 'manuscript') or                   ancestor::tei:fileDesc/tei:sourceDesc//tei:msDesc">A document typed manuscript has the manuscript's
                  description (msDesc) in its sourceDesc.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-required">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="idno-type-unknown" corresp="#tier-required">
                <valItem ident="category">
                  <desc>in the formal title: the document's category</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="type">
                  <desc>in the formal title: the document's type</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="work">
                  <desc>in an export's formal title: the permalink of the work the file belongs to</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="version">
                  <desc>in an export's formal title: the permalink of the version</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="witness">
                  <desc>in an export's formal title: the permalink of the witness</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="transcription">
                  <desc>in an export's formal title: the permalink of the transcription</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="wikidata">
                  <desc>Wikidata's item (Q…)</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="viaf">
                  <desc>the Virtual International Authority File</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="gnd">
                  <desc>the Gemeinsame Normdatei</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="geonames">
                  <desc>GeoNames</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="tgn">
                  <desc>the Getty Thesaurus of Geographic Names</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="pleiades">
                  <desc>Pleiades, for ancient places</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="mirabile">
                  <desc>Mirabile</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="clavis">
                  <desc>the Clavis canonum</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="biblissima">
                  <desc>Biblissima</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="schoenberg">
                  <desc>the Schoenberg Database of Manuscripts</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="hmml">
                  <desc>the Hill Museum &amp; Manuscript Library</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="bodleian">
                  <desc>the Bodleian Libraries' catalogues</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="manusOnline">
                  <desc>Manus Online</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="ieArk">
                  <desc>the Bodleian's ARK for an intellectual entity</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="crArk">
                  <desc>the Bodleian's ARK for a catalogue record</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="SCN">
                  <desc>the Bodleian's Summary Catalogue number</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="shelfmark">
                  <desc>a manuscript's shelfmark</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="ISBN">
                  <desc>a publication's ISBN</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="url">
                  <desc>an address on the web</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="biblatex">
                  <desc>a publication's biblatex key</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
      </specGrp>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="header-responsibility">
    <head>Responsibility and funding</head>
    <p>After its titles, the title statement may name who did what for the document: its editors, the scholars whose
      study it rests on, the cataloguer of its manuscript, its encoders. Each is a <gi>respStmt</gi> with what they
      did, <gi>resp</gi>, dated by <att>when</att> where known, and who, a <gi>persName</gi> or an <gi>orgName</gi>
      pointing to its record in <att>ref</att> (<ptr target="#marked-names"/>). A <gi>funder</gi> names who paid
      for the work. Both are <hi>full</hi>. The Council of Carthage of 397 names its editor and its funder:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.CAR-0397">
      <respStmt>
        <resp when="1974">Editor</resp>
        <persName>Charles Munier</persName>
      </respStmt>
      <funder>
        <orgName>
          <ref target="https://erc.europa.eu/homepage">European Research Council</ref>
        </orgName>
      </funder>
    </egXML>
    <p>The header points to records as the text does, by <att>ref</att>, in the same forms: a title to its work, an
      author or an editor to a person. <att>key</att> and <att>corresp</att> keep TEI's other uses; a record's
      pointer in them is reported. <gi>provenance</gi>, <gi>acquisition</gi> and <gi>origin</gi>, in a manuscript's
      description, have no <att>ref</att> in TEI: the record they point to is named inside them, by a name with
      <att>ref</att>, and the import makes the relation from that name.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="record-pointer-in-ref">
      <sourceDesc>
        <bibl>
          <author key="#new_person_Exemplus">Exemplus</author>
          <title>Epistolae</title>
        </bibl>
      </sourceDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="record-pointer-form">
      <sourceDesc>
        <bibl>
          <author ref="new_person_Exemplus">Exemplus</author><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <title>Epistolae</title>
        </bibl>
      </sourceDesc>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="header-pointers-specs">
      <!-- The header points to records the same way: titles to works, authors and editors to persons, places
           and institutions to theirs. provenance, acquisition and origin have no @ref in TEI: the record they
           point to is named inside them, by a name with @ref. -->
      <elementSpec ident="title" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-required">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="author" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="editor" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="teiHeader" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-required">
        <constraintSpec ident="record-pointer-in-ref" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A record is pointed to by @ref, not by @key or @corresp.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@key or @corresp]">
              <sch:report id="record-pointer-in-ref" role="warning" test="some $p in                 tokenize(normalize-space(string-join((@key, @corresp), ' ')), ' ') satisfies matches($p,                 '^#?[Nn]ew_[A-Za-z]+_|^#?(PERS\.\d+|solemne_person_\d+)$|^https?://\S+/id/(person|place|institution|event|manuscript|publication)/')">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt;
                points to a record in @key or @corresp (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(string-join((@key,                 @corresp), ' '))"/>): a record is pointed to by @ref, on a name inside provenance, acquisition and
                origin.</sch:report>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="header-publication">
    <head>Publication and source</head>
    <p>The publication statement (<gi>publicationStmt</gi>) says who publishes the file, not its source: for the
      project's files, the project, with its address. It's <hi>required</hi>, and may be a paragraph:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <publicationStmt>
        <publisher>SOLEMNE: 'The Social Life of Early Medieval Normative Texts'</publisher>
        <address>
          <orgName type="institution">Radboud University</orgName>
          <street>Houtlaan 4</street>
          <settlement>Nijmegen</settlement>
          <postCode>6525 XZ</postCode>
          <country>Netherlands</country>
        </address>
      </publicationStmt>
    </egXML>
    <p>The source description (<gi>sourceDesc</gi>), also <hi>required</hi>, describes what the text was transcribed
      from: an edition, as a <gi>biblStruct</gi> (<ptr target="#editions-and-literature"/>); a manuscript, as a
      manuscript description (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>); for a reconstruction, the editor's publication, with
      the witnesses it rests on in a <gi>listWit</gi>. The Council of Mainz, 813, from Werminghoff's edition:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <sourceDesc>
        <biblStruct>
          <analytic>
            <title level="a">Concilium Moguntinense. 813. (mense Maio) - Iun. 9.</title>
          </analytic>
          <monogr>
            <editor>
              <persName>
                <forename>Albert</forename>
                <surname>Werminghoff</surname>
              </persName>
            </editor>
            <title level="m">Concilia aevi Karolini</title>
            <imprint>
              <pubPlace>Hannover</pubPlace>
              <date>1906</date>
            </imprint>
            <biblScope unit="page">258-273</biblScope>
          </monogr>
          <series>
            <title level="s">Monumenta Germaniae Historica</title>
            <biblScope unit="volume">2.1</biblScope>
          </series>
        </biblStruct>
      </sourceDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="idno-type-unknown">
      <sourceDesc>
        <bibl>
          <title>Concilia exempli gratia</title>
          <idno type="isbn">0-000-00000-0</idno><!-- breaks the rule -->
        </bibl>
      </sourceDesc>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="header-encoding">
    <head>How the text was encoded</head>
    <p>The encoding description (<gi>encodingDesc</gi>) says, once for the file, what the text's encoding needs
      explained. All of it is <hi>full</hi>. In its editorial declaration (<gi>editorialDecl</gi>), a
      <gi>normalization</gi> says what the transcription normalised (<ptr target="#transcription-spelling"/>); the
      sections below give the rest.</p>

    <div xml:id="header-inferred-units">
      <head>Inferred units</head>
      <p>Where the encoder didn't mark the units of reception (<ptr target="#text-units"/>), so that a division's
        paragraphs may be several units, a <gi>segmentation</gi> typed <val>inferred-units</val> says so, with a
        paragraph, which TEI requires. For the whole file, it is the default (<att>default</att> true) or stands
        alone. For a few divisions among many marked units, it has <att>default</att> false and an id, and those
        divisions point to it in <att>decls</att>; one that none points to is reported.</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <editorialDecl>
          <segmentation xml:id="units-inferred-here" type="inferred-units" default="false">
            <p>The units of reception aren't marked in the chapters that point here: each paragraph may be one.</p>
          </segmentation>
        </editorialDecl>
        <div type="chapter" n="7" decls="#units-inferred-here">
          <p>…</p>
          <p>…</p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="inferred-units-pointed-to">
        <editorialDecl>
          <segmentation xml:id="inferred-in-some-chapters" type="inferred-units" default="false">
            <p>The units of reception aren't marked in some chapters: each is one.</p>
          </segmentation>
        </editorialDecl>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="segmentation-type-unknown">
        <editorialDecl>
          <segmentation type="units-inferred"><!-- breaks the rule -->
            <p>Units inferred.</p>
          </segmentation>
        </editorialDecl>
      </egXML>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="header-curation">
      <head>Curation</head>
      <p>How far a text has been checked is its curation: <gi>correction</gi>, in the editorial declaration, with its
        <att>status</att>, <val>high</val>, <val>medium</val>, <val>low</val> or <val>unknown</val>, and a paragraph
        saying how, which TEI requires. The whole text's is the default; a division or a paragraph whose text was
        checked otherwise points to another <gi>correction</gi> in <att>decls</att>. Of several, one is the default.
        A text's completeness is never stated: the application counts it from the gaps.</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <editorialDecl>
          <correction xml:id="checked-text" status="high" default="true">
            <p>Checked against the edition.</p>
          </correction>
          <correction xml:id="unchecked-chapter" status="low">
            <p>Not checked yet.</p>
          </correction>
        </editorialDecl>
        <div type="chapter" n="12" decls="#unchecked-chapter">
          <p>…</p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <p>A file that states no curation is imported as <val>medium</val>, checked at least once, when it is one of
        the project's coded files, a file of the Patrologia Latina or SCTA's Gratian, and as <val>low</val> when it
        comes from handwritten text recognition. <val>high</val> is set in the application, node by node. What was
        left out by choice is said once, in <gi>samplingDecl</gi>.</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="correction-default">
        <editorialDecl>
          <correction xml:id="checked-against-the-edition" status="high">
            <p>Checked against the edition.</p>
          </correction>
          <correction xml:id="not-checked-yet" status="low">
            <p>Not checked yet.</p>
          </correction>
        </editorialDecl>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="decls-target">
        <editorialDecl>
          <correction xml:id="checked" status="high">
            <p>Checked against the edition.</p>
          </correction>
        </editorialDecl>
        <div type="chapter" n="1" decls="#unchecked">
          <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <specGrp xml:id="header-encoding-specs">
        <!-- The declarations a text and its parts point to: curation and inferred units. -->
        <elementSpec ident="editorialDecl" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
          <constraintSpec ident="decls-target" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>@decls points to a correction or a segmentation in the header.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:*[@decls]">
                <sch:assert id="decls-target" test="every $t in tokenize(normalize-space(@decls), ' ') satisfies                   (starts-with($t, '#') and exists(/tei:TEI/tei:teiHeader//(tei:correction |                   tei:segmentation)[@xml:id = substring($t, 2)]))">@decls (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(@decls)"/>) points to a correction or a segmentation in the
                  header.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="correction-default" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>Of several corrections, one is the default.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:editorialDecl[count(tei:correction) gt 1]">
                <sch:assert id="correction-default" test="count(tei:correction[@default = 'true']) = 1">Of several
                  corrections, one is the default (@default="true"): the whole text's.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="inferred-units-pointed-to" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>A statement of inferred units for some divisions is pointed to by them.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:segmentation[@type = 'inferred-units'][@default = 'false']">
                <sch:let name="id" value="concat('#', @xml:id)"/>
                <sch:assert id="inferred-units-pointed-to" role="warning" test="@xml:id and                   //@decls[tokenize(normalize-space(.), ' ') = $id]">A statement of inferred units for some divisions
                  (@default="false") is pointed to by their @decls.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <!-- The units of reception, where the encoders didn't mark them. -->
        <elementSpec ident="segmentation" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
          <classes mode="change">
            <memberOf key="att.typed"/>
          </classes>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="segmentation-type-unknown" corresp="#tier-full">
                <valItem ident="inferred-units">
                  <desc>the units of reception aren't marked: each division that holds paragraphs is one</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
      </specGrp>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="header-external-ids">
      <head>Other projects' ids</head>
      <p>A node's ids in other projects, SCTA's resources, the Clavis canonum's keys, Corpus Corporum's sections, go
        in <att>sameAs</att>, on the division or the paragraph, each with a prefix declared in the header by a
        <gi>prefixDef</gi> in <gi>listPrefixDef</gi>, which turns it into an address. The application knows
        four:</p>
      <list>
        <item><code>scta</code>, SCTA: <code>http://scta.info/resource/$1</code>;</item>
        <item><code>clavis</code>, the Clavis canonum:
          <code>https://data.mgh.de/databases/clavis/db/legacysearch?key=$1</code>;</item>
        <item><code>corpuscorporum</code>, Corpus Corporum's Patrologia Latina: <code>https://mlat.uzh.ch/browser/$1</code>;</item>
        <item><code>pseudoisidor</code>, the Pseudoisidor project, whose address the guide gives when a file first
          carries its ids.</item>
      </list>
      <p>Any other prefix may be declared, and is reported; a prefix used without its declaration is an error. A
        Clavis key on a chapter:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <listPrefixDef>
          <prefixDef ident="clavis" matchPattern="(.+)" replacementPattern="https://data.mgh.de/databases/clavis/db/legacysearch?key=$1"/>
        </listPrefixDef>
        <div type="chapter" n="27" sameAs="clavis:IS35.27">
          <p>…</p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <p>A text of the Patrologia Latina has Corpus Corporum's id, <code>cps_2.</code> (its corpus 2, the Patrologia
        Latina) and the text's id there; a paragraph adds <code>:</code>, its division's position, <code>;</code>
        and its position among the division's children, counted from 1 with the heading. For Absalon of Lund's
        Testamentum, PL 209, the text is <code>corpuscorporum:cps_2.AbsLun.Testam17</code> and a paragraph
        <code>corpuscorporum:cps_2.AbsLun.Testam17:3;16</code>. The converter from PL's files writes them, on the text
        and on each paragraph that is one of the application's nodes; an encoder doesn't.</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <listPrefixDef>
          <prefixDef ident="corpuscorporum" matchPattern="(.+)" replacementPattern="https://mlat.uzh.ch/browser/$1"/>
        </listPrefixDef>
        <p sameAs="corpuscorporum:cps_2.AbsLun.Testam17:3;16">…</p>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="same-as-prefix-declared">
        <div type="chapter" n="1" sameAs="clavis:IS01.01">
          <head type="number">I.</head>
          <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
          <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="prefix-known">
        <listPrefixDef>
          <prefixDef ident="unknown" matchPattern="(.+)" replacementPattern="https://example.org/$1">
            <p>An identifier of a project the application doesn't know.</p>
          </prefixDef>
        </listPrefixDef>
      </egXML>
      <p>A record's or a manuscript's ids in other catalogues aren't pointers but identifiers: <gi>idno</gi>s typed
        by their authority (<ptr target="#records"/>).</p>
      <specGrp xml:id="header-external-ids-specs">
        <!-- External ids: a node's in @sameAs, its prefix declared in the header; a record's or a manuscript's in an
             idno typed by its authority. -->
        <elementSpec ident="prefixDef" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
          <constraintSpec ident="same-as-prefix-declared" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>A prefix used in @sameAs is declared in the header.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:*[@sameAs]">
                <sch:let name="declared" value="/tei:TEI/tei:teiHeader//tei:prefixDef/@ident"/>
                <sch:assert id="same-as-prefix-declared" test="every $id in tokenize(normalize-space(@sameAs), ' ')                   satisfies (not(matches($id, '^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.\-]*:')) or matches($id, '^https?:') or                   substring-before($id, ':') = $declared)">The prefixes in @sameAs (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(@sameAs)"/>) are declared in the header, by prefixDef in
                  listPrefixDef.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="prefix-known" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>The prefixes the application knows are scta, clavis, pseudoisidor and corpuscorporum.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:prefixDef">
                <sch:assert id="prefix-known" role="warning" test="@ident = ('scta', 'clavis', 'pseudoisidor',                   'corpuscorporum')">The prefix <sch:value-of select="@ident"/> isn't one the application knows (scta,
                  clavis, pseudoisidor, corpuscorporum).</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>
      </specGrp>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="header-calendars-and-vocabularies">
      <head>Calendars and vocabularies</head>
      <p>The calendars and eras a file's dates use are declared once, and each date points to its own (<ptr target="#dates"/>). An export declares the terms of the application's vocabularies it uses, in
        <gi>classDecl</gi>, each with its permalink (<ptr target="#exports"/>); a file may declare terms too, such as
        a segment's (<ptr target="#marked-other"/>).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="header-abstract">
    <head>The abstract</head>
    <p>The profile description (<gi>profileDesc</gi>) may hold an <gi>abstract</gi>: what the document is, its
      context, and what the file leaves out of it. It's <hi>full</hi>, and the import keeps it as the document's
      description. The Council of Aachen of 836:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.AAC-0836">
      <abstract>
        <p>Note that edition also contains a preface to this council, which has been omitted here.</p>
      </abstract>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="header-revisions">
    <head>The history of the file</head>
    <p>The revision description (<gi>revisionDesc</gi>) lists the file's changes, the latest first, each a
      <gi>change</gi> with its date in <att>when</att> and who made it in <att>who</att>. The import keeps it as the
      file gives it, read-only, as the history before the import. An export writes the application's releases and a
      summary of the changes since the last, and, on request, each change set (<ptr target="#exports"/>). It's
      <hi>full</hi>. The Council of Carthage of 397:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.CAR-0397">
      <revisionDesc>
        <change when="2025-03-21">Initial XML-TEI encoding.</change>
      </revisionDesc>
    </egXML>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="text" n="III">
  <head>The text</head>
  <p>The text of a file is its <gi>body</gi>: the document as the source gives it, its divisions, their headings and
    their paragraphs. The import makes each of them a node of the document's tree (<ptr target="#documents-and-nodes"/>)
    and keeps the order the file gives; an export writes them back in the same form, each with the id the node got at
    import. This part says how the text is divided and how it is transcribed; <ptr target="#marked-in-the-text"/> says
    what may be marked inside it.</p>
  <p>A file needs no division at all: a body holding paragraphs is a document of one node's text, and an empty body
    passes the checks. Most files divide their text, and divisions, their headings and page breaks are
    <hi>common</hi>; units, registers and the transcription's finer marks are <hi>full</hi>.</p>
  <p>Inside the body stand only the elements of the text's structure and of the annotation layers
    (<ptr target="#marked-in-the-text"/>). An element the schema doesn't take from TEI at all fails the RelaxNG; one
    that the schema takes for the header or the records, such as <gi>orgName</gi>, fails the Schematron when it stands
    in the text.</p>
  <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="element-outside-the-schema">
    <p>Placuit ut <emph>episcopi</emph> maneant.</p><!-- breaks the rule -->
  </egXML>
  <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="text-elements">
    <p>Placuit ut episcopi <orgName>ecclesiae</orgName> maneant.</p>
  </egXML>

  <div xml:id="text-divisions">
    <head>Divisions</head>
    <p>A division (<gi>div</gi>) holds headings, then either paragraphs or divisions, never both. Its headings stand
      where the source puts them, usually first. Page breaks, notes and gaps may stand between headings, paragraphs
      and divisions (<ptr target="#text-between"/>). The body follows the same rule: it holds headings with either
      paragraphs or divisions.</p>
    <p>Every division has a type, from a closed list, so that a misspelled type fails the RelaxNG:</p>
    <list>
      <item><val>section</val>: a book, a part, or one text inside a document, such as a council in a collection;</item>
      <item><val>chapter</val>: a numbered or titled unit, as the source divides it; chapters may hold chapters;</item>
      <item><val>unit</val>: a subdivision without a heading of its own, where several stand side by side
        (<ptr target="#text-units"/>);</item>
      <item><val>preface</val>, <val>closer</val> and <val>subscriptions</val>: what opens a text, what closes it
        (a closing rubric, a letter's farewell and date), and its signatories
        (<ptr target="#text-prefaces-and-closers"/>);</item>
      <item><val>register</val> and <val>registerItem</val>: a list of chapter titles and its entries
        (<ptr target="#text-registers"/>);</item>
      <item><val>part</val>, <val>distinctio</val>, <val>causa</val>, <val>quaestio</val>, <val>capitulum</val> and
        <val>dictum</val>: Gratian's levels (<ptr target="#text-gratian"/>).</item>
    </list>
    <p>Divisions follow the source: an edition's chapters as its editor numbers them, a manuscript's as its rubrics
      and initials mark them. Where the source marks no division, the text stays in paragraphs; the guide doesn't
      ask for divisions the source doesn't have. The Council of Mainz of 813, in Werminghoff's edition, is a series
      of chapters, each with a rubric and a number:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <div type="chapter" n="1">
        <head type="rubric"><pb n="261"/>De fide catholica firmiter retinenda.</head>
        <head type="number">Cap.I</head>
        <p>Initium enim actionis nostrae de fide esse decrevimus, quae bonorum est omnium fundamentum, …</p>
      </div>
      <div type="chapter" n="2">
        <head type="rubric">De spe habenda in Deum.</head>
        <head type="number">Cap.II</head>
        <p>Spes vero in Deum firmiter a cunctis Christianis tenenda est. …</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>The names and quotations that the file marks in these chapters are left out here; <ptr target="#marked-in-the-text"/> shows them. A division without its type, or with a type not in the list, fails
      the RelaxNG, and so does a division that holds paragraphs and divisions side by side:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="division-type-missing">
      <div n="1"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="division-type-misspelled">
      <div type="chpater" n="1"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="division-holds-paragraphs-and-divisions">
      <div type="chapter" n="1"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
        <div type="unit">
          <p>Et clerici.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>The import makes each division a node of the kind <term>division</term>, with its type; a division's
      paragraphs, where it holds no divisions, are one unit of reception (<ptr target="#text-units"/>).</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="text-divisions-specs">
      <!-- The structure of the text. -->
      <macroSpec ident="solemne.structure" mode="add" xml:id="division-holds-paragraphs-and-divisions" corresp="#tier-required">
        <desc>What a division or the body holds: headings with either paragraphs or divisions, never both, and page
          breaks, notes and gaps between them.</desc>
        <content>
          <alternate>
            <alternate minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <elementRef key="head"/>
              <macroRef key="solemne.between"/>
              <elementRef key="p"/>
            </alternate>
            <alternate minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <elementRef key="head"/>
              <macroRef key="solemne.between"/>
              <elementRef key="div"/>
            </alternate>
          </alternate>
        </content>
      </macroSpec>

      <macroSpec ident="solemne.between" mode="add">
        <desc>What may stand between headings, paragraphs and divisions.</desc>
        <content>
          <alternate>
            <elementRef key="pb"/>
            <elementRef key="note"/>
            <elementRef key="gap"/>
          </alternate>
        </content>
      </macroSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="body" module="textstructure" mode="change" corresp="#tier-required">
        <content>
          <macroRef key="solemne.structure"/>
        </content>
        <constraintSpec ident="text-elements" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-required">
          <desc>Inside the text, only the annotation layers' elements.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:body//tei:*">
              <sch:assert id="text-elements" test="local-name() = ('div', 'head', 'p', 'note', 'pb', 'lb', 'gap',                 'persName', 'placeName', 'eventName', 'bibl', 'cit', 'quote', 'ref', 'seg', 'date', 'subst', 'add',                 'del', 'unclear', 'foreign', 'choice', 'corr', 'sic', 'abbr', 'expan', 'supplied', 'rs',                 'anchor')">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt; isn't one of the text's elements.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="div" module="textstructure" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <content>
          <macroRef key="solemne.structure"/>
        </content>
        <constraintSpec ident="unit-alone" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>Units are written only where they stand side by side.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:div[@type = 'unit']">
              <sch:assert id="unit-alone" test="count(../tei:div) gt 1">A unit stands alone in its division: its
                paragraphs belong to the division itself.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="unit-holds-text" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A unit holds paragraphs only.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:div[@type = 'unit']">
              <sch:assert id="unit-holds-text" test="not(tei:div) and not(tei:head)">A unit holds paragraphs only,
                without headings or divisions.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="register-item-in-register" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A register's items stand in a register.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:div[@type = 'registerItem']">
              <sch:assert id="register-item-in-register" test="parent::tei:div[@type = 'register']">A register item
                stands in a register.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="number-heading-n" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-common">
          <desc>A division with a number heading carries the number as a value.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:div[tei:head[@type = 'number']]">
              <sch:assert id="number-heading-n" test="@n">A division with a number heading
                (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(tei:head[@type = 'number'][1])"/>) has @n.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req" xml:id="division-type-missing" corresp="#tier-common">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="division-type-misspelled" corresp="#tier-common">
              <valItem ident="section">
                <desc>a book, a part, or one text inside a document (a council in a collection)</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="chapter">
                <desc>a numbered or titled unit; chapters may hold chapters</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="unit">
                <desc>a subdivision without a heading of its own, where several stand side by side: a norm and its
                  authority statements, one signatory among the subscriptions</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="preface"/>
              <valItem ident="closer">
                <desc>a closing rubric, or a letter's farewell and date</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="subscriptions">
                <desc>the signatories</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="register">
                <desc>the list of chapter titles</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="registerItem">
                <desc>one entry of a register</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="part">
                <desc>a part of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="distinctio">
                <desc>a distinction of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="causa">
                <desc>a causa of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="quaestio">
                <desc>a question of a causa</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="capitulum">
                <desc>a canon of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="dictum">
                <desc>Gratian's own words between the canons</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="n" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
            <desc>the division's number, as a value: 49 for a heading XLVIIII; a letter after it for a chapter after a
              numbered one, or for a number repeated</desc>
            <datatype xml:id="division-number-not-a-value" corresp="#tier-common">
              <dataRef name="token" restriction="[0-9]+[a-z]?"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-headings">
    <head>Headings</head>
    <p>A heading (<gi>head</gi>) is a node of its own, with its own text, and has a type:</p>
    <list>
      <item><val>number</val>: the division's number as the source writes it, with the word that goes with it
        ("Cap. II.", "CAPUT V.", "XLVIIII.");</item>
      <item><val>rubric</val>: what the text is about ("De spe habenda in Deum.");</item>
      <item><val>inscription</val>: where the text comes from ("Ex eadem Epistola."). A heading that begins with its
        source is an inscription, even when it goes on with its subject.</item>
    </list>
    <p>A division may have several headings, of one type or of several, in the order the source gives them. The
      Collectio Dacheriana, in d'Achery's edition, gives a chapter's number, its rubric and its inscription:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.Dach">
      <div type="chapter" n="5">
        <pb n="519"/>
        <head type="number">CAPUT V.</head>
        <head type="rubric">Quod ad militiam saecularem post poenitentiam redire non liceat.</head>
        <head type="inscription">Ex eadem Epistola.</head>
        <p>Contrarium est omnino ecclesiasticis regulis post poenitentiae actionem redire ad militiam saecularem, cum
          Apostolus dicat: Nemo militans Deo implicat se negotiis saecularibus. …</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>The Council of Aachen of 816 heads a chapter with its source, then its subject: one inscription.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.AAC-0816">
      <div type="chapter" n="49">
        <head type="number">XLVIIII.</head>
        <head type="inscription">In Antioceno concilio de peregrinorum susceptione, t. VII.</head>
        <p>Nullus peregrinorum sine pacificis, id est commendaticiis, suscipiatur epistolis.</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>A heading without a type, or with one not in the list, fails the RelaxNG:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="heading-type-missing">
      <div type="chapter" n="1">
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head>De episcopis.</head><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="heading-type-misspelled">
      <div type="chapter" n="1">
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="inscripto">De episcopis.</head><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>An inscription is also a claim about the text's source: the application reads it as an attribution, the source
      the text claims (<ptr target="#marked-statements"/>).</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="text-headings-specs">
      <elementSpec ident="head" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req" xml:id="heading-type-missing" corresp="#tier-common">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="heading-type-misspelled" corresp="#tier-common">
              <valItem ident="number">
                <desc>the division's number as written ("Cap. II.")</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="rubric">
                <desc>what the text is about ("De subintroductis mulieribus")</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="inscription">
                <desc>where the text comes from ("Ex concilio Nicaeno"), even when it goes on with its subject</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-numbers">
    <head>Numbers</head>
    <p>A numbered division carries its number as a value in <att>n</att>: digits, and a letter after them where
      needed. The number heading keeps the number as the source writes it; <att>n</att> gives what it counts, so that
      citations can use it: <val>49</val> for "XLVIIII.", <val>5</val> for "CAPUT V.". A chapter without a number
      after a numbered one takes that number and a letter (<val>11a</val> after 11); a number the source repeats
      takes the next letter. A division with a number heading has <att>n</att> (a Schematron error otherwise), and
      <att>n</att> holds nothing but the value (a RelaxNG error):</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="number-heading-n">
      <div type="chapter">
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="division-number-not-a-value">
      <div type="chapter" n="I"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>The import keeps the value as the node's number, and the citation of a chapter or a register item is made from
      it (<ptr target="#citations-and-permalinks"/>). A division without a number heading may still have
      <att>n</att>, where the numbering of the file gives one.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-units">
    <head>Units</head>
    <p>A unit (<val>unit</val>) is a subdivision without a heading of its own, written only where several stand side
      by side: a norm and the authority statements that follow it, each signatory among the subscriptions, the
      paragraphs of a preface that are read one by one. A division whose text is one unit holds its paragraphs
      itself: a unit that stands alone is an error, and so is a unit that holds headings or divisions.</p>
    <p>The preface of the Council of Mainz of 813 is three units, the greeting, the account of the council, and its
      decision:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <div type="preface">
        <div type="unit">
          <p><pb n="259"/>In nomine patris et filii et Spiritus sancti. Gloriosissimo et christianissimo imperatori
            Karolo Augusto, verae religionis rectori ac defensori sanctae Dei eclesiae, una cum prole sua eiusque
            fidelibus vita et salus, honor et benedictio cum victoria sine fine mansura.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="unit">
          <p>Almifice reverentiae vestrae patefacimus nos humillimi famuli ac missi vestri, … modis quibus poterant
            iusticias terminantes.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="unit">
          <p>In his itaque omnibus primo decrevimus unicuique personae vel sexui congruum honorem impendere … in
            omnes aeternitates saeculi, amen.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="unit-alone">
      <div type="chapter" n="1">
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
        <div type="unit">
          <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="unit-holds-text">
      <div type="chapter" n="1">
        <head type="number">I.</head>
        <head type="rubric">De episcopis.</head>
        <div type="unit">
          <head type="rubric">De eodem.</head>
          <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="unit">
          <p>Et clerici.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>The text of a division that holds no divisions, a unit or a chapter of paragraphs, is one unit of reception:
      what a reader, a search or the detection of text reuse takes as one piece. Where the encoder didn't mark the
      units, so that a chapter's paragraphs may be several units, the header says so, for the whole file or for some
      divisions (<ptr target="#header-inferred-units"/>).</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-registers">
    <head>Registers</head>
    <p>A register (<val>register</val>) is a list of a text's chapter titles; each entry is a
      <val>registerItem</val>, with the number and the title the register gives, and stands in a register (a
      Schematron error otherwise). A file that is only a register holds one register division in its body. The
      register of the Collectio 400 capitulorum in a Munich manuscript begins:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <div type="register">
        <div type="registerItem" n="1">
          <head type="number"><pb n="158ra"/>I</head>
          <head type="rubric">De confirmatione caritatis dei</head>
        </div>
        <div type="registerItem" n="2">
          <head type="number">II</head>
          <head type="rubric">De amore confirmata proximi per deum</head>
        </div>
        <div type="registerItem" n="3">
          <head type="number">III</head>
          <head type="rubric">De deo quod non est personarum acceptor</head>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="register-item-in-register">
      <div type="registerItem" n="2">
        <head type="number">II.</head>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>Which chapter a register item lists is a relation (<ptr target="#relations"/>), not a pointer in the
      text.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-prefaces-and-closers">
    <head>Prefaces, closers and subscriptions</head>
    <p>What opens a text before its chapters is a <val>preface</val> (above, <ptr target="#text-units"/>); what
      closes it, a closing rubric or a letter's farewell and date, a <val>closer</val>; its signatories,
      <val>subscriptions</val>, each signatory a unit. The Council of Aschheim, 756, ends with a closing rubric, and
      the Council of Soissons, 744, with its signatories:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.ASC-0756">
      <div type="closer">
        <head type="rubric">FINIT.</head>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.SOI-0744">
      <div type="subscriptions">
        <div type="unit">
          <p>Signum inluster vir Pippino, maiorum domius.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="unit">
          <p>Signum Radobodo.</p>
        </div>
        <div type="unit">
          <p>Signum Ariberto.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-gratian">
    <head>Gratian's levels</head>
    <p>Gratian's Decretum has levels of its own, which the citations of its readers use ("C. 16 q. 6 c. 2"): a
      <val>part</val>, a <val>distinctio</val>, a <val>causa</val> and a <val>quaestio</val> are kinds of section; a
      <val>capitulum</val> (a canon) and a <val>dictum</val> (Gratian's own words between the canons) are kinds of
      chapter. They're types like the others, each with its number in <att>n</att>. The skeleton of a question of a
      causa, its text left out:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <div type="part" n="2">
        <div type="causa" n="16">
          <div type="quaestio" n="6">
            <div type="dictum" n="1">
              <p>…</p>
            </div>
            <div type="capitulum" n="2">
              <p>…</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-between">
    <head>What stands between</head>
    <p>Page breaks, notes and gaps may stand between headings, paragraphs and divisions, where the source has them.
      The application anchors each at the start of the next node: a page break before a chapter belongs to the
      chapter's first heading. The edition of the Roman council of 826 leaves out several pages after a heading,
      which the file says by a gap and a note:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.ROM-0826">
      <div type="section">
        <head type="rubric">B. CANONES CONCILII ROMANI.</head>
        <gap reason="editorial"/>
        <note type="editorial">Several pages of the edition are left out here: the introduction and the register of
          the council.</note>
        <div type="chapter" n="1">
          <head type="number"><pb n="566"/>I.</head>
          <head type="rubric">Ut episcopus bono adprobatus opere ordinetur.</head>
          <p>Beatissimi Pauli discretione atque ammonitione episcopum consecrari omnino oportet desiderabilem boni
            operis, … quae <pb n="567"/>secuntur, …</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="text-transcription">
    <head>The transcription</head>
    <p>These rules strike a balance between the practices of two projects, Capitularia and Carolingian Canon Law:
      precise where the text is concerned, plain where its layout is.</p>

    <div xml:id="transcription-sources">
      <head>Editions and manuscripts</head>
      <p>The transcription gives the text of the source, whatever it is, keeping its structure, its spelling and its
        punctuation, with the exceptions below. From an edition, the text, its divisions, spelling and punctuation are
        the editor's, obvious misprints aside. The critical apparatus isn't encoded; where it gives something the
        guide needs, such as a later hand's change, it may be used. From a manuscript, the spelling and punctuation
        are the scribe's, obvious slips aside, and the divisions follow the rubrication.</p>
      <p>Layout comes second: what is written, and how the text is organised, rather than where it stands on the
        page. So the layout is encoded only in page breaks, in where a change was made (above the line, in the
        margin), and in what can't be read.</p>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="transcription-page-breaks">
      <head>Page breaks</head>
      <p>A page break (<gi>pb</gi>) stands where the page begins, and gives the page in <att>n</att>, which is
        required: an edition's page (<val>255</val>), a manuscript's folio with <val>r</val> or <val>v</val>
        (<val>120r</val>), and a column by a letter after it (<val>356vb</val>). There's no column break: a page
        break carries its column. A page break may point to its image, the canvas of a IIIF manifest, in
        <att>facs</att>; in an edition that gives the pages of several manuscripts, each page break names its
        manuscript in <att>edRef</att> (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>). A Munich manuscript of the Collectio 400
        capitulorum turns to folio 177r inside a quotation:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
        <p>… Non duplicatio <pb n="177r"/>coniugii sed profectus est honestatis.</p>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="page-break-without-number">
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi<pb/><!-- breaks the rule --> in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      </egXML>
      <specGrp xml:id="text-page-breaks-specs">
        <elementSpec ident="pb" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="n" mode="change" usage="req" xml:id="page-break-without-number" corresp="#tier-common">
              <desc>the page, folio or column: 255, 120ra, 0187A</desc>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
      </specGrp>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="transcription-spelling">
      <head>Abbreviations, spelling, punctuation</head>
      <p>Abbreviations are expanded silently. One whose expansion is doubtful may be given with its expansions, as a
        <gi>choice</gi> of <gi>abbr</gi> and <gi>expan</gi>; the corpus has none so far, so the example is made
        up:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <p>… secundum <choice><abbr>rem</abbr><expan>rationem</expan></choice> …</p>
      </egXML>
      <p>The spelling is the source's: "ę" for an e caudata, the diphthongs <q>ae</q> and <q>oe</q> as written, and
        capitals where the manuscript has them. The transcription isn't diplomatic: no special characters for the
        letters' forms. A manuscript's punctuation may be simplified: a point as <q>.</q>, a punctus elevatus and
        other double signs as <q>;</q>, a comma as <q>,</q>. An edition's punctuation is kept. What the file
        normalises, it says once in the header (<ptr target="#header-encoding"/>).</p>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="transcription-gaps">
      <head>Gaps and lacunae</head>
      <p>A gap (<gi>gap</gi>) stands where the text is missing, and may say why, in <att>reason</att>, from a closed
        list; without a reason, the reason is unknown:</p>
      <list>
        <item><val>lacuna</val>: the witness lacks the text: lost leaves, or a lacuna in the edition it comes
          from;</item>
        <item><val>damage</val>: the support is damaged where the text stood;</item>
        <item><val>illegible</val>: the text can't be read (<gi>unclear</gi> is for what can be half read);</item>
        <item><val>reproduction</val>: the text can't be read in the images or the film used;</item>
        <item><val>deleted</val>: struck out, and not readable;</item>
        <item><val>sampling</val>: not transcribed, by the encoder's choice, as when a text is given by its incipit
          and explicit;</item>
        <item><val>editorial</val>: left out for another reason, which a note gives.</item>
      </list>
      <p>Its extent, where known, is a <att>quantity</att> with its <att>unit</att> (<val>leaves</val>,
        <val>lines</val>, <val>words</val>, <val>chars</val>), which go together, or <att>extent</att> in words. A
        text given by its incipit and explicit is one paragraph, with a gap for what is left out between them:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <p>Incipit … <gap reason="sampling" extent="the rest of the letter"/> … explicit.</p>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="gap-reason-unknown">
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant. <gap reason="missing"/><!-- breaks the rule --></p>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="gap-unit-unknown">
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant. <gap reason="lacuna" quantity="2" unit="folios"/><!-- breaks the rule --></p>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="gap-quantity-unit">
        <p>Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant. <gap reason="lacuna" quantity="2"/></p>
      </egXML>
      <p>The application counts a text's completeness from its gaps: it is never stated.</p>
      <specGrp xml:id="text-gaps-specs">
        <!-- Gaps: why the text is missing, and how much, where known. -->
        <elementSpec ident="gap" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
          <constraintSpec ident="gap-quantity-unit" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>A gap's quantity is given with its unit.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:gap[@quantity or @unit]">
                <sch:assert id="gap-quantity-unit" test="@quantity and @unit">A gap's quantity is given with its unit
                  (leaves, lines, words or chars), and a unit with its quantity.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="reason" mode="change" usage="opt" corresp="#tier-full">
              <desc>why the text is missing; without a reason, it's unknown</desc>
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="gap-reason-unknown" corresp="#tier-full">
                <valItem ident="lacuna">
                  <desc>the witness lacks the text: lost leaves, or a lacuna in the edition it comes from</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="damage">
                  <desc>the support is damaged where the text stood</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="illegible">
                  <desc>the text can't be read (unclear is for what can be half read)</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="reproduction">
                  <desc>the text can't be read in the images or the film used</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="deleted">
                  <desc>struck out, and not readable</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="sampling">
                  <desc>not transcribed, by the encoder's choice: an incipit and an explicit only</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="editorial">
                  <desc>left out for another reason, given in a note</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="unit" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
              <desc>the unit of the gap's quantity</desc>
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="gap-unit-unknown" corresp="#tier-full">
                <valItem ident="leaves"/>
                <valItem ident="lines"/>
                <valItem ident="words"/>
                <valItem ident="chars">
                  <desc>characters</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
      </specGrp>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="transcription-unclear">
      <head>Illegible text and errors</head>
      <p>What can be half read is <gi>unclear</gi>; what can't be read at all is a <gi>gap</gi> with the reason
        <val>illegible</val>, or <val>damage</val>, or <val>reproduction</val>:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
        <head type="rubric">De ablutione <unclear>infantis</unclear>.</head>
      </egXML>
      <p>An error of the source is kept, and may be marked by <gi>sic</gi>, alone or in a <gi>choice</gi> with its
        correction, <gi>corr</gi>. For the rare cases where the editor of the file adds what the source lacks, the
        addition is <gi>supplied</gi>. Both are editorial notes on the text (<ptr target="#marked-notes"/>).</p>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="transcription-changes">
      <head>Scribal changes and their hands</head>
      <p>A scribe's or a later reader's change is encoded where it stands: an addition by <gi>add</gi>, with
        <att>place</att> (<val>above</val>, <val>below</val>, <val>margin</val>, …), a deletion by <gi>del</gi>, and
        a correction, the one replacing the other, by <gi>subst</gi> holding both. Where a scribe corrected a word,
        the corrected reading is the text, and the application keeps the other reading with the change. A change
        may name the hand that made it, in <att>hand</att>, pointing to the hand described in the manuscript's
        description (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>). In the Munich manuscript of the Collectio 400 capitulorum, a
        letter is added above the line, and a word replaced:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
        <quote source="LEO_ep.167_6">Ancillam a thoro abi<add place="above">g</add>ere. …</quote>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
        <head type="rubric">Non coire cum omni <subst><del>pecode</del><add>masculo</add></subst></head>
      </egXML>
      <p>A correction whose added and deleted parts can't be told apart is a <gi>seg</gi> typed <val>corr</val>.
        Line breaks (<gi>lb</gi>) are kept where a file has them, and the import drops them.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="marked-in-the-text" n="IV">
  <head>What is marked in the text</head>
  <p>Inside its paragraphs and headings, a file may mark what the text says: the names of persons, places and events,
    the works it names, the statements it makes with their references and quotations, its formulae, the sources and
    parallels an edition gives, editorial remarks, dates and other languages, beside the scribal changes, gaps and
    unclear words of <ptr target="#text-transcription"/>. In the application a node's text is plain text, and each
    mark is an annotation of a layer, anchored to a span of the text or a point in it (<ptr target="#annotations"/>). The import splits the marked text into its plain text and its annotations; an export
    writes each annotation back in the same form, inline where it nests in the others, and in the standOff where it
    doesn't (<ptr target="#exports"/>).</p>
  <p>Nothing in this part is required. Names of persons are <hi>common</hi>, since most files mark them; the rest is
    <hi>full</hi>: a file marks as much as its encoder claims.</p>

  <div xml:id="marked-names">
    <head>Names and their records</head>
    <p>A person's, a place's or an event's name is marked by <gi>persName</gi>, <gi>placeName</gi> or
      <gi>eventName</gi>, around the words of the text, and points to its record in <att>ref</att>, in one of three
      forms:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a placeholder: <code>#new_</code>, the kind of record, <code>_</code>, and a name of the encoder's own
        (<code>#new_person_Arno</code>), the same for every name of that record in the file;</item>
      <item>an id in the file (<code>#PERS.1234</code>), where the record is listed in the file's standOff (<ptr target="#records"/>);</item>
      <item>a permalink of the application (<code>https://…/id/person/PERS.1234</code>).</item>
    </list>
    <p>The import makes a provisional record of each placeholder, one for all the names that share it, for the
      project to identify; it uses a permalink as given, without matching: a record merged since forwards to the
      record that replaced it, and a permalink that leads nowhere is reported. An export writes permalinks. In the
      preface of the Council of Mainz of 813, the king's envoys name themselves and the city:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <p>Almifice reverentiae vestrae patefacimus nos humillimi famuli ac missi vestri, <persName ref="#new_person_Hildibaldus">Hildibaldus</persName> scilicet sacri palatii archiepiscopus, <persName ref="#new_person_rihholfus">Rihholfus</persName> et <persName ref="#new_person_Arno">Arno</persName>
        archiepiscopi seu <persName ref="#new_person_Bernhard">Bernharius</persName>, una cum reliquis coepiscopis
        atque abbatibus et caetero clero, quia venimus secundum iussionem vestram in civitatem <placeName ref="#new_place_Mainz">Mogontiam</placeName> …</p>
    </egXML>
    <p>An event is named the same way; the council names itself:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <p>… Placuit itaque <eventName ref="#new_event_mainz">sancto concilio,</eventName> ut ita discernantur, sicut
        in regula clericorum dictum est. …</p>
    </egXML>
    <p>A pointer to a record has one of the three forms, or fails the RelaxNG; a placeholder without its
      <code>#</code> is the commonest slip:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="record-pointer-form">
      <p>Placuit <persName ref="new_person_Paul">Paulo</persName><!-- breaks the rule --> ut episcopi maneant.</p>
    </egXML>
    <p>A record of a kind no name element fits, a diocese or a religious order, is marked by <gi>rs</gi> (<ptr target="#marked-other"/>).</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="marked-names-specs">
      <!-- Names point to their records: a placeholder until the import resolves it, then a permalink. -->
      <dataSpec ident="solemne.recordPointer" mode="add" xml:id="record-pointer-form" corresp="#tier-full">
        <desc>A pointer to a record: a placeholder (#new_person_…), an identifier in the file, or a permalink.</desc>
        <content>
          <dataRef name="token" restriction="#new_[a-z]+_\S+|#[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.\-]*|https?://\S+"/>
        </content>
      </dataSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="persName" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="placeName" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="eventName" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="marked-works">
    <head>Works named</head>
    <p>A work the text names, a book of the Bible, a Father's treatise, a council's canons, is marked by
      <gi>bibl</gi>, and points to the work's record in <att>ref</att>, in the forms of the names' records. The names
      inside it stay names. The council of 813 sets its bishops to read the Gospel and Gregory's pastoral book, and
      quotes Jerome's letter to Nepotian:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <p>… legentes atque tractantes <bibl ref="#new_bibl_gospels">sanctum evangelium</bibl> necnon epistolas et
        actus apostolorum, canones quoque ac diversa sanctorum patrum opuscula <bibl ref="#new_bibl_pastoralcare">pastoralemque librum Gregorii</bibl> cum caeteris sacris dogmatibus …</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <cit><ref>Tamen <persName ref="#new_person_jerome">Hieronimus</persName> in <bibl ref="#new_bibl_nepotianum">epistola ad <persName ref="#new_person_Nepotianum">Nepotianum</persName></bibl>
          dicit:</ref> <quote>Omnium Christianorum domos quasi proprias amare debemus, …</quote></cit>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="marked-works-specs">
      <elementSpec ident="bibl" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="marked-statements">
    <head>Statements, references, quotations, formulae</head>
    <p>A statement cites an authority: it is a <gi>cit</gi> holding its reference, <gi>ref</gi>, the words that
      name the authority, and its quotation, <gi>quote</gi>, the words quoted. Whether the quotation is exact, or
      rightly attributed, doesn't change the form: the statements attributed to an authority are worth finding even
      where the attribution is wrong. A quotation names its source in <att>source</att> (<ptr target="#marked-pointers"/>); the words that introduce an authority by a set phrase ("ut lex dicit", "unde
      psalmista commemorat") are a formula, a <gi>seg</gi> typed <val>formula</val>, marked and not yet identified.
      Chapter II of the Council of Mainz, 813, is a chain of statements:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <div type="chapter" n="2">
        <head type="rubric">De spe habenda in <persName ref="#new_person_god">Deum</persName>.</head>
        <head type="number">Cap.II</head>
        <p>Spes vero in <persName ref="#new_person_god">Deum</persName> firmiter a cunctis Christianis tenenda est.
            <cit><ref><seg type="formula">Unde psalmista commemorat:</seg></ref> <quote source="PSA_61_9">Sperate in
              eo omnis congregatio populi et reliqua</quote></cit>, <cit><ref><seg type="formula">et
              iterum:</seg></ref> <quote source="PSA_36_3">Spera in <persName ref="#new_person_god">Domino</persName>
            et fac bonitatem</quote></cit>, … <cit><ref><seg type="formula">et apostolus:</seg></ref> <quote source="ROM_8_24">Spe enim salvi facti sumus.</quote></cit></p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>The application reads a statement as three annotations: the reference, the quotation with its source, and the
      attribution, the source the reference claims. A reference whose claim sits in a heading rather than in the
      text's words is typed <val>from_paratext</val>, the only type a reference in the text takes; a link to a
      manuscript's images is typed <val>iiif</val> (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>). A quotation has no type: its
      source goes in <att>source</att>. A formula, and every other <gi>seg</gi>, has a type from its closed list.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="reference-type-unknown">
      <cit><ref type="paratext">Dominus ait:</ref><!-- breaks the rule --> <quote source="MAT_5_3">Beati pauperes</quote></cit>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="quotation-typed">
      <cit><ref>Apostolus ait:</ref> <quote type="ROM_12_5">unum corpus sumus</quote><!-- breaks the rule --></cit>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="seg-type-missing">
      <p><seg>Si quis</seg><!-- breaks the rule --> episcopus migrauerit, deponatur.</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="seg-type-misspelled">
      <p><seg type="fomula">Si quis</seg><!-- breaks the rule --> episcopus migrauerit, deponatur.</p>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="marked-statements-specs">
      <!-- Quotations; and fragments: a span that crosses others or the structure is split into fragments joined by
           @next and @prev, each nesting where it stands. -->
      <elementSpec ident="quote" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="fragment-next" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A fragment's @next points to the next fragment in the file, of the same kind, whose @prev points
            back.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@next]">
              <sch:let name="next" value="//tei:*[@xml:id = substring-after(normalize-space(current()/@next), '#')]"/>
              <sch:assert id="fragment-next" test="count($next) = 1 and local-name($next) = local-name() and                 normalize-space($next/@prev) = concat('#', @xml:id)">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt;'s @next (<sch:value-of select="@next"/>) points to the next fragment in the file, a &lt;<sch:name/>&gt; whose @prev points
                back.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="fragment-prev" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A fragment's @prev points to the previous fragment in the file, of the same kind, whose @next points
            back.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@prev]">
              <sch:let name="prev" value="//tei:*[@xml:id = substring-after(normalize-space(current()/@prev), '#')]"/>
              <sch:assert id="fragment-prev" test="count($prev) = 1 and local-name($prev) = local-name() and                 normalize-space($prev/@next) = concat('#', @xml:id)">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt;'s @prev (<sch:value-of select="@prev"/>) points to the previous fragment in the file, a &lt;<sch:name/>&gt; whose @next
                points back.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="fragment-attributes" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>Only the first fragment carries the attributes that describe the whole span; a later one carries its
            xml:id, @prev and @next, and the attributes the RelaxNG requires (a segment's type), with the first
            fragment's values.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@prev]">
              <sch:let name="prev" value="//tei:*[@xml:id = substring-after(normalize-space(current()/@prev), '#')]"/>
              <sch:let name="other" value="@*[not(name() = ('xml:id', 'prev', 'next'))]"/>
              <sch:assert id="fragment-attributes" test="every $a in $other satisfies (self::tei:seg and name($a) =                 'type' and $a = $prev/@type)">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt; is a later fragment (it has @prev): it carries only
                its xml:id, @prev and @next, and the span's other attributes (<sch:value-of select="string-join(for $a                 in $other return concat('@', name($a)), ', ')"/>) go on its first fragment.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="fragment-order" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A fragment's @next points to an element later in the file: the fragments follow the text's
            order.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@next]">
              <sch:let name="next" value="//tei:*[@xml:id = substring-after(normalize-space(current()/@next), '#')]"/>
              <sch:assert id="fragment-order" test="every $n in $next satisfies $n &gt;&gt; .">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt;'s @next
                (<sch:value-of select="@next"/>) points to a fragment later in the file: the fragments follow the
                text's order.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="source" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>the source quoted: see the pointers' forms</desc>
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.pointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="delete" xml:id="quotation-typed" corresp="#tier-full"/>
          <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="ref" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="reference-type-unknown" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="from_paratext">
                <desc>a statement's reference whose claim sits in a heading</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="iiif">
                <desc>in a manuscript's surrogates: a IIIF manifest of its images</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="marked-pointers">
    <head>Sources and the pointers' forms</head>
    <p>A quotation's <att>source</att>, and a source note's, hold one pointer or more, separated by spaces, each in
      one of these forms:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a document's code (<ptr target="#codes-and-ids"/>), alone (<code>C.NIC-0325</code>) or with a citation
        after an underscore, the canon or chapter of that document (<code>C.NIC-0325_3</code>,
        <code>D.LEO-0447-414_11</code>, <code>COLL.Dach.ms-Brn-Burg-425_2.49</code>); an underscore stands only
        there;</item>
      <item>a Bible reference: a book's code, the chapter, and the verse, each range with a hyphen
        (<code>MAT_18_19</code>, <code>PHP_3_17-20</code>, <code>JOH_1</code>); the Psalms by the Vulgate's
        numbers;</item>
      <item>a work by its siglum in Gryson's <title>Répertoire général des auteurs ecclésiastiques latins</title>:
        the author, the work and the place cited (<code>IS_ety_1.2.3</code>, <code>LEO_ep.167_6</code>);</item>
      <item>an SCTA resource, by its address (<code>http://scta.info/resource/…</code>).</item>
    </list>
    <p>The first three may begin with <code>cf_</code> for a loose reference: a parallel or an echo rather than a
      quotation. The Collectio Dacheriana, in d'Achery's edition, echoes Ezekiel:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.Dach">
      <p>… uenia peccatorum fideliter præsumatur ab illo, qui <quote source="cf_EZE_18_23">non uult mortem peccatoris,
          sed ut conuertatur</quote> a perditione poenitendo, et saluatus miseratione Domini uiuat. …</p>
    </egXML>
    <p>The 73 books of the Bible have one code each. Their names are the Nova Vulgata's: the older Vulgate's I to IV
      Regum are <code>1SA</code>, <code>2SA</code>, <code>1KI</code> and <code>2KI</code>. <code>solemne-tei
        lists</code> prints the same table.</p>
    <table xml:id="bible-codes">
      <head>The Bible's books and their codes</head>
      <row role="label">
        <cell>Code</cell>
        <cell>Book</cell>
      </row>
          <row><cell><code>GEN</code></cell><cell>Genesis</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>EXO</code></cell><cell>Exodus</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>LEV</code></cell><cell>Leviticus</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>NUM</code></cell><cell>Numeri</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>DEU</code></cell><cell>Deuteronomium</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JOS</code></cell><cell>Iosue</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JDG</code></cell><cell>Iudicum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>RUT</code></cell><cell>Ruth</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1SA</code></cell><cell>I Samuelis</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2SA</code></cell><cell>II Samuelis</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1KI</code></cell><cell>I Regum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2KI</code></cell><cell>II Regum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1CH</code></cell><cell>I Paralipomenon</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2CH</code></cell><cell>II Paralipomenon</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>EZR</code></cell><cell>Esdrae</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>NEH</code></cell><cell>Nehemiae</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>TOB</code></cell><cell>Tobiae</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JDT</code></cell><cell>Iudith</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>EST</code></cell><cell>Esther</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1MA</code></cell><cell>I Machabaeorum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2MA</code></cell><cell>II Machabaeorum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JOB</code></cell><cell>Iob</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>PSA</code></cell><cell>Psalmi</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>PRO</code></cell><cell>Proverbia</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>ECC</code></cell><cell>Ecclesiastes</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>SOS</code></cell><cell>Canticum Canticorum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>WIS</code></cell><cell>Sapientia</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>SIR</code></cell><cell>Ecclesiasticus</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>ISA</code></cell><cell>Isaias</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JER</code></cell><cell>Ieremias</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>LAM</code></cell><cell>Lamentationes</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>BAR</code></cell><cell>Baruch</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>EZE</code></cell><cell>Ezechiel</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>DAN</code></cell><cell>Daniel</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>HOS</code></cell><cell>Osee</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JOE</code></cell><cell>Ioel</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>AMO</code></cell><cell>Amos</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>OBA</code></cell><cell>Abdias</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JON</code></cell><cell>Ionas</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>MIC</code></cell><cell>Michaeas</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>NAH</code></cell><cell>Nahum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>HAB</code></cell><cell>Habacuc</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>ZEP</code></cell><cell>Sophonias</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>HAG</code></cell><cell>Aggaeus</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>ZEC</code></cell><cell>Zacharias</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>MAL</code></cell><cell>Malachias</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>MAT</code></cell><cell>Matthaeus</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>MAR</code></cell><cell>Marcus</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>LUK</code></cell><cell>Lucas</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JOH</code></cell><cell>Ioannes</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>ACT</code></cell><cell>Actus Apostolorum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>ROM</code></cell><cell>Romanos</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1CO</code></cell><cell>I Corinthios</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2CO</code></cell><cell>II Corinthios</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>GAL</code></cell><cell>Galatas</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>EPH</code></cell><cell>Ephesios</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>PHP</code></cell><cell>Philippenses</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>COL</code></cell><cell>Colossenses</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1TH</code></cell><cell>I Thessalonicenses</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2TH</code></cell><cell>II Thessalonicenses</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1TI</code></cell><cell>I Timotheum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2TI</code></cell><cell>II Timotheum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>TIT</code></cell><cell>Titum</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>PHM</code></cell><cell>Philemonem</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>HEB</code></cell><cell>Hebraeos</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JAM</code></cell><cell>Iacobi</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1PE</code></cell><cell>I Petri</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2PE</code></cell><cell>II Petri</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>1JO</code></cell><cell>I Ioannis</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>2JO</code></cell><cell>II Ioannis</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>3JO</code></cell><cell>III Ioannis</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>JUD</code></cell><cell>Iudae</cell></row>
          <row><cell><code>REV</code></cell><cell>Apocalypsis</cell></row>
    </table>
    <p>The RelaxNG checks each pointer's form: an older code, an underscore inside a code, a book that isn't among
      the 73 or a verse that isn't a number fails. It doesn't check that the document or the verse exists; the
      import resolves each pointer into a relation to a canon, and reports what it can't resolve.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="source-pointer-form">
      <p>Placuit ut episcopi maneant.<note type="source" source="CNIC.325.2"/><!-- breaks the rule: an older code --></p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="source-pointer-form">
      <p>Placuit ut episcopi maneant.<note type="source" source="C.NIC_0325_3"/><!-- breaks the rule: an underscore in the code --></p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="source-pointer-form">
      <cit><ref>Dominus ait:</ref> <quote source="MATT_5_3">Beati pauperes</quote><!-- breaks the rule: no such book --></cit>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="source-pointer-form">
      <cit><ref>Apostolus ait:</ref> <quote source="1CO_9_14f">qui euangelium adnuntiant</quote><!-- breaks the rule: no such verse --></cit>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="marked-pointers-specs">
      <!-- The pointers' forms, for quote/@source and note/@source. -->
      <dataSpec ident="solemne.pointer" mode="add" xml:id="source-pointer-form" corresp="#tier-full">
        <desc>A pointer to a source: a document's code, alone or with a citation after an underscore (C.NIC-0325_3); a
          Bible reference by book, chapter and verse (MAT_18_19, PHP_3_17-20); a Gryson siglum and the place cited
          (IS_ety_1.2.3); each may start with cf_ for a loose reference; or an SCTA resource. A code holds letters,
          digits, dots and hyphens; an underscore only separates a code from its citation.</desc>
        <content>
          <dataRef name="token" restriction="(cf_)?((C|D|COLL|P|EP|H|EX|W)\.[A-Za-z0-9]+([.\-][A-Za-z0-9]+)*(_[A-Za-z0-9]+([.\-][A-Za-z0-9]+)*)?|(GEN|EXO|LEV|NUM|DEU|JOS|JDG|RUT|1SA|2SA|1KI|2KI|1CH|2CH|EZR|NEH|TOB|JDT|EST|1MA|2MA|JOB|PSA|PRO|ECC|SOS|WIS|SIR|ISA|JER|LAM|BAR|EZE|DAN|HOS|JOE|AMO|OBA|JON|MIC|NAH|HAB|ZEP|HAG|ZEC|MAL|MAT|MAR|LUK|JOH|ACT|ROM|1CO|2CO|GAL|EPH|PHP|COL|1TH|2TH|1TI|2TI|TIT|PHM|HEB|JAM|1PE|2PE|1JO|2JO|3JO|JUD|REV)_[0-9]+(-[0-9]+)?(_[0-9]+(-[0-9]+)?)?|[A-Z][A-Za-z]*(-[A-Z][A-Za-z]*)*_[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*(\.[A-Za-z0-9]+)*(_[A-Za-z0-9]+([.\-][A-Za-z0-9]+)*)?)|https?://scta\.info/resource/\S+"/>
        </content>
      </dataSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="marked-notes">
    <head>Notes: sources, parallels, editorial remarks</head>
    <p>A note in the text has a type, its layer:</p>
    <list>
      <item><val>source</val>: where a passage comes from, as an edition says it: its words, and the codes of its
        sources in <att>source</att>;</item>
      <item><val>parallel</val>: a parallel elsewhere, an edition's "Cf." note;</item>
      <item><val>editorial</val>: a remark of the editor of the file, about the text or the transcription.</item>
    </list>
    <p>A note stands where its source puts it: at a point of a paragraph, or between paragraphs and divisions,
      where it annotates the division. The Council of Mainz, 813, in its chapter on the sacraments, cites Leo's
      decretal, which its editor identifies in a note, and the editor notes a parallel in a later council:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.MAI-0813">
      <div type="chapter" n="4">
        <head type="rubric">De sacramentis ecclesiae.</head>
        <head type="number">IIII</head>
        <p>Sacramenta itaque baptismatis volumus, … <cit><ref>sicut in decretis <persName ref="#new_person_leo">Leonis papae</persName> sub duobus continetur capitulis, in capitulo
              XL:</ref> <quote source="D.LEO-0447-414_11">Haec duo tempora, id est Pascha et Pentecosten, ad
              baptizandum a Romano pontifice legitima praefixa sunt<note type="source">Leonis decreta c. 11
                (Jaffe-K. n. 414).</note></quote></cit>, …</p>
        <note type="parallel">Cf. concil. Mogunt. 847 c. 3; l. I. p. 176.</note>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>Where an edition's source note is only codes, they go in <att>source</att>, in the pointers' forms, and the
      note holds no words; codes left in a note's words are reported. A note holds words, or its codes, or both.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <p>… legitima praefixa sunt<note type="source" source="D.LEO-0447-414_11"/> …</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="source-note-codes">
      <p>Placuit ut episcopi maneant.<note type="source">C.NIC-0325_15</note></p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="note-has-content">
      <p>Placuit ut episcopi maneant.<note type="editorial"/></p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="note-type-misspelled">
      <p>Placuit ut episcopi maneant.<note type="souce" source="C.NIC-0325_3"/><!-- breaks the rule --></p>
    </egXML>
    <p>Notes typed <val>links</val>, <val>authorities</val> and <val>extra</val> belong to records (<ptr target="#records"/>); in the text, they're errors.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="text-note-type">
      <p>Placuit ut episcopi maneant.<note type="links">See elsewhere.</note></p>
    </egXML>
    <p>The import keeps a source note's words and codes until the project resolves them into relations; a parallel
      and an editorial note keep their words, and an editorial note its author. An error of the source marked by
      <gi>sic</gi>, or an addition by <gi>supplied</gi>, is an editorial note too (<ptr target="#transcription-unclear"/>).</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="marked-notes-specs">
      <!-- Notes: in the text, typed by their layer. -->
      <elementSpec ident="note" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="text-note-type" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>In the text, a note's type is its layer.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:body//tei:note">
              <sch:assert id="text-note-type" test="@type = ('source', 'parallel', 'editorial')">A note in the text
                is typed source, parallel or editorial.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="note-has-content" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A note holds words, or its codes in source.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:note">
              <sch:assert id="note-has-content" test="normalize-space(.) != '' or @source or *">A note holds words,
                or its codes in @source.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="source-note-codes" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A source note's codes are in source, not in its words.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:body//tei:note[@type = 'source']">
              <sch:report id="source-note-codes" role="warning" test="some $w in tokenize(normalize-space(.),                 '[\s;,]+') satisfies matches($w,                 '^(cf_)?((C|D|COLL|P|EP|H|EX|W)\.[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+(_[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+)?|[1-4]?[A-Z]{2,4}_[0-9]+(_[0-9]+)?(-[0-9]+)?)$')">A
                source note's words hold a code (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>): it belongs in
                @source.</sch:report>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="note-type-misspelled" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="source">
                <desc>in the text: the source a passage comes from, its codes in source</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="parallel">
                <desc>in the text: a parallel elsewhere ("Cf. concil. Mogunt. 847 c. 3")</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="editorial">
                <desc>in the text: an editor's remark</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="links">
                <desc>in a record: links to pages about it that aren't an authority's record</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="authorities">
                <desc>in a record, in earlier files: an authority's numbers, which the import reads as its ids</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="extra">
                <desc>in a record: the fields its form has no element for, in a feature structure</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="source" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>the sources the note points to: see the pointers' forms</desc>
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.pointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="marked-dates">
    <head>Dates</head>
    <p>A date in the text is marked by <gi>date</gi>, in the one form of dates that records and relations use too:
      the wording as the text gives it, and its bounds in attributes. It names the calendar of its words: a day the
      text reckons in the Julian calendar says so, and its bounds give the Gregorian day (<ptr target="#dates"/>). The coded files mark none yet; the
      example is made up: V Idus Iunias is 9 June in the Julian calendar, 13 June in the Gregorian in 813.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <calendarDesc>
        <calendar xml:id="julian-mainz">
          <p>The Julian calendar.</p>
        </calendar>
      </calendarDesc>
      <p>… Data <date when="0813-06-13" calendar="#julian-mainz">V Idus Iunias</date> …</p>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="marked-languages">
    <head>Languages</head>
    <p>Words in another language than the text's are marked by <gi>foreign</gi>, the language in
      <att>xml:lang</att>; the text's own language is said once in the header. A made-up example:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <p>… quod Graeci <foreign xml:lang="grc">ὁμοούσιον</foreign> dicunt …</p>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="marked-other">
    <head>What no element fits</head>
    <p>TEI has an element for most of what the layers mark. For the rest:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a kind of mark with no element of its own is a <gi>seg</gi>, typed by its layer, and its term, where the
        layer has terms, in <att>ana</att>, pointing to a category of a taxonomy declared in the header;</item>
      <item>a record of a kind no name element fits, a diocese or a religious order, is an <gi>rs</gi>, its kind in
        <att>type</att> and its record in <att>ref</att>, in the forms of the names' records;</item>
      <item>a span that crosses others or the structure, a quotation over several paragraphs, is split into
        fragments;</item>
      <item>a point, or a span that can't be marked inline at all, is marked by anchors, which a stand-off annotation
        points to.</item>
    </list>
    <p>A segment's term, made up:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <taxonomy xml:id="formulae">
        <category xml:id="formula-of-the-law">
          <catDesc>A formula that cites the law</catDesc>
        </category>
      </taxonomy>
      <p>… <seg type="formula" ana="#formula-of-the-law">ut lex dicit</seg> …</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="seg-term-declared">
      <p><seg type="formula" ana="#formula-of-authority">ut lex dicit</seg></p>
    </egXML>
    <p>A record of another kind, made up:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <p>… per <rs type="diocese" ref="#new_diocese_Moguntia">dioecesim</rs> …</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="record-pointer-form">
      <p>Placuit ut episcopi per <rs type="diocese" ref="new_diocese_Samplia">dioecesim</rs><!-- breaks the rule --> maneant.</p>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="marked-other-specs">
      <elementSpec ident="seg" module="linking" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="seg-term-declared" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A segment's term, in @ana, is a category of a taxonomy in the header.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:seg[@ana]">
              <sch:let name="declared" value="/tei:TEI/tei:teiHeader//tei:classDecl//tei:category/@xml:id"/>
              <sch:assert id="seg-term-declared" test="every $t in tokenize(normalize-space(@ana), ' ') satisfies                 (starts-with($t, '#') and substring($t, 2) = $declared)">A segment's term (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(@ana)"/>) points to a category of a taxonomy in the header's
                classDecl.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ana" mode="add" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>the segment's term: a category of a taxonomy in the header</desc>
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="teidata.pointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req" xml:id="seg-type-missing" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="seg-type-misspelled" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="formula">
                <desc>a formula introducing an authority ("ut lex dicit")</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="corr">
                <desc>a scribe's correction</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="rs" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>

    <div xml:id="marked-fragments">
      <head>Fragments</head>
      <p>A span that can't nest where it stands is split into fragments, each nesting in its paragraph or heading,
        joined by <att>next</att> and <att>prev</att>: each fragment has an <att>xml:id</att>, each but the last
        points to the next by <att>next</att>, and each but the first to the one before by <att>prev</att>. The
        Collectio 400 capitulorum quotes two passages over several paragraphs; they're fragments. The rules:</p>
      <list>
        <item>each fragment's <att>next</att> and <att>prev</att> point to an element of the same kind in the file,
          which points back (<code>fragment-next</code>, <code>fragment-prev</code>);</item>
        <item><att>next</att> points to an element later in the file: the fragments follow the text's order, so a
          chain has one first fragment and no loop (<code>fragment-order</code>);</item>
        <item>only the first fragment carries the attributes that describe the whole span, a quotation's
          <att>source</att>, a segment's <att>ana</att>, a name's <att>ref</att>; a later one carries its
          <att>xml:id</att>, <att>prev</att> and <att>next</att>, and a segment's <att>type</att>, which the RelaxNG
          requires on every segment, with the first fragment's value (<code>fragment-attributes</code>). So two
          fragments can't disagree.</item>
      </list>
      <p>A quotation over two paragraphs, and a formula over two, made up:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <div type="chapter" n="1">
          <head type="number">I.</head>
          <p><quote xml:id="fragments-a" next="#fragments-b" source="C.NIC-0325_15">Placuit ut episcopi in
              ciuitatibus suis</quote></p>
          <p><quote xml:id="fragments-b" prev="#fragments-a">maneant.</quote></p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <div type="chapter" n="2">
          <head type="number">II.</head>
          <p>Placuit ut <seg type="formula" xml:id="formula-a" next="#formula-b">si quis</seg></p>
          <p><seg type="formula" xml:id="formula-b" prev="#formula-a">episcopus</seg> migrauerit, deponatur.</p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="fragment-next">
        <div type="chapter" n="1">
          <head type="number">I.</head>
          <p><quote xml:id="next-wrong-a" next="#next-wrong-c">Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis</quote></p>
          <p><quote xml:id="next-wrong-b">maneant.</quote></p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="fragment-prev">
        <div type="chapter" n="1">
          <head type="number">I.</head>
          <p><quote xml:id="prev-wrong-a">Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis</quote></p>
          <p><quote xml:id="prev-wrong-b" prev="#prev-wrong-a">maneant.</quote></p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="fragment-order">
        <div type="chapter" n="1">
          <head type="number">I.</head>
          <p><quote xml:id="order-wrong-b" prev="#order-wrong-a">maneant.</quote></p>
          <p><quote xml:id="order-wrong-a" next="#order-wrong-b" source="C.NIC-0325_15">Placuit ut episcopi in
              ciuitatibus suis</quote></p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="fragment-attributes">
        <div type="chapter" n="1">
          <head type="number">I.</head>
          <p><quote xml:id="attributes-wrong-a" next="#attributes-wrong-b" source="C.NIC-0325_15">Placuit ut episcopi
              in ciuitatibus suis</quote></p>
          <p><quote xml:id="attributes-wrong-b" prev="#attributes-wrong-a" source="C.NIC-0325_15">maneant.</quote></p>
        </div>
      </egXML>
      <p>The import makes one annotation of the fragments, over the whole span.</p>
    </div>

    <div xml:id="marked-anchors">
      <head>Anchors</head>
      <p>An <gi>anchor</gi> marks a point. A stand-off <gi>annotation</gi>, in the standOff's
        <gi>listAnnotation</gi>, points to one anchor, or to the range between two by TEI's <code>range()</code>.
        It's the form for what can't be marked inline at all; an export uses it for annotations that cross stronger
        ones (<ptr target="#exports"/>). A made-up example:</p>
      <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
        <p>Placuit <anchor xml:id="span-start"/>ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant<anchor xml:id="span-end"/>.</p>
        <listAnnotation>
          <annotation xml:id="annotation-1" target="#range(span-start,span-end)">
            <note type="editorial">A remark on the whole clause.</note>
          </annotation>
        </listAnnotation>
      </egXML>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="records" n="V">
  <head>Records</head>
  <p>A record is a thing the texts are about, or come from, which the application keeps once and every file points
    to: a person, a place, an institution, an event, a manuscript, a publication, a work and its versions. A file
    names records where its text names them (<ptr target="#marked-names"/>), and may list them, with what it knows of
    them, in its <gi>standOff</gi>: persons in <gi>listPerson</gi>, places in <gi>listPlace</gi>, institutions in
    <gi>listOrg</gi>, events in <gi>listEvent</gi>, works, versions and publications in <gi>listBibl</gi>. Every
    record may have its names, its dates in the one form (<ptr target="#dates"/>), its ids elsewhere, its sources,
    notes, and its relations to other records (<ptr target="#relations"/>). Manuscripts have a part of their own
    (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>), and publications will (<ptr target="#editions-and-literature"/>).</p>
  <p>Persons are <hi>common</hi>, since most files list those they name; the rest is <hi>full</hi>.</p>

  <div xml:id="records-ids">
    <head>Placeholders, ids and permalinks</head>
    <p>A record listed in a file has an <att>xml:id</att>, which the file's names point to by <att>ref</att>
      (<code>#new_person_Arno</code>):</p>
    <list>
      <item>a placeholder's, for a record the application doesn't have yet: <code>new_</code>, the kind, and a name of
        the encoder's own (<code>new_person_Arno</code>, <code>new_place_Mainz</code>); the import makes a
        provisional record of it, with what the entry holds, for the project to identify;</item>
      <item>a record's, for one it has: its kind and its number (<code>PERS.1234</code>); a work's or a version's is
        its code (<code>C.NIC-0325</code>).</item>
    </list>
    <p>A name may also point to a record the file doesn't list, by its permalink
      (<code>https://…/id/person/PERS.1234</code>), which the import uses as given. An export points by permalinks,
      and lists the records it points to, with their names, so that the file reads on its own (<ptr target="#exports"/>). The records of the Council of Carthage of 397 list its two councils by placeholders,
      with their dates:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.CAR-0397">
      <listEvent>
        <event xml:id="new_event_carthage_0397" when="0397">
          <eventName>Council of Carthage, 397</eventName>
        </event>
        <event xml:id="new_event_hippo_0393" when="0393">
          <eventName>Council of Hippo, 393</eventName>
        </event>
      </listEvent>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-persons">
    <head>Persons</head>
    <p>A person is a <gi>person</gi>, with one main name, <gi>persName</gi> typed <val>main</val>, and any number
      typed <val>alternative</val>: the spellings and forms the sources use. A person's life is dated by
      <gi>birth</gi>, <gi>death</gi> and <gi>floruit</gi>, in the one form. Simplician of Milan, in the records of the
      Council of Carthage of 397, revised to the one form: born about 320, died in 400 or 401:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.CAR-0397">
      <person xml:id="new_person_simplicianus">
        <persName type="main">Simplician, bishop of Milan (?)</persName>
        <birth notBefore="0315" notAfter="0325" when-custom="0320~" datingMethod="#edtf">c. 320</birth>
        <death notBefore="0400" notAfter="0401" when-custom="[0400,0401]" datingMethod="#edtf">400 or 401</death>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <p>A person in the standOff has a record's id or a placeholder's; a placeholder's person has one main name; a
      floruit gives its dates. Each is checked:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="person-id">
      <person xml:id="person_7">
        <persName type="main">Exemplus</persName>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="person-main-name">
      <person xml:id="new_person_Secundus">
        <persName type="alternative">Secundus</persName>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="floruit-dates">
      <person xml:id="new_person_Quintus">
        <persName type="main">Quintus</persName>
        <floruit/>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <p>Offices and roles, a scribe's hand, an author's works, are relations from the person (<ptr target="#relations"/>), not parts of the record.</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="records-persons-specs">
      <elementSpec ident="person" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <constraintSpec ident="person-id" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-common">
          <desc>A person listed in the standOff is a record or a placeholder for one.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:standOff//tei:listPerson/tei:person">
              <sch:assert id="person-id" test="matches(@xml:id, '^(PERS\.\d+|solemne_person_\d+|new_person_\S+)$')">A
                person in the standOff has an @xml:id: a record's (PERS. and a number, or solemne_person_ and a
                number), or a placeholder's (new_person_ and a name).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="person-main-name" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-common">
          <desc>A new person has one main name.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:standOff//tei:listPerson/tei:person">
              <sch:assert id="person-main-name" test="count(tei:persName[@type = 'main']) = 1 or                 not(starts-with(@xml:id, 'new_person_'))">A new person has one main name (persName typed
                main).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>

      <elementSpec ident="floruit" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="floruit-dates" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A floruit gives its dates.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:floruit">
              <sch:assert id="floruit-dates" test="@when or @notBefore or @notAfter or @from or @to or @period">A
                floruit gives its dates in its attributes.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-places">
    <head>Places</head>
    <p>A place is a <gi>place</gi>, with its name, <gi>placeName</gi>, and, where known, its <gi>location</gi>: the
      country, region and settlement it lies in, and its coordinates in <gi>geo</gi> (latitude, then longitude).
      Benediktbeuern, which owned a manuscript in Munich, as Mirabile gives it:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <place xml:id="new_place_benediktbeuern">
        <placeName>Benediktbeuern</placeName>
        <location>
          <country>Germania</country>
          <region>Bayern</region>
          <settlement>Benediktbeuern</settlement>
        </location>
      </place>
    </egXML>
    <p>A diocese, a province, a kingdom: each a place, its kind in <att>type</att> (<ptr target="#records-rare"/>).</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-institutions">
    <head>Institutions</head>
    <p>An institution, a religious house, a chapter, a library, is an <gi>org</gi>, its kind in <att>type</att>, with
      one main name, <gi>orgName</gi> typed <val>main</val>. Where it stands is a relation to a place, as an owner's
      is (<ptr target="#relations"/>). A name of an institution elsewhere in the file points to its record by
      <att>ref</att>, as other names do. The abbey of Benediktbeuern, with its id in Mirabile:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <org xml:id="new_org_benediktbeuern" type="religiousHouse">
        <orgName type="main">SS. Benedikt und Jakob, abbazia OSB</orgName>
        <idno type="mirabile">6074</idno>
      </org>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="records-institutions-specs">
      <elementSpec ident="orgName" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-events">
    <head>Events</head>
    <p>An event, a council or a synod, is an <gi>event</gi>, dated in its attributes in the one form. TEI 4.12 lets
      it hold a name (<gi>eventName</gi>), or a description, or paragraphs, not two of them; a name is what the
      import reads first. Where it took place, and what it enacted, are relations (<ptr target="#relations"/>). An
      event points to a record by <att>ref</att>, or describes one, and its description has words:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="event-described">
      <event when="0600">
        <desc>A council.</desc>
      </event>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="event-words">
      <event when="0601">
        <p/>
      </event>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="records-events-specs">
      <elementSpec ident="event" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="event-described" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An event points to a record, or describes one.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:event">
              <sch:assert id="event-described" test="@ref or tei:eventName or tei:p">An event points to a record
                (@ref) or describes one, with eventName or p.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="event-words" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An event's description isn't empty.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:event/tei:desc | tei:event/tei:p">
              <sch:assert id="event-words" test="normalize-space(.) != ''">An event's description isn't
                empty.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-works">
    <head>Works and versions</head>
    <p>A work, a council's canons or a collection, and each of its versions, a translation or a recension, is a
      <gi>bibl</gi> in <gi>listBibl</gi>, typed <val>work</val> or <val>version</val> (<ptr target="#levels"/>). It
      holds its title, its author by <att>ref</att>, its code (an untyped <gi>idno</gi>), its category (an
      <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>category</val>), and its date in the one form. A version is a version of its work by a
      relation (<ptr target="#relations-levels"/>). Mirabile's Institutiones of Justinian, from a manuscript's
      records, revised to the form of a work:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <listBibl>
        <bibl type="work" xml:id="new_work_institutiones">
          <author ref="#new_person_iustinianus">Iustinianus imperator</author>
          <title>Institutiones</title>
          <idno type="mirabile">2106</idno>
        </bibl>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <p>A work names its canonical version, whose canons are the work's, and, where that version has no text in the
      application, the version whose text stands in for it; otherwise the earliest version with a text stands in.
      Both are defaults the project chooses, not claims about the texts, so they are pointers in the work's record,
      <gi>ptr</gi> typed <val>canonical</val> and <val>stand-in</val>, to the versions' records. Nicaea's canons are
      canonical in Greek, which the corpus doesn't hold; a made-up record lets a Latin version stand in:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <listBibl>
        <bibl type="work" xml:id="C.NIC-0325">
          <title>Council of Nicaea, 325</title>
          <idno>C.NIC-0325</idno>
          <idno type="category">C</idno>
          <date when="0325">325</date>
          <ptr type="canonical" target="#new_version_nicaea_greek"/>
          <ptr type="stand-in" target="#new_version_nicaea_dionysius"/>
        </bibl>
        <bibl type="version" xml:id="new_version_nicaea_greek">
          <title>Council of Nicaea, 325, the Greek canons</title>
        </bibl>
        <bibl type="version" xml:id="new_version_nicaea_dionysius">
          <title>Council of Nicaea, 325, in Dionysius Exiguus's translation</title>
        </bibl>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <p>A work names at most one canonical version and one stand-in, and a pointer of another type is an error:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="version-choices">
      <listBibl>
        <bibl type="work" xml:id="new_work_exemplum_bis">
          <title>Exemplum</title>
          <ptr type="canonical" target="#new_version_exemplum_latin"/>
          <ptr type="canonical" target="#new_version_exemplum_greek"/><!-- breaks the rule -->
        </bibl>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="pointer-type">
      <listBibl>
        <bibl type="work" xml:id="new_work_exemplum">
          <title>Exemplum</title>
          <ptr type="best" target="#new_version_exemplum"/><!-- breaks the rule -->
        </bibl>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <p>The import makes a provisional work or version of a placeholder, and reads its canonical and stand-in
      choices; an export writes each work and version it points to, with them.</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="records-works-specs">
      <!-- A work's choices of versions: its canonical version and the version that stands in for it; and, in a
           relation, what a text came through. -->
      <elementSpec ident="ptr" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="version-choices" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A work names at most one canonical version and one stand-in.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:bibl[tei:ptr[@type = ('canonical', 'stand-in')]]">
              <sch:assert id="version-choices" test="count(tei:ptr[@type = 'canonical']) le 1 and count(tei:ptr[@type                 = 'stand-in']) le 1">A work names at most one canonical version (ptr type="canonical") and one
                version that stands in for it (ptr type="stand-in").</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="pointer-type" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="canonical">
                <desc>in a work's record: its canonical version</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="stand-in">
                <desc>in a work's record: the version whose text stands in for the canonical version's</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="via">
                <desc>in a relation's description: the work, version or transcription a text came through</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-witnesses">
    <head>Witnesses</head>
    <p>A witness is a copy of a text: in a manuscript, at its folios, or in a publication, at its pages (<ptr target="#levels"/>). A file names the witnesses it needs in the source description's <gi>listWit</gi>, each a
      <gi>witness</gi> with an <att>xml:id</att>, holding a pointer to its manuscript or publication and its folios
      or pages: an edition carrying the page breaks of several manuscripts points to each from its page breaks by
      <att>edRef</att> (<ptr target="#transcription-page-breaks"/>), and a reconstruction lists the witnesses its editor
      reconstructed it from (<ptr target="#editions-and-literature"/>). A made-up list:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <listWit>
        <witness xml:id="wit-M"><bibl><ptr target="#new_manuscript_munich_4592"/></bibl>, <locus from="158r" to="207v">ff. 158r–207v</locus></witness>
        <witness xml:id="wit-E"><bibl><ptr target="#new_bibl_maassen_1870"/><citedRange unit="page" from="852" to="853">852–853</citedRange></bibl></witness>
      </listWit>
    </egXML>
    <p>The import joins each witness to its manuscript or publication, at its folios or pages.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-work-groups">
    <head>Work groups</head>
    <p>A work group is a scholarly construct over several works, "the Pseudo-Isidorian forgeries": a
      <gi>listBibl</gi> typed <val>workGroup</val>, with its name in <gi>head</gi>, what it is in <gi>desc</gi>, and
      its members, each a <gi>bibl</gi> holding a <gi>ptr</gi> to the work's record (TEI lets a list of
      bibliography hold only bibliographic items). Every heading is typed (<ptr target="#text-headings"/>); a group's
      name is typed <val>rubric</val>, which says nothing more there. A made-up group:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <listBibl type="workGroup" xml:id="new_group_pseudo_isidore">
        <head type="rubric">The Pseudo-Isidorian forgeries</head>
        <desc>The forgeries made in the circle of the False Decretals, in the middle of the 9th century.</desc>
        <bibl><ptr target="#new_work_false_decretals"/></bibl>
        <bibl><ptr target="#new_work_benedictus_levita"/></bibl>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <p>A group is stored with its name, description, author and members; it is never a level of a text.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-rare">
    <head>Rare kinds and extra fields</head>
    <p>A record of a kind the guide has no section for, a diocese or a religious order, takes the nearest form,
      with its kind in <att>type</att>: a diocese is a <gi>place</gi>, an order an <gi>org</gi>. A name of it in the
      text is an <gi>rs</gi>, its kind in <att>type</att> and its record in <att>ref</att> (<ptr target="#marked-other"/>). The kinds and their fields are defined in the application, as in Heurist; a field
      the form has no element for is an <gi>f</gi>, named as the field, in an <gi>fs</gi> typed by the kind, in a
      <gi>note</gi> typed <val>extra</val>: TEI lets a place, an institution or an event hold an <gi>fs</gi> only in
      a note, and every kind does the same. A made-up diocese:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <place xml:id="new_place_diocese_exemplum" type="diocese">
        <placeName>Dioecesis Exemplensis</placeName>
        <note type="extra">
          <fs type="diocese">
            <f name="metropolitan">Provincia Exemplensis</f>
          </fs>
        </note>
      </place>
    </egXML>
    <p>Only a kind that no form fits needs a new section of the guide.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-external-ids">
    <head>Ids elsewhere</head>
    <p>A record's ids in other projects' catalogues are <gi>idno</gi>s, each typed by its authority, from a closed
      list: <val>wikidata</val>, <val>viaf</val>, <val>gnd</val>, <val>geonames</val>, <val>tgn</val>,
      <val>pleiades</val>, <val>mirabile</val>, <val>clavis</val>, <val>biblissima</val>, <val>schoenberg</val>,
      <val>hmml</val>, <val>bodleian</val>, <val>manusOnline</val>, and the Bodleian's <val>ieArk</val>,
      <val>crArk</val> and <val>SCN</val> (<ptr target="#header-external-ids"/>). The import keeps them beside the
      record's own id, and matches by them. Justinian, as Mirabile names him, with his id there:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <person xml:id="new_person_iustinianus">
        <persName type="main">Iustinianus imperator</persName>
        <persName type="alternative">Flavius Petrus Sabbatius Iustinianus</persName>
        <persName type="alternative">Justinian I</persName>
        <idno type="mirabile">19360</idno>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <p>An authority may number each kind of record on its own. Mirabile does: 20174 is a work in its index of titles,
      and another record in its index of authors. The kind of record says which index its number is from, and the
      import makes the link from it, the index's address followed by a name and the number, as the corpus's links
      have them; an export writes the number:</p>
    <table xml:id="mirabile-indices">
      <head>Mirabile's indices, by the kind of record</head>
      <row role="label">
        <cell>Record</cell>
        <cell>Index</cell>
        <cell>Link</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>a person</cell>
        <cell>authors</cell>
        <cell><code>https://www.mirabileweb.it/author/-author/19840</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>a work or a version</cell>
        <cell>titles</cell>
        <cell><code>https://www.mirabileweb.it/title/-/20174</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>a manuscript</cell>
        <cell>manuscripts</cell>
        <cell><code>https://www.mirabileweb.it/manuscript/-manuscript/220110</code></cell>
      </row>
    </table>
    <p>Mirabile's other indices, such as its institutions', join the table as the application reads them. Bernardus
      of Pavia and his Summa decretalium, each with the number of its own index:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <person xml:id="new_person_bernardus_papiensis">
        <persName type="main">Bernardus Papiensis praepositus</persName>
        <idno type="mirabile">19840</idno>
      </person>
      <listBibl>
        <bibl type="work" xml:id="new_work_summa_decretalium">
          <title>Summa decretalium</title>
          <author ref="#new_person_bernardus_papiensis">Bernardus Papiensis</author>
          <idno type="mirabile">20174</idno>
        </bibl>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <p>Links to pages that aren't an authority's record, a library's page on a person or a scholar's on a work, go
      in a note typed <val>links</val>, holding a list of links, each with its target and words:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <person xml:id="new_person_exemplus_linked">
        <persName type="main">Exemplus</persName>
        <note type="links">
          <list type="links">
            <item><ref target="https://example.org/exemplus">A page on Exemplus</ref></item>
          </list>
        </note>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <p>An authority's record linked there is reported, since its id goes in an <gi>idno</gi>; the import reads it as
      the id, as it reads the earlier files' notes typed <val>authorities</val>, which hold an authority's numbers. A
      note of links holds only its list, and each link its target and words:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="link-authority">
      <person xml:id="new_person_Sextus">
        <persName type="main">Sextus</persName>
        <note type="links">
          <list type="links">
            <item><ref target="https://www.mirabileweb.it/author/-author/19840">Mirabile</ref></item><!-- breaks the rule -->
          </list>
        </note>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="links-list">
      <person xml:id="new_person_Tertius">
        <persName type="main">Tertius</persName>
        <note type="links">See the catalogue.</note>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="link-target">
      <person xml:id="new_person_Quartus">
        <persName type="main">Quartus</persName>
        <note type="links">
          <list type="links">
            <item>
              <ref target="https://example.org/person/7"/>
            </item>
          </list>
        </note>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="records-external-ids-specs">
      <!-- A note of links holds a list, each link a target and words; an authority's record is an idno, not a
           link. -->
      <elementSpec ident="list" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="links-list" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A note of links holds a list of links.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:standOff//tei:note[@type = 'links']">
              <sch:assert id="links-list" test="tei:list[@type = 'links'] and not(text()[normalize-space()])">A note
                of links holds a list of links, and no words of its own.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="link-target" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A link has a target and words.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:standOff//tei:list[@type = 'links']/tei:item">
              <sch:assert id="link-target" test="tei:ref[@target][normalize-space(.) != '']">A link has a @target and
                words.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="link-authority" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An authority's record is an id, not a link.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:note[@type = 'links']//tei:ref[@target]">
              <!-- The records of the authorities in idno/@type's list, by their addresses. -->
              <sch:let name="authorities" value="'(mirabileweb\.it/(author|title|manuscript)/|wikidata\.org/(wiki|entity)/Q|viaf\.org/viaf/|d-nb\.info/gnd/|geonames\.org/[0-9]|vocab\.getty\.edu/(page/)?tgn/|pleiades\.stoa\.org/places/|sdbm\.library\.upenn\.edu/|manus\.iccu\.sbn\.it/|medieval\.bodleian\.ox\.ac\.uk/catalog/)'"/>
              <sch:assert id="link-authority" role="warning" test="not(matches(@target, $authorities))">An
                authority's record is an id: an idno typed by the authority, not a link in a note of links
                (<sch:value-of select="@target"/>).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="records-sources">
    <head>Sources</head>
    <p>Every record, text and annotation names where it comes from. A record's source is a <gi>bibl</gi> typed
      <val>source</val> in it: the source's name, the record's id there (an <gi>idno</gi> typed by the authority),
      a link to it (<gi>ref</gi>), the date it was taken (<gi>date</gi> typed <val>retrieved</val>), and the
      licence it was taken under (<gi>licence</gi>, in <gi>availability</gi>). The import keeps it as the record's
      provenance. The Institutiones again, with its source:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <listBibl>
        <bibl type="work" xml:id="new_work_institutiones_sourced">
          <title>Institutiones</title>
          <bibl type="source">
            <title>Mirabile</title>
            <idno type="mirabile">2106</idno>
            <ref target="http://www.mirabileweb.it/title/-/2106">mirabileweb.it</ref>
            <date type="retrieved" when="2025-01-10">10 January 2025</date>
          </bibl>
        </bibl>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <p>The date retrieved is made up, since the file doesn't give it; Mirabile states no licence for its records,
      and so none is given. A made-up place taken from Wikidata, whose data is under CC0:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <place xml:id="new_place_exemplum_sourced">
        <placeName>Exemplum</placeName>
        <bibl type="source">
          <title>Wikidata</title>
          <date type="retrieved" when="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</date>
          <availability>
            <licence target="https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/">CC0 1.0</licence>
          </availability>
        </bibl>
      </place>
    </egXML>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="relations" n="VI">
  <head>Relations</head>
  <p>A relation is a typed statement from one thing to another: a chapter is a text of a council's canon, a
    manuscript was owned by an abbey, a hand was a scribe's, a bishop held office in a church. It joins records,
    nodes of texts, or other relations, and may carry a date, a certainty, a role and a place, its evidence, its
    author, and, for a judgement, its method. Facts that belong to one record alone are its fields (<ptr target="#records"/>); what joins two things, may be uncertain or disputed, or needs evidence, is a
    relation.</p>
  <p>A file writes its relations in the standOff's <gi>listRelation</gi>, one <gi>relation</gi> each. The import reads
    them as the encoder's statements, beside the observations it makes itself from the source notes (<ptr target="#marked-notes"/>) and the relations it makes from a description's history (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>). Relations are <hi>full</hi>.</p>

  <div xml:id="relations-form">
    <head>The form of a relation</head>
    <list>
      <item><hi>its type</hi>: <att>name</att>, the type's name (<val>isTextOf</val>), and, in exports, <att>ref</att>,
        the type's permalink;</item>
      <item><hi>its ends</hi>: <att>active</att>, what it is from, and <att>passive</att>, what it is to; for a
        symmetric type, <att>mutual</att>, both ends, once. An end is an id in the file (<code>#hand-A</code>), a
        placeholder, a permalink, or the code of a document or a canon, as <att>source</att> names them (<ptr target="#marked-pointers"/>);</item>
      <item><hi>observation or judgement</hi>: <att>type</att>, <val>observation</val> for what a witness shows,
        <val>judgement</val> for what holds of a version or a work, naming its evidence;</item>
      <item><hi>its certainty</hi>: <att>cert</att>, <val>high</val>, <val>medium</val> or <val>low</val>, for
        certain, probable and possible; none means certain. A disagreement is two relations, not a fourth
        value;</item>
      <item><hi>its author</hi>: <att>resp</att>, pointing to the person who states it;</item>
      <item><hi>its date</hi>, where the type has one (an owner's years, an office's), in the relation's own
        attributes, in the one form (<ptr target="#dates"/>);</item>
      <item><hi>its evidence</hi>: the passages it rests on, by pointers in <att>source</att>, and the publications,
        with their pages, in its description.</item>
    </list>
    <p>TEI 4.12 lets a relation hold one <gi>desc</gi>, and nothing else. What the type needs beyond its ends goes
      there, each in its element:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a role (bishop, abbot, owner), by <gi>roleName</gi>;</item>
      <item>a place (an office held in a diocese), by <gi>placeName</gi> with <att>ref</att>;</item>
      <item>a judgement's method ("in all witnesses", "in the canonical witness", "the editor's text", "the
        register"), by <gi>term</gi> typed <val>method</val>;</item>
      <item>what a text came through, for <val>derivedFrom</val>, by <gi>ptr</gi> typed <val>via</val>, pointing to the
        work, version or transcription;</item>
      <item>a publication as evidence, by a <gi>bibl</gi> holding a <gi>ptr</gi> to it and its pages in
        <gi>citedRange</gi>.</item>
    </list>
    <p>A made-up judgement: a collection's chapter derives, probably, from a council's canon through another
      collection, in all the witnesses its encoder checked, after an edition's note:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <listRelation>
        <relation name="derivedFrom" type="judgement" cert="medium" resp="#new_person_encoder" active="COLL.Dach_1_12" passive="C.NIC-0325_3">
          <desc><term type="method">in all witnesses</term>, via <ptr type="via" target="#new_work_hispana"/>,
            after <bibl><ptr target="#new_bibl_edition"/><citedRange unit="page" from="12" to="13">12–13</citedRange></bibl></desc>
        </relation>
      </listRelation>
    </egXML>
    <p>An office, with its role and its years, and a symmetric relation between two hands, both made up:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <listRelation>
        <relation name="holdsOfficeIn" type="observation" active="#new_person_exemplus" passive="#new_org_ecclesia_exemplensis" from="0801" to="0814">
          <desc><roleName>episcopus</roleName></desc>
        </relation>
        <relation name="sameHandAs" type="judgement" cert="low" mutual="#hand-A #hand-C"/>
      </listRelation>
    </egXML>
    <p>A relation has a name, its type is one of the two, and its certainty one of the three:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="relation-without-name">
      <relation type="observation" active="#ch1" passive="C.NIC-0325_3"/><!-- breaks the rule -->
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="relation-type-unknown">
      <relation name="isTextOf" type="opinion" active="#ch1" passive="C.NIC-0325_3"/><!-- breaks the rule -->
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="relation-certainty">
      <relation name="isTextOf" type="observation" cert="unknown" active="#ch1" passive="C.NIC-0325_3"/><!-- breaks the rule -->
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="relations-specs">
      <!-- Relations are observations or judgements, certain, probable or possible; what they need beyond their ends
           stands in their one desc. -->
      <elementSpec ident="relation" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="name" mode="change" usage="req" xml:id="relation-without-name" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="semi" mode="add">
              <valItem ident="isTextOf"><desc>a unit is a text of another (fixed)</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="derivedFrom"><desc>a unit derives from another, through a work, version or
                  transcription, the via (fixed)</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="versionOf"><desc>a version is a version of a work (fixed)</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="witnessOf"><desc>a witness is a witness of a version or a work (fixed)</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="transcriptionOf"><desc>a transcription is a transcription of a witness
                  (fixed)</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="in"><desc>a witness is in a manuscript or a publication, at folios or pages
                  (fixed)</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="isVerbatimOf"><desc>a unit is a text of another, word for word</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="isVariantOf"><desc>a unit is a text of another, with variants</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="isShorterVersionOf"><desc>a unit is a text of another, shortened</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="translationOf"><desc>a version translates another</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="revisionOf"><desc>a version revises another</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="abridgementOf"><desc>a version abridges another</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="augmentationOf"><desc>a version augments another</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="adaptationOf"><desc>a work adapts another</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="basedOn"><desc>a work is based on another</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="origin"><desc>a manuscript or unit was made at a place or institution</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="ownedBy"><desc>a manuscript or unit was owned by a person or institution, with
                  dates</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="scribe"><desc>a hand was a person's</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="artist"><desc>a decoration was a person's or a workshop's</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="author"><desc>a work's author was a person</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="enactedAt"><desc>a work was enacted at an event, a council's canons at the
                  council</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="tookPlaceAt"><desc>an event took place at a place</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="holdsOfficeIn"><desc>a person held office in an institution, with a role and
                  dates</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="lists"><desc>a register item lists a chapter</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="sameScribeAs"><desc>two hands are the same scribe's (symmetric)</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="sameHandAs"><desc>two hands are the same (symmetric)</desc></valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="relation-type-unknown" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="observation">
                <desc>what a witness shows</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="judgement">
                <desc>what holds of a version or a work, with its method</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="cert" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="relation-certainty" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="high">
                <desc>certain</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="medium">
                <desc>probable</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="low">
                <desc>possible</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="relations-types">
    <head>The types</head>
    <p>Each type says what it joins, and has a reverse: "a version of" reads back as "has as a version". The six types
      of the texts' levels and units are fixed; the others are a vocabulary of the application, which grows as the
      project needs, each type mapped later to SAWS or another ontology. The guide holds the types to start with;
      a name outside them is reported, so that a slip is seen and a new type is added to the vocabulary on
      purpose.</p>
    <table xml:id="relation-types-table">
      <head>The relation types to start with</head>
      <row role="label">
        <cell>Name</cell>
        <cell>From, to</cell>
        <cell>Reverse</cell>
      </row>
      <row><cell><val>isTextOf</val> (fixed)</cell><cell>unit, unit: a node or a canon</cell><cell>has as a text</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>derivedFrom</val> (fixed)</cell><cell>unit, unit; via a work, version or
          transcription</cell><cell>used in</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>versionOf</val> (fixed)</cell><cell>version, work</cell><cell>has as a version</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>witnessOf</val> (fixed)</cell><cell>witness, version or work</cell><cell>has as a
          witness</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>transcriptionOf</val> (fixed)</cell><cell>transcription, witness</cell><cell>has as a
          transcription</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>in</val> (fixed)</cell><cell>witness, manuscript or publication; at folios or
          pages</cell><cell>holds</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>isVerbatimOf</val>, <val>isVariantOf</val>, <val>isShorterVersionOf</val></cell><cell>unit,
          unit: narrower terms of <val>isTextOf</val>, after SAWS</cell><cell>as SAWS has them</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>translationOf</val>, <val>revisionOf</val>, <val>abridgementOf</val>,
          <val>augmentationOf</val></cell><cell>version, version</cell><cell>translated as, revised as, abridged as,
          augmented as</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>adaptationOf</val>, <val>basedOn</val></cell><cell>work, work</cell><cell>adapted as, basis
          of</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>origin</val></cell><cell>manuscript or unit, place or institution</cell><cell>origin
          of</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>ownedBy</val></cell><cell>manuscript or unit, person or institution; with
          dates</cell><cell>owner of</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>scribe</val></cell><cell>hand, person</cell><cell>scribe of</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>artist</val></cell><cell>decoration, person or workshop</cell><cell>artist of</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>author</val></cell><cell>work, person</cell><cell>author of</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>enactedAt</val></cell><cell>work, event: a council's canons, the council</cell><cell>enacted</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>tookPlaceAt</val></cell><cell>event, place</cell><cell>place of</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>holdsOfficeIn</val></cell><cell>person, institution; with a role and dates</cell><cell>office held
          by</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>lists</val></cell><cell>register item, chapter</cell><cell>listed in</cell></row>
      <row><cell><val>sameScribeAs</val>, <val>sameHandAs</val></cell><cell>hand, hand</cell><cell>symmetric: by
          <att>mutual</att></cell></row>
    </table>
    <p>A family of texts is never encoded: the application works it out from the relations.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="relation-term">
      <relation name="isQuotedIn" type="observation" active="#ch1" passive="C.NIC-0325_3"/>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="relations-types-specs">
      <!-- The relation types to start with, which the Schematron warns outside of; the same list as the name's
           values, which a test keeps equal. -->
      <elementSpec ident="listRelation" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="relation-term" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A relation's type is one of the types to start with, or a new term of the vocabulary.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:relation[@name]">
              <sch:let name="terms" value="('isTextOf', 'derivedFrom', 'versionOf', 'witnessOf', 'transcriptionOf',                 'in', 'isVerbatimOf', 'isVariantOf', 'isShorterVersionOf', 'translationOf', 'revisionOf',                 'abridgementOf', 'augmentationOf', 'adaptationOf', 'basedOn', 'origin', 'ownedBy', 'scribe',                 'artist', 'author', 'enactedAt', 'tookPlaceAt', 'holdsOfficeIn', 'lists', 'sameScribeAs',                 'sameHandAs')"/>
              <sch:assert id="relation-term" role="warning" test="@name = $terms">The relation type <sch:value-of select="@name"/> isn't among the guide's types: check its spelling, or add it to the application's
                vocabulary.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="relations-levels">
    <head>The levels' relations</head>
    <p>The four relations between the levels, <val>versionOf</val>, <val>witnessOf</val>,
      <val>transcriptionOf</val> and <val>in</val>, are the ones an imported file needs least: the import makes them
      from the header. A file's text is a transcription of its witness; the witness is in the manuscript or the
      publication its source description gives (<ptr target="#records-witnesses"/>), at its folios or pages, and is a
      witness of the work its code names. An encoder writes them only to say more: that a version listed in the file
      is a version of a work, or that a witness is of a version and not only of its work. An export writes the
      levels' ids in the formal title (<ptr target="#header-title"/>), and their relations in its
      standOff.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <listRelation>
        <relation name="versionOf" type="judgement" active="#new_version_nicaea_dionysius_ii" passive="C.NIC-0325"/>
      </listRelation>
    </egXML>
    <p><val>isTextOf</val> joins a node of a witness to its version's canon: it is how a witness's own structure is
      mapped to the canons (<ptr target="#levels"/>). The import makes it as an observation from each source note
      that names a canon; an encoder writes it where no note does.</p>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="dates" n="VII">
  <head>Dates</head>
  <p>Everything dated has one kind of date: a council and a person's life (<ptr target="#records"/>), a manuscript's
    unit, its hands and its binding (<ptr target="#manuscripts"/>), a relation, such as an owner's from 1460 to 1520
    (<ptr target="#relations"/>), and a date the text names (<ptr target="#marked-dates"/>). A date keeps the words
    its source gives ("sec. IX ultimo terzo", "ERA DLXXXVII"), and what they mean: a point or a span, its bounds, its
    precision (a century, a year, a month, a day), whether it's circa or uncertain, and the calendar or era of its
    words. The application searches every date by its bounds, shows it in its words, and exchanges it in EDTF, the
    Extended Date/Time Format of ISO 8601-2.</p>
  <p>Nothing in this part is required. <gi>date</gi> is <hi>common</hi>, since the editions' imprints carry one; the
    rest is <hi>full</hi>.</p>

  <div xml:id="dates-form">
    <head>The one form</head>
    <p>A date is written once, in one form, whatever element carries it:</p>
    <list>
      <item><hi>its words</hi>, as the element's text, where the element has text (<gi>date</gi>, <gi>origDate</gi>,
        <gi>birth</gi>, <gi>floruit</gi>); a record's date may have none;</item>
      <item><hi>its bounds</hi>, always where they can be given, in TEI's attributes, as years of four digits
        (<code>0813</code>), months (<code>0816-08</code>) or days (<code>0813-06-13</code>):
        <list>
          <item>a point known to its precision: <att>when</att>;</item>
          <item>a point somewhere between two dates: <att>notBefore</att> and <att>notAfter</att>;</item>
          <item>a span, such as a bishop's years or an owner's: <att>from</att> and <att>to</att>;</item>
        </list>
      </item>
      <item><hi>its EDTF</hi>, where the bounds can't say what the words do: in <att>when-custom</att> for a point,
        in <att>from-custom</att> and <att>to-custom</att> for a span's ends, with <att>datingMethod</att> pointing
        to the file's declaration of EDTF, <code>#edtf</code> (<ptr target="#dates-calendars"/>). EDTF says:
        <list>
          <item>circa, by <code>~</code> (<code>0800~</code>), uncertain, by <code>?</code> (<code>0756?</code>), or
            both, by <code>%</code>;</item>
          <item>a century or a decade, by unspecified digits (<code>08XX</code>, <code>081X</code>);</item>
          <item>a point among a few dates, by a set (<code>[0755,0756]</code>).</item>
        </list>
      </item>
    </list>
    <p>The import makes one date of them: the words, the bounds, the precision (from the EDTF, or from the bounds'
      form), circa and certainty (from the EDTF's marks), and the calendar. An export writes the same form. The
      application's date form proposes the bounds and the EDTF from the words; an encoder may write them by hand.
      TEI's other forms of the same date, its ISO attributes (<att>when-iso</att>, …), a named period
      (<att>period</att>), and the custom bounds (<att>notBefore-custom</att>, <att>notAfter-custom</att>), aren't
      taken: one form.</p>
    <p>A century is the commonest date of a manuscript. The bounds follow the words: "sec. X" is 901 to 1000. Its
      EDTF is <code>09XX</code>, which EDTF reads as 900 to 999; the checks allow the year's difference (<ptr target="#dates-checks"/>):</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.His.ms-Vat-BAV-Pal575">
      <origDate notBefore="0901" notAfter="1000" when-custom="09XX" datingMethod="#edtf" calendar="#gregorian">sec.
        X</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <p>A part of a century needs no EDTF: the bounds say it, and the words keep how it was said.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.Dach.ms-Brn-Burg-425">
      <origDate notBefore="0867" notAfter="0900" calendar="#gregorian">sec. IX ultimo terzo</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <p>A circa date gives in its bounds how far the encoder, or the source, reads the circa; its EDTF keeps the year
      named:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.Laur.ms-Vat-BAV-Pal574">
      <origDate notBefore="0776" notAfter="0825" when-custom="0800~" datingMethod="#edtf" calendar="#gregorian">sec.
        VIII-IX (ca. 800)</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <p>An uncertain year, and the years it may be instead, is a point between bounds with its EDTF marked uncertain.
      The edition of the Council of Aschheim dates it "a. 756 vel 755-60"; as a record of the council:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <event xml:id="new_event_aschheim" notBefore="0755" notAfter="0760" when-custom="0756?" datingMethod="#edtf">
        <eventName>Concilium Ascheimense</eventName>
      </event>
    </egXML>
    <p>A span has its two ends. In the records of the Council of Carthage of 397, Aurelius of Carthage flourishes
      from 388 to 423:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.CAR-0397">
      <person xml:id="new_person_aurelius_carthage">
        <persName type="main">Aurelius, archbishop of Carthage</persName>
        <floruit from="0388" to="0423">from 0388 to 0423</floruit>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <p>Where a span's ends are uncertain, each end has its EDTF, and the bounds hold the earliest start and the
      latest end:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <person xml:id="new_person_exemplus_episcopus">
        <persName type="main">Exemplus, bishop</persName>
        <floruit from="0745" to="0760" from-custom="0750~" to-custom="0760" datingMethod="#edtf">c. 750 to
          760</floruit>
      </person>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="dates-form-specs">
      <!-- One form of date: TEI's W3C bounds and EDTF in the custom attributes; TEI's other forms are left out. -->
      <classSpec ident="att.datable.iso" type="atts" module="namesdates" mode="change">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="when-iso" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="notBefore-iso" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="notAfter-iso" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="from-iso" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="to-iso" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </classSpec>
      <classSpec ident="att.datable.custom" type="atts" module="namesdates" mode="change">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="notBefore-custom" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="notAfter-custom" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="datingPoint" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </classSpec>
      <classSpec ident="att.datable" type="atts" module="tei" mode="change">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="period" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </classSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="dates-calendars">
    <head>Calendars and eras</head>
    <p>The bounds are always in the Christian era, in the Gregorian calendar, as TEI defines them; EDTF is too. The
      words may reckon otherwise: by the Spanish era, 38 years ahead of the Christian, by the Julian calendar's days,
      by a king's years. <att>calendar</att> names the reckoning of the words, pointing to a <gi>calendar</gi> that the
      file declares once, in the profile description's <gi>calendarDesc</gi>; the bounds give what the words come to.
      Each declaration says what the calendar is, and may link to a description of it in <att>target</att>.</p>
    <p>A manuscript's date (<gi>origDate</gi>) and a date in the text always name the calendar of their words, as the
      Bodleian's catalogues do for a manuscript's: <code>#gregorian</code> for a modern statement, such as a
      catalogue's "sec. IX" or "a. 1009", and the source's own otherwise, such as <code>#julian</code> for a day it
      gives in the Julian calendar, or an era. These are the dates whose calendar matters. A unit said undated names
      none. The dates of publications, of records and of the header are modern years in the Christian era, and name
      none either. The declarations the guide uses:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <calendarDesc>
        <calendar xml:id="gregorian">
          <p>The Gregorian calendar, in the Christian era: the calendar of modern statements of date.</p>
        </calendar>
        <calendar xml:id="edtf" target="https://www.loc.gov/standards/datetime/">
          <p>The Extended Date/Time Format (EDTF), ISO 8601-2: the form of the dates' custom attributes.</p>
        </calendar>
        <calendar xml:id="julian">
          <p>The Julian calendar, in which the sources give their days.</p>
        </calendar>
        <calendar xml:id="spanish-era">
          <p>The Spanish era, which counts from 38 BC.</p>
        </calendar>
      </calendarDesc>
    </egXML>
    <p>A day reckoned in the Julian calendar says so, and its bounds give the Gregorian day: later by 2 days in the 6th
      century, 3 in the 7th, 4 in the 8th and 9th, 5 in the 10th, 6 in the 11th, 7 in the 12th and 13th, each
      difference starting on 1 March of the century's first year. A month or a year keeps its units: "mense Augusto
      822" is <code>0822-08</code>, since a few days at its edges make no difference at its precision. The
      application shows a date in its words, and can give the Julian day back from the calendar. The Council of
      Valencia, 549, dates itself by the era, the king's years and the Roman day, which is 4 December in the Julian
      calendar:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.VLA-0549">
      <calendarDesc>
        <calendar xml:id="julian-valencia">
          <p>The Julian calendar.</p>
        </calendar>
        <calendar xml:id="spanish-era-valencia">
          <p>The Spanish era.</p>
        </calendar>
      </calendarDesc>
      <head type="inscription">ITEM <eventName ref="#new_event_valencia">VALLETANUM CONCILIUM</eventName>, VI
        EPISCOPORUM HABITUM <date when="0549-12-06" calendar="#spanish-era-valencia #julian-valencia">ERA DLXXXVII
          ANNO XV <persName ref="#new_person_teudericus">TEUDERICI</persName> REGIS SUB DIE II NONAS
        DECEMBRES</date></head>
    </egXML>
    <p>A manuscript's unit dated to the day, in a catalogue that gives the Julian day:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.Ans.ms-Par-BnF-15392">
      <calendarDesc>
        <calendar xml:id="julian-ans">
          <p>The Julian calendar.</p>
        </calendar>
      </calendarDesc>
      <origDate when="1009-03-29" calendar="#julian-ans">a. 1009, 23 marzo</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <p>A manuscript's date or a date in the text without a calendar is an error, and so is a calendar the file
      doesn't declare:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="date-calendar">
      <origDate notBefore="0801" notAfter="0900">sec. IX</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="calendar-declared">
      <p>Habitum <date when="0549" calendar="#spanish-era">ERA DLXXXVII</date>.</p>
    </egXML>
    <p>The ids of the declarations are the file's own; the examples above give each its own, since the guide's ids
      are unique, except <code>gregorian</code> and <code>edtf</code>, which the guide's examples take as declared.
      A file usually needs <code>gregorian</code> and <code>edtf</code>, and <code>julian</code> where it dates to
      the day.</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="dates-calendars-specs">
      <!-- A manuscript's date and the text's dates name their calendar; a date's calendars are declared. -->
      <elementSpec ident="calendar" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="date-calendar" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A manuscript's date and a date in the text name the calendar of their words.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:origDate[not(@type = 'undated')] | tei:text//tei:date">
              <sch:assert id="date-calendar" test="@calendar">A manuscript's date and a date in the text name the
                calendar of their words by @calendar: #gregorian for a modern statement ("sec. IX"), the source's
                own otherwise (a Julian day, an era).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="calendar-declared" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A date's calendar and dating method point to a declared calendar.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@calendar or @datingMethod]">
              <sch:assert id="calendar-declared" test="every $p in tokenize(normalize-space(string-join((@calendar,                 @datingMethod), ' ')), ' ') satisfies (starts-with($p, '#') and                 //tei:calendarDesc/tei:calendar[@xml:id = substring($p, 2)])">@calendar and @datingMethod point to a
                calendar declared in the profile description's calendarDesc (<sch:value-of select="string-join((@calendar, @datingMethod), ' ')"/>).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="dates-checks">
    <head>What the checks require</head>
    <p>The RelaxNG checks each bound's form. The Schematron checks that a date reads one way:</p>
    <list>
      <item>a point between bounds gives both, and a span both its ends (warnings);</item>
      <item>no bound comes after its pair: <att>notBefore</att> after <att>notAfter</att>, or <att>from</att> after
        <att>to</att> (an error);</item>
      <item>an EDTF value has EDTF's form, as this part uses it, and its <att>datingMethod</att> (an error);</item>
      <item>the bounds agree with the EDTF, by years: an exact date has the bounds' years; one with unspecified
        digits may differ from them by a year at each end, as a century does; a circa or uncertain one lies within
        the bounds (a warning);</item>
      <item>a manuscript's date and a date in the text name their calendar (an error);</item>
      <item>every <att>calendar</att> and <att>datingMethod</att> points to a declared <gi>calendar</gi> (an
        error).</item>
    </list>
    <p>Folios and pages also have <att>from</att> and <att>to</att> (<gi>locus</gi>, <gi>biblScope</gi>,
      <gi>citedRange</gi>): these rules leave them alone.</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="date-span-bounds">
      <p>Datum <date notBefore="0755" calendar="#gregorian">post annum 755</date>.</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="date-from-to">
      <p>Episcopus <date from="0750" calendar="#gregorian">ab anno 750</date>.</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="date-bounds-order">
      <origDate notBefore="1000" notAfter="0901" calendar="#gregorian">sec. X</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="date-edtf-form">
      <origDate notBefore="0901" notAfter="1000" when-custom="sec. X" datingMethod="#edtf" calendar="#gregorian">sec.
        X</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="date-edtf-bounds">
      <origDate notBefore="0901" notAfter="1000" when-custom="08XX" datingMethod="#edtf" calendar="#gregorian">sec.
        X</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="dates-specs">
      <!-- A date reads one way: its bounds complete and in order, its EDTF well formed and agreeing with them. The
           rules apply to every dated element; folios and pages are left alone. -->
      <elementSpec ident="date" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <constraintSpec ident="date-span-bounds" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A point between bounds gives both.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@notBefore or @notAfter][not(self::tei:locus or self::tei:biblScope or               self::tei:citedRange)]">
              <sch:assert id="date-span-bounds" role="warning" test="@notBefore and @notAfter">A point between bounds
                gives both, @notBefore and @notAfter.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="date-from-to" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A span gives both its ends.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@from or @to][not(self::tei:locus or self::tei:biblScope or               self::tei:citedRange)]">
              <sch:assert id="date-from-to" role="warning" test="@from and @to">A span gives both its ends, @from and
                @to.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="date-bounds-order" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>No bound comes after its pair.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[(@notBefore and @notAfter) or (@from and @to)][not(self::tei:locus or               self::tei:biblScope or self::tei:citedRange)]">
              <!-- Each bound as a day, the earliest day of the first and the latest of the second, compared as
                   text: ISO dates of the same length sort as they follow. -->
              <sch:let name="first" value="for $b in (@notBefore, @from) return concat($b, substring('-01-01',                 string-length($b) - 3))"/>
              <sch:let name="last" value="for $b in (@notAfter, @to) return concat($b, substring('-12-31',                 string-length($b) - 3))"/>
              <sch:assert id="date-bounds-order" test="(not(@notBefore and @notAfter) or $first[1] le $last[1]) and                 (not(@from and @to) or $first[last()] le $last[last()])">No bound comes after its pair: @notBefore
                before @notAfter, @from before @to.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="date-edtf-form" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An EDTF value has EDTF's form, and its dating method.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@when-custom or @from-custom or @to-custom]">
              <sch:let name="day" value="'-?[0-9]{2}([0-9]{2}|[0-9]X|XX)(-([0-9]{2}|XX)(-([0-9]{2}|XX))?)?[~?%]?'"/>
              <sch:let name="item" value="concat('(', $day, ')(\.\.(', $day, '))?')"/>
              <sch:let name="edtf" value="concat('^((', $day, ')|\[(', $item, ')(,(', $item, '))*\])$')"/>
              <sch:assert id="date-edtf-form" test="@datingMethod = '#edtf' and (every $v in (@when-custom,                 @from-custom, @to-custom) satisfies matches(normalize-space($v), $edtf))">An EDTF value is a date
                (0813, 0816-08, 0813-06-13), its last digits unspecified (08XX) or itself marked circa or uncertain
                (~, ?, %), or a set of them ([0755,0756], [0867..0900]); and the element points to EDTF by
                @datingMethod="#edtf".</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="date-edtf-bounds" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>The bounds agree with the EDTF, by years.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@datingMethod = '#edtf'][@when-custom or @from-custom or @to-custom]">
              <!-- The EDTF's earliest and latest years, its unspecified digits read as 0 and 9; and the bounds'
                   years. Exact: equal; unspecified digits: a year apart at most; circa or uncertain: within. -->
              <sch:let name="w" value="normalize-space(@when-custom)"/>
              <sch:let name="wy" value="tokenize(replace($w, '[^0-9X]+', ' '), ' ')[string-length(.) = 4]"/>
              <sch:let name="wlo" value="min(for $y in $wy return xs:integer(translate($y, 'X', '0')))"/>
              <sch:let name="whi" value="max(for $y in $wy return xs:integer(translate($y, 'X', '9')))"/>
              <sch:let name="lo" value="for $b in (@notBefore, @when)[1] return substring($b, 1, 4)"/>
              <sch:let name="hi" value="for $b in (@notAfter, @when)[1] return substring($b, 1, 4)"/>
              <sch:let name="blo" value="if ($lo castable as xs:integer) then xs:integer($lo) else ()"/>
              <sch:let name="bhi" value="if ($hi castable as xs:integer) then xs:integer($hi) else ()"/>
              <sch:let name="point" value="empty($wlo) or empty($blo) or empty($bhi) or (if (matches($w, '[~?%]'))                 then $blo le $wlo and $whi le $bhi else if (contains($w, 'X')) then abs($blo - $wlo) le 1 and                 abs($bhi - $whi) le 1 else $blo eq $wlo and $bhi eq $whi)"/>
              <sch:let name="f" value="normalize-space(@from-custom)"/>
              <sch:let name="flo" value="min(for $y in tokenize(replace($f, '[^0-9X]+', ' '), ' ')[string-length(.) =                 4] return xs:integer(translate($y, 'X', '0')))"/>
              <sch:let name="fb" value="substring(@from, 1, 4)"/>
              <sch:let name="bf" value="if ($fb castable as xs:integer) then xs:integer($fb) else ()"/>
              <sch:let name="start" value="empty($flo) or empty($bf) or (if (matches($f, '[~?%]')) then $bf le $flo                 else if (contains($f, 'X')) then abs($bf - $flo) le 1 else $bf eq $flo)"/>
              <sch:let name="t" value="normalize-space(@to-custom)"/>
              <sch:let name="thi" value="max(for $y in tokenize(replace($t, '[^0-9X]+', ' '), ' ')[string-length(.) =                 4] return xs:integer(translate($y, 'X', '9')))"/>
              <sch:let name="tb" value="substring(@to, 1, 4)"/>
              <sch:let name="bt" value="if ($tb castable as xs:integer) then xs:integer($tb) else ()"/>
              <sch:let name="end" value="empty($thi) or empty($bt) or (if (matches($t, '[~?%]')) then $thi le $bt                 else if (contains($t, 'X')) then abs($bt - $thi) le 1 else $bt eq $thi)"/>
              <sch:assert id="date-edtf-bounds" role="warning" test="$point and $start and $end">The bounds agree with
                the EDTF, by years: an exact date has the bounds' years, a century or decade (08XX) differs by a year
                at most, a circa or uncertain date lies within them.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="manuscripts" n="VIII">
  <head>Manuscripts</head>
  <p>A manuscript is described once, in an <gi>msDesc</gi>: in the source description of a file that transcribes a
    text from it (<ptr target="#file-and-header"/>), or as a record in a <gi>listBibl</gi> (<ptr target="#records"/>).
    The description is TEI's own, restricted to what the application holds of a manuscript: the manuscript, its
    codicological units, each unit's contents and physical description, its hands and decoration, the binding, and
    the history of each. It is modelled on the Bodleian Libraries' guidelines for their catalogues, whose
    descriptions the project converts into its own (<ptr target="#manuscripts-bodleian"/>).</p>
  <p>The import makes the manuscript's record, its units, and an item for each text, joined to the work's record; it
    makes the relations a description states: where a unit was made, who owned the manuscript, which scribe a hand
    was. An export writes the same form. Everything in a description is <hi>full</hi>.</p>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-description">
    <head>The description and the manuscript's identifier</head>
    <p>A description holds the manuscript's identifier; its binding, in a physical description of its own; the
      history of the whole book, where its units were joined and who has owned it since; what is additional, its
      images, literature and sources; and its units, one at least, each with its contents, physical description and
      history. The Collectio 400 capitulorum's manuscript, Munich, Clm 4592, in short: the binding and the
      additional are left out, and of its three units, the one that holds the collection:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <msDesc>
        <msIdentifier>
          <settlement ref="#new_place_muenchen">München</settlement>
          <repository ref="#new_org_bsb">Bayerische Staatsbibliothek</repository>
          <idno type="shelfmark">Clm 4592</idno>
        </msIdentifier>
        <history>
          <provenance><orgName ref="#new_org_benediktbeuern">SS. Benedikt und Jakob</orgName>,
            Benediktbeuern</provenance>
        </history>
        <msPart n="3">
          <msIdentifier>
            <idno>3, ff. 120–207</idno>
          </msIdentifier>
          <msContents>
            <msItem n="1" corresp="COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
              <locus from="158r" to="207v">ff. 158r–207v</locus>
              <title ref="#new_work_collectio_cd">Collectio CD capitulorum</title>
            </msItem>
          </msContents>
          <physDesc>
            <objectDesc form="codex">
              <supportDesc material="perg">
                <support>membr.</support>
                <extent>ff. 120–207, of ff. II, 207, II' in all <dimensions type="leaf" unit="mm">
                    <height>240</height>
                    <width>160</width>
                  </dimensions></extent>
              </supportDesc>
            </objectDesc>
          </physDesc>
          <history>
            <origin>
              <origPlace ref="#new_place_salzburg">Salzburg</origPlace>
              <origDate notBefore="0826" notAfter="0850" calendar="#gregorian">sec. IX secondo quarto</origDate>
            </origin>
          </history>
        </msPart>
      </msDesc>
    </egXML>
    <p>The file gives the physical description of the whole book too, and repeats it in each unit; the leaves are
      wrapped in <gi>num</gi>, the places and the library have no records, the units are numbered 11, 22 and 33, and
      the items point to the works by <att>key</att>. The template <code>manuscript-collection.xml</code> holds the
      whole description. Here, as everywhere in this part, the example is revised to the guide's forms.</p>
    <p>The identifier names the manuscript's country, region and town, and its library, each pointing to its record
      by <att>ref</att>; the library's fonds, where it has one, in <gi>collection</gi>, the shelfmark still written
      whole in <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>shelfmark</val>; the manuscript's ids in other catalogues, each an
      <gi>idno</gi> typed by the authority (<ptr target="#records-external-ids"/>); its other identifiers, each an
      <gi>altIdentifier</gi> typed <val>former</val> (a former shelfmark), <val>siglum</val> (an editor's siglum) or
      <val>code</val> (the short code that files of the manuscript carry, <code>Mun-BSB-4592</code>); and its
      conventional names, in <gi>msName</gi>, one per language. A made-up manuscript:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <msIdentifier>
        <country ref="#new_place_exemplia">Exemplia</country>
        <region ref="#new_place_exemplia_borealis">Exemplia borealis</region>
        <settlement ref="#new_place_exemplum">Exemplum</settlement>
        <repository ref="#new_org_bibliotheca_exemplensis">Bibliotheca Exemplensis</repository>
        <collection ref="#new_org_codices_canonici">Codices canonici</collection>
        <idno type="shelfmark">Can. 12</idno>
        <idno type="mirabile">123456</idno>
        <altIdentifier type="former">
          <idno>Theol. 7</idno>
        </altIdentifier>
        <altIdentifier type="siglum">
          <idno>E</idno>
        </altIdentifier>
        <msName xml:lang="la">Codex Exemplensis</msName>
      </msIdentifier>
    </egXML>
    <p>A description holds only the elements this part names, each in its place: the contents and the physical
      description on a unit; on the manuscript, a physical description of the binding alone; no unit inside a unit;
      in <gi>adminInfo</gi>, the record's history alone; terms in a binding. Its prose, beside every value, holds a
      small set, so that what it mentions stays readable: <gi>locus</gi>; <gi>persName</gi>, <gi>placeName</gi> and
      <gi>orgName</gi> with <att>ref</att>; <gi>title</gi> with <att>ref</att>; <gi>date</gi>; <gi>foreign</gi>;
      <gi>hi</gi>, for italics and the like; and <gi>ref</gi>. A bibliography inside it keeps the forms of Part IX.
      Not the diplomatic set of the Bodleian's catalogues (<gi>am</gi>, <gi>ex</gi>, <gi>supplied</gi>,
      <gi>damage</gi>, <gi>fw</gi>, <gi>figure</gi>, <gi>formula</gi>, verse): quoting the manuscript is what
      transcriptions do. A count of leaves is words, without <gi>num</gi>. Each of these is an error; a description
      without a unit is one too:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="manuscript-unit">
      <msDesc>
        <msIdentifier>
          <settlement>Nowhere</settlement>
          <repository>National Library</repository>
          <idno type="shelfmark">MS 12</idno>
        </msIdentifier>
      </msDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="description-elements">
      <supportDesc material="perg">
        <support>Parchment.</support>
        <extent>ff. <num>207</num><!-- breaks the rule --></extent>
      </supportDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="alt-identifier-type">
      <msIdentifier>
        <settlement>Nowhere</settlement>
        <repository>National Library</repository>
        <idno type="shelfmark">MS 12</idno>
        <altIdentifier type="full_shelfmark"><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <idno>Nowhere, National Library, MS 12</idno>
        </altIdentifier>
      </msIdentifier>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-description-specs">
      <!-- A description has at least one unit, and holds only the profile's elements, in their places. -->
      <elementSpec ident="msDesc" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="manuscript-unit" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A manuscript's description has at least one codicological unit.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:msDesc">
              <sch:assert id="manuscript-unit" test="tei:msPart">A manuscript's description has at least one
                codicological unit (msPart), with its own identifier: the unit holds the physical description, the
                date, the hands and the decoration.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="description-elements" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>Inside a description, only the profile's elements, in their places, and the prose's phrase-level
            elements.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <!-- The literature and the names keep their own forms (Parts V and IX), so what they hold isn't looked
                 at here. -->
            <sch:rule context="tei:msDesc//*[not(ancestor::*[self::tei:bibl or self::tei:biblStruct or self::tei:listBibl               or self::tei:persName or self::tei:placeName or self::tei:orgName][ancestor::tei:msDesc])]">
              <sch:let name="profile" value="('msIdentifier', 'country', 'region', 'settlement', 'institution',                 'repository', 'collection', 'idno', 'altIdentifier', 'msName', 'msPart', 'msContents', 'summary',                 'textLang', 'msItem', 'locus', 'locusGrp', 'author', 'title', 'rubric', 'incipit', 'explicit',                 'finalRubric', 'colophon', 'note', 'bibl', 'biblStruct', 'listBibl', 'physDesc', 'p', 'objectDesc',                 'supportDesc', 'support', 'extent', 'dimensions', 'height', 'width', 'depth', 'foliation',                 'collation', 'condition', 'layoutDesc', 'layout', 'handDesc', 'handNote', 'decoDesc', 'decoNote',                 'additions', 'bindingDesc', 'binding', 'term', 'history', 'origin', 'origDate', 'origPlace',                 'provenance', 'acquisition', 'additional', 'adminInfo', 'recordHist', 'source', 'surrogates',                 'persName', 'placeName', 'orgName', 'date', 'foreign', 'hi', 'ref')"/>
              <!-- The places: contents on a unit; on the manuscript, a physical description of the binding alone,
                   and a binding only there; no unit inside a unit; the record's history alone in adminInfo; terms
                   in a binding. -->
              <sch:let name="placed" value="(not(self::tei:msContents) or parent::tei:msPart) and                 (not(parent::tei:physDesc/parent::tei:msDesc) or self::tei:bindingDesc) and (not(self::tei:bindingDesc)                 or ../parent::tei:msDesc) and (not(self::tei:msPart) or parent::tei:msDesc) and                 (not(parent::tei:adminInfo) or self::tei:recordHist) and (not(self::tei:term) or                 ancestor::tei:binding)"/>
              <sch:assert id="description-elements" test="local-name() = $profile and $placed">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt;
                isn't one of a manuscript description's elements here. A description holds the profile's elements in
                their places: the contents and the physical description on a unit, the binding alone on the
                manuscript, no unit inside a unit; its prose holds only locus, names, title, date, foreign, hi and
                ref.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>

      <!-- The manuscript's place, library and fonds point to their records. -->
      <elementSpec ident="country" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="region" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="settlement" module="namesdates" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="institution" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="repository" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="collection" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <!-- A manuscript's other identifiers: a former shelfmark, an editor's siglum, the project's short code. -->
      <elementSpec ident="altIdentifier" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="alt-identifier-type" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="former">
                <desc>a former shelfmark</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="siglum">
                <desc>an editor's siglum for the manuscript</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="code">
                <desc>the project's short code for the manuscript, as in a file's code (Mun-BSB-4592)</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-units">
    <head>Units</head>
    <p>A unit (<gi>msPart</gi>) is a codicological unit: leaves made together, which the manuscript may have joined to
      others. Every description has one at least: a manuscript made in one go has one. A unit's identifier is an
      <gi>idno</gi> without a type, with its number or letter and its leaves (<code>3, ff. 120–207</code>);
      <att>n</att> gives its order. A unit holds its own contents, physical description and history; the manuscript
      holds only what belongs to the whole book: its binding, its history since its units were joined, and what is
      additional. A unit isn't divided into units: a unit made of smaller ones that were once a book of their own is
      described by its parts, each a unit of the manuscript, with that history in each.</p>
    <p>Every unit is dated, in its origin's <gi>origDate</gi> (<ptr target="#manuscripts-history"/>), or said undated,
      by an <gi>origDate</gi> typed <val>undated</val>, holding the source's words if it has any, and no bounds. The
      import refuses neither, but reports a unit that says nothing:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <origDate type="undated">s. d.</origDate>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="unit-dated">
      <msPart>
        <msIdentifier>
          <idno>B</idno>
        </msIdentifier>
      </msPart>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-units-specs">
      <!-- Every unit is dated, or said undated. -->
      <elementSpec ident="msPart" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="unit-dated" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>Every unit is dated, or said undated.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:msPart">
              <sch:assert id="unit-dated" test="tei:history/tei:origin/tei:origDate[@when or @notBefore or @notAfter or                 @from or @to or @type = 'undated']">A unit is dated, in its origin's origDate with its bounds, or
                said undated (origDate type="undated").</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-contents">
    <head>Contents</head>
    <p>A unit's contents (<gi>msContents</gi>) are its items, each a text in it (<gi>msItem</gi>): its folios, by
      <gi>locus</gi> with <att>from</att> and <att>to</att>, or <gi>locusGrp</gi> for leaves apart; its
      <gi>author</gi> and its <gi>title</gi>, each pointing to its record by <att>ref</att>, the title to the work;
      the manuscript's own words, <gi>rubric</gi>, <gi>incipit</gi>, <gi>explicit</gi>, <gi>finalRubric</gi> and
      <gi>colophon</gi>; its languages, <gi>textLang</gi>; and notes. An item's parts are items inside it: a
      collection's register and prefaces, a text copied within another. Contents not listed one by one are said in
      <gi>summary</gi>. An item that a file transcribes names its transcription by <att>corresp</att>: the file's
      code, as source notes name documents (<ptr target="#marked-pointers"/>), or, in exports, the transcription's
      permalink; each file's description can point to every transcription of the manuscript. An item's category is
      its work's, not the item's. In Clm 4592, the Constitutum Silvestri is excerpted inside the collection:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <msContents>
        <msItem n="1" corresp="COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
          <locus from="158r" to="207v">ff. 158r–207v</locus>
          <title ref="#new_work_collectio_cd">Collectio CD capitulorum</title>
          <textLang mainLang="la">Latin</textLang>
          <msItem n="2">
            <locus from="194r" to="195r">ff. 194r–195r</locus>
            <author ref="#new_person_symmachus_pseudo">Symmachus papa pseudo</author>
            <title ref="#new_work_constitutum_silvestri">Constitutum Silvestri</title>
            <note>Excerpts, <foreign xml:lang="la">in collectionem CD capitulorum</foreign>.</note>
          </msItem>
        </msItem>
      </msContents>
    </egXML>
    <p>The file lists the excerpts as two more items of the unit, one with the author and one without, with escaped
      italics in their notes. An item without its folios is reported, since sources often give none:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="item-locus">
      <msContents>
        <msItem n="1">
          <title>Collectio CD capitulorum</title>
        </msItem>
      </msContents>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-contents-specs">
      <!-- An item has its folios; its category is its work's. -->
      <elementSpec ident="msItem" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="item-locus" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An item has its folios.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:msItem">
              <sch:assert id="item-locus" role="warning" test="tei:locus or tei:locusGrp">An item gives its folios,
                by locus, or locusGrp for leaves apart, where its source gives them.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="class" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-physical">
    <head>The physical description</head>
    <p>A unit's physical description (<gi>physDesc</gi>) may begin with paragraphs, then holds its parts, each in
      prose with its values in attributes: the object (<gi>objectDesc</gi>), its form in <att>form</att>
      (<val>codex</val>, <val>roll</val>, …); its support (<gi>supportDesc</gi>), the material in
      <att>material</att> (<val>perg</val>, <val>chart</val>, …), described in <gi>support</gi>, with the
      <gi>extent</gi> in words and the leaves' <gi>dimensions</gi>, their unit always given, <gi>foliation</gi>,
      <gi>collation</gi> and <gi>condition</gi>; its layout (<gi>layoutDesc</gi>), each <gi>layout</gi> with its
      <att>columns</att>, <att>ruledLines</att> and <att>writtenLines</att>; then its hands, decoration and
      additions. A unit's extent may say what the whole book counts. Forms and materials come from vocabularies that
      grow: a term outside the guide's seeds is reported, to be checked or added to the application's vocabulary
      (<ptr target="#appendix-tables"/> lists the seeds). Clm 4592's second unit:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <physDesc>
        <objectDesc form="codex">
          <supportDesc material="perg">
            <support>membr.</support>
            <extent>ff. 76–117 (78–119) <dimensions type="leaf" unit="mm">
                <height>240</height>
                <width>160</width>
              </dimensions></extent>
          </supportDesc>
          <layoutDesc>
            <layout columns="2">2 colonne</layout>
          </layoutDesc>
        </objectDesc>
      </physDesc>
    </egXML>
    <p>The file gives the material only in words; the vocabulary's <val>perg</val> says it for the application.
      Dimensions without their unit are an error, and a form or a material outside the seeds is reported:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="dimensions-unit">
      <supportDesc material="perg">
        <support>Parchment.</support>
        <extent>ff. 207 <dimensions type="leaf"><!-- breaks the rule -->
            <height>250</height>
            <width>170</width>
          </dimensions></extent>
      </supportDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="form-term">
      <objectDesc form="libellus"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <supportDesc material="perg">
          <support>Parchment.</support>
        </supportDesc>
      </objectDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="material-term">
      <supportDesc material="vellum"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <support>Vellum.</support>
      </supportDesc>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-physical-specs">
      <!-- The object's form and its material from their vocabularies; dimensions with their unit. -->
      <elementSpec ident="objectDesc" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="form-term" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An object's form is one of the vocabulary's seeds, or a new term of it.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:objectDesc[@form]">
              <sch:let name="terms" value="('codex', 'roll', 'sheet', 'faltbuch', 'roll-codex', 'booklet', 'quire',                 'other', 'unknown')"/>
              <sch:assert id="form-term" role="warning" test="@form = $terms">The form <sch:value-of select="@form"/>
                isn't among the vocabulary's seeds: check its spelling, or add it to the application's
                vocabulary.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="form" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="semi" mode="replace">
              <valItem ident="codex"><desc>leaves in gatherings, bound, read by turning them</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="roll"><desc>membranes sewn or glued end to end, read by unrolling</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="sheet"><desc>a single sheet, kept flat or folded, not part of a codex</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="faltbuch"><desc>a codex whose leaves are unfolded rather than turned</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="roll-codex"><desc>a roll folded and bound as a codex</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="booklet"><desc>a gathering of bifolia with a cover, complete in its text</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="quire"><desc>a complete gathering of bifolia, without a cover</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="other"><desc>another form</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="unknown"><desc>the form isn't known</desc></valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="supportDesc" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="material-term" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A support's material is one of the vocabulary's seeds, or a new term of it.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:supportDesc[@material]">
              <sch:let name="terms" value="('perg', 'chart', 'papyrus', 'palm', 'mixed', 'other', 'unknown')"/>
              <sch:assert id="material-term" role="warning" test="@material = $terms">The material <sch:value-of select="@material"/> isn't among the vocabulary's seeds: check its spelling, or add it to the
                application's vocabulary.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="material" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="semi" mode="replace">
              <valItem ident="perg"><desc>parchment</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="chart"><desc>paper</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="papyrus"><desc>papyrus</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="palm"><desc>palm leaves</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="mixed"><desc>paper and parchment together</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="other"><desc>another material</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="unknown"><desc>the material isn't known</desc></valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="dimensions" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="dimensions-unit" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>Dimensions give their unit.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:dimensions">
              <sch:assert id="dimensions-unit" test="@unit">Dimensions give their unit (@unit, such as
                mm).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-hands">
    <head>Hands</head>
    <p>A unit's hands are its <gi>handDesc</gi>'s <gi>handNote</gi>s, one per hand, each with an <att>xml:id</att>,
      its script and its execution from vocabularies (<att>script</att>, <att>execution</att>, which TEI lacks and
      the guide adds, as the Bodleian's guidelines do), its share of the unit (<att>scope</att>: <val>sole</val>,
      <val>major</val> or <val>minor</val>), its folios, and prose. The hands are counted from their notes. A scribe
      is named in the prose by <gi>persName</gi> with <att>ref</att>, and the hand is the scribe's by a relation
      (<ptr target="#relations"/>), which can carry its certainty and evidence. A change in the text, an
      <gi>add</gi>, a <gi>del</gi> or a <gi>subst</gi>, points to the hand that made it by <att>hand</att>
      (<ptr target="#transcription-changes"/>); a hand pointed to in the file is described there. A made-up unit with two
      hands, a correction by the second, and the main hand's scribe:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <handDesc>
        <handNote xml:id="hand-A" script="minusculeCaroline" execution="libraria" scope="major">
          <locus from="1r" to="40v">ff. 1r–40v</locus>: the main hand, perhaps <persName ref="#new_person_exemplus_scriba">Exemplus</persName>, whose name stands at the foot of f. 40v.</handNote>
        <handNote xml:id="hand-B" script="minusculeCaroline" scope="minor">Corrections between the lines, in a
          browner ink.</handNote>
      </handDesc>
      <p>Si quis <add hand="#hand-B">episcopus</add> migrauerit, deponatur.</p>
      <listRelation>
        <relation name="scribe" type="judgement" cert="medium" active="#hand-A" passive="#new_person_exemplus_scriba"/>
      </listRelation>
    </egXML>
    <p>A hand pointed to that the file doesn't describe is an error, and a script or an execution outside the seeds
      is reported:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="hand-declared">
      <p>Si quis <add hand="#hand-B">episcopus</add><!-- breaks the rule --> deponatur.</p>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="script-term">
      <handDesc>
        <handNote xml:id="hand-carolingian" script="carolingian"><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <p>The main hand.</p>
        </handNote>
      </handDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="execution-term">
      <handDesc>
        <handNote xml:id="hand-calligraphic" script="minusculeCaroline" execution="calligraphic"><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <p>The main hand.</p>
        </handNote>
      </handDesc>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-hands-specs">
      <!-- A unit's hands: counted from their notes; each with its script and execution from the vocabularies; the
           scribe by a name in the prose and a relation; a hand pointed to exists. -->
      <elementSpec ident="handDesc" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="hands" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="handNote" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="script-term" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A hand's script is one of the vocabulary's seeds, or a new term of it.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:handNote[@script]">
              <sch:let name="terms" value="('capitalsSquare', 'capitalsRustic', 'uncial', 'halfUncial',                 'minusculeInsular', 'minusculeVernacular', 'minusculeCaroline', 'minuscule', 'protogothic',                 'textualisNorthern', 'textualisSouthern', 'semitextualis', 'cursivaAntiquior', 'cursiva', 'hybrida',                 'gothicoAntiqua', 'humanistica', 'humanisticaTextualis', 'humanisticaSemitextualis',                 'humanisticaCursiva', 'muhaqqaq', 'rayhani', 'naskh', 'maghribi', 'bihari', 'nasta_liq', 'thuluth',                 'tawqi_', 'riqa_', 'ghubar', 'ta_liq', 'diwani', 'ruq_ah', 'siyaqah', 'unknown')"/>
              <sch:assert id="script-term" role="warning" test="every $s in tokenize(normalize-space(@script), ' ')                 satisfies $s = $terms">The script <sch:value-of select="@script"/> isn't among the vocabulary's seeds:
                check its spelling, or add it to the application's vocabulary.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="execution-term" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A hand's execution is one of the vocabulary's seeds, or a new term of it.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:handNote[@execution]">
              <sch:let name="terms" value="('formata', 'libraria', 'currens')"/>
              <sch:assert id="execution-term" role="warning" test="@execution = $terms">The execution <sch:value-of select="@execution"/> isn't among the vocabulary's seeds: check its spelling, or add it to the
                application's vocabulary.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="hand-declared" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A hand pointed to in the file is described there.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:*[@hand]">
              <sch:assert id="hand-declared" test="every $h in tokenize(normalize-space(@hand), ' ')[starts-with(.,                 '#')] satisfies //tei:handNote[@xml:id = substring($h, 2)]">@hand points to a hand the file describes,
                a handNote with that xml:id (<sch:value-of select="@hand"/>).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="script" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="semi" mode="replace">
              <valItem ident="capitalsSquare"><desc>square capitals</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="capitalsRustic"><desc>rustic capitals</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="uncial"><desc>uncial</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="halfUncial"><desc>half-uncial</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="minusculeInsular"><desc>insular minuscule</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="minusculeVernacular"><desc>English vernacular minuscule</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="minusculeCaroline"><desc>Caroline minuscule</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="minuscule"><desc>a minuscule not named otherwise</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="protogothic"><desc>protogothic, praegothica</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="textualisNorthern"><desc>northern textualis</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="textualisSouthern"><desc>southern textualis</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="semitextualis"><desc>semitextualis</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="cursivaAntiquior"><desc>cursiva antiquior</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="cursiva"><desc>cursiva</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="hybrida"><desc>hybrida</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="gothicoAntiqua"><desc>gothico-antiqua</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="humanistica"><desc>a humanistic script not named otherwise</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="humanisticaTextualis"><desc>humanistic textualis</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="humanisticaSemitextualis"><desc>humanistic semitextualis</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="humanisticaCursiva"><desc>humanistic cursive</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="muhaqqaq"><desc>muhaqqaq</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="rayhani"><desc>rayhani</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="naskh"><desc>naskh</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="maghribi"><desc>maghribi</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="bihari"><desc>bihari</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="nasta_liq"><desc>nasta'liq</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="thuluth"><desc>thuluth</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="tawqi_"><desc>tawqi'</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="riqa_"><desc>riqa'</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="ghubar"><desc>ghubar</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="ta_liq"><desc>ta'liq</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="diwani"><desc>diwani</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="ruq_ah"><desc>ruq'ah</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="siyaqah"><desc>siyaqah</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="unknown"><desc>the script isn't known</desc></valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="execution" mode="add" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>how carefully the hand writes its script</desc>
            <datatype>
              <dataRef key="teidata.enumerated"/>
            </datatype>
            <valList type="semi">
              <valItem ident="formata"><desc>calligraphic</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="libraria"><desc>an ordinary book hand</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="currens"><desc>informal, cursive</desc></valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="scribe" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="scribeRef" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="scriptRef" mode="delete"/>
          <attDef ident="medium" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-decoration">
    <head>Decoration</head>
    <p>A unit's decoration is its <gi>decoDesc</gi>'s <gi>decoNote</gi>s, each of a kind, in <att>type</att> from the
      vocabulary of decoration (<val>colInit</val> for coloured initials, <val>rubrication</val>,
      <val>miniature</val>, …), with its folios and prose. A made-up unit:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <decoDesc>
        <decoNote type="colInit"><locus from="1r" to="40v">ff. 1r–40v</locus>: red and green initials at the
          chapters.</decoNote>
        <decoNote type="rubrication">Rubrics in red.</decoNote>
      </decoDesc>
    </egXML>
    <p>A kind outside the seeds is reported:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="decoration-term">
      <decoDesc>
        <decoNote type="initials"><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <p>Red initials.</p>
        </decoNote>
      </decoDesc>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-decoration-specs">
      <!-- A decoration's type from its vocabulary. -->
      <elementSpec ident="decoNote" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="decoration-term" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A decoration's type is one of the vocabulary's seeds, or a new term of it.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:decoNote[@type]">
              <sch:let name="terms" value="('border', 'diagram', 'illustration', 'initial', 'marginal', 'micrography',                 'miniature', 'drawing', 'rubrication', 'bas-de-page', 'map', 'headpiece', 'canonTable', 'carpetPage',                 'histInit', 'decInit', 'flourInit', 'colInit', 'plainInit', 'chrysography', 'lineFill', 'cadel',                 'instructions', 'unfilled', 'none', 'other', 'foreedge')"/>
              <sch:assert id="decoration-term" role="warning" test="@type = $terms">The decoration <sch:value-of select="@type"/> isn't among the vocabulary's seeds: check its spelling, or add it to the
                application's vocabulary.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="semi" mode="replace">
              <valItem ident="border"><desc>borders</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="diagram"><desc>diagrams</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="illustration"><desc>illustrations</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="initial"><desc>initials</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="marginal"><desc>marginal decoration</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="micrography"><desc>micrography</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="miniature"><desc>miniatures, coloured drawings</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="drawing"><desc>uncoloured drawings, sketches</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="rubrication"><desc>rubrication and other highlighting</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="bas-de-page"><desc>scenes at the foot of the page</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="map"><desc>maps</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="headpiece"><desc>a headpiece</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="canonTable"><desc>canon tables</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="carpetPage"><desc>carpet pages</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="histInit"><desc>historiated initials</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="decInit"><desc>decorated initials, not historiated</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="flourInit"><desc>pen-flourished initials or borders</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="colInit"><desc>coloured initials, not decorated or flourished</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="plainInit"><desc>plain initials, in the ink of the text</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="chrysography"><desc>writing in gold</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="lineFill"><desc>line fillers</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="cadel"><desc>cadels</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="instructions"><desc>instructions for the decoration</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="unfilled"><desc>space left for decoration, unfilled</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="none"><desc>no decoration</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="other"><desc>another kind</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="foreedge"><desc>a painted or decorated fore-edge</desc></valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-binding">
    <head>The binding</head>
    <p>The binding is the manuscript's, in its own physical description, which holds nothing else: a
      <gi>bindingDesc</gi> with a <gi>binding</gi>, described in paragraphs. Oxford encoding guide: "The standard
      dating attributes should be used on the binding element if information about the date of the binding is
      available." Its bounds go on <gi>binding</gi>, in the one form of dates (<ptr target="#dates"/>), and where
      they aren't known, <att>contemporary</att> says whether it is the manuscript's first binding: <val>true</val>,
      <val>false</val>, or <val>unknown</val> where nothing is known. The binding's materials and techniques are
      <gi>term</gi>s in its prose, each pointing by <att>ref</att> to its concept in Ligatus's Language of Bindings;
      its dimensions, with <gi>depth</gi> beside height and width, stand in its prose too. An earlier binding of one
      unit, before it was joined to the others, is said in a paragraph of the unit's physical description. A made-up
      binding:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <bindingDesc>
        <binding notBefore="1450" notAfter="1500" contemporary="false">
          <p><term ref="http://w3id.org/lob/concept/1238">Calfskin</term> over wooden boards, blind-tooled; two clasps,
            lost. <dimensions type="binding" unit="mm">
              <height>250</height>
              <width>175</width>
              <depth>60</depth>
            </dimensions></p>
        </binding>
      </bindingDesc>
    </egXML>
    <p>A binding neither dated nor said contemporary is an error, and so is a value of <att>contemporary</att> outside
      the three:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="binding-dated">
      <binding><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <p>Calf over boards.</p>
      </binding>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="binding-contemporary">
      <binding contemporary="inapplicable"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <p>Calf over boards.</p>
      </binding>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-binding-specs">
      <!-- A binding is dated, or said contemporary, not, or unknown. -->
      <elementSpec ident="binding" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="binding-dated" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A binding is dated, or says whether it is contemporary.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:binding">
              <sch:assert id="binding-dated" test="@contemporary or @when or @notBefore or @notAfter or @from or                 @to">A binding is dated by its bounds, or says whether it is contemporary with the manuscript
                (@contemporary: true, false, or unknown where nothing is known).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="contemporary" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="binding-contemporary" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="true">
                <desc>the binding is contemporary with the manuscript</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="false">
                <desc>the binding is later</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="unknown">
                <desc>whether it is contemporary isn't known</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-history">
    <head>History</head>
    <p>A unit's history (<gi>history</gi>) is its origin and its owners; the manuscript's, the owners of the whole book
      since its units were joined, and where it was assembled. The origin (<gi>origin</gi>) holds the unit's date,
      <gi>origDate</gi>, in the one form of dates, its calendar named (<ptr target="#dates"/>), and its place,
      <gi>origPlace</gi>, pointing to the place's record by <att>ref</att>; the import makes the relation
      <val>origin</val> from them. Each owner is a <gi>provenance</gi>, or an <gi>acquisition</gi> for the library
      that holds the manuscript now, with its dates and the owner named inside by <gi>persName</gi>,
      <gi>orgName</gi> or <gi>placeName</gi> with <att>ref</att>, since TEI gives neither a pointer of its own; the
      import makes the relation <val>ownedBy</val>. Oxford encoding guide: "where possible each provenance event
      should occur in its own provenance element, which should have relevant dating attributes (when, notBefore,
      etc.)." Clm 4592's third unit was made at Salzburg in the second quarter of the ninth century:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.400.ms-Mun-BSB-4592">
      <origin>
        <origPlace ref="#new_place_salzburg">Salzburg</origPlace>
        <origDate notBefore="0826" notAfter="0850" calendar="#gregorian">sec. IX secondo quarto</origDate>
      </origin>
    </egXML>
    <p>A made-up owner, with the years of its ownership:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <provenance notBefore="1460" notAfter="1520">Owned by <orgName ref="#new_org_ecclesia_exemplensis">the cathedral
          of Exemplum</orgName>, whose ex libris stands on f. 1r.</provenance>
    </egXML>
    <p>An <gi>origDate</gi> has no type but <val>undated</val> (<ptr target="#manuscripts-units"/>):</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="orig-date-type">
      <origDate type="circa" notBefore="0776" notAfter="0825" calendar="#gregorian">ca. 800</origDate><!-- breaks the rule -->
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="manuscripts-history-specs">
      <!-- A unit's date, or its statement that it is undated; its place points to its record. -->
      <elementSpec ident="origDate" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="orig-date-type" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="undated">
                <desc>the unit is undated: no bounds, and the source's words if it has any</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="origPlace" module="msdescription" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="ref" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
              <dataRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
            </datatype>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-additional">
    <head>Images, literature, sources</head>
    <p>What a description adds about the manuscript goes in the manuscript's <gi>additional</gi>: the description's
      own sources, in <gi>adminInfo</gi>'s <gi>recordHist</gi>, each a <gi>bibl</gi> typed <val>source</val> in
      <gi>source</gi>, as every record names its own (<ptr target="#records-sources"/>); the manuscript's images, in
      <gi>surrogates</gi>, each IIIF manifest a <gi>ref</gi> typed <val>iiif</val>, its address in <att>target</att>
      and its provider in its words, which the application shows the pages from (a page break's canvas is the page
      break's, <ptr target="#transcription-page-breaks"/>); and the literature on it, in <gi>listBibl</gi>, in the
      forms of Part IX. A made-up manuscript's:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <additional>
        <adminInfo>
          <recordHist>
            <source>
              <bibl type="source">
                <title>Mirabile</title>
                <idno type="mirabile">123456</idno>
                <date type="retrieved" when="2026-11-04">4 November 2026</date>
              </bibl>
            </source>
          </recordHist>
        </adminInfo>
        <surrogates>
          <ref type="iiif" target="https://example.org/iiif/can-12/manifest.json">Bibliotheca Exemplensis</ref>
        </surrogates>
        <listBibl>
          <bibl><ptr target="#new_bibl_catalogue_exemplensis"/><citedRange unit="page" from="12" to="13">12–13</citedRange></bibl>
        </listBibl>
      </additional>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="manuscripts-bodleian">
    <head>The Bodleian's descriptions</head>
    <p>The Bodleian Libraries' catalogues, and others written in their form, describe manuscripts in TEI with a schema
      of their own, which this part's description is modelled on. Their descriptions are an import format: the
      application converts them into the project's form, as a tool of this package will. They needn't pass this
      guide's schema; before they are converted, they are checked against their own, at the commit the package
      pins:</p>
    <eg>solemne-tei validate --format bodleian description.xml</eg>
    <p>The table in <ptr target="#appendix-mappings"/> says what the conversion does with each element and attribute
      of the Bodleian's description: keeps it, moves it, makes a record or a relation of it, or drops it, and why. Of
      the project's coded files, 38 carry a description in the Bodleian's form, written before this guide; the
      corrections bring them to the guide's form.</p>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="editions-and-literature" n="IX">
  <head>Editions and literature</head>
  <p>A publication, the edition a text was transcribed from or the literature on it, is a <gi>biblStruct</gi>: in the
    source description of a file that transcribes an edition (<ptr target="#header-publication"/>); in the standOff's
    <gi>listBibl</gi>, as a record that witnesses and other records point to (<ptr target="#records-witnesses"/>); in
    a manuscript's literature (<ptr target="#manuscripts-additional"/>). The application keeps publications as
    records modelled on biblatex, related to one another, a text in a volume and a volume in a series, and one kind
    serves for the editions and the literature. Pages belong to the relation, not to the publication: a council's
    canons are in Werminghoff's volume at pages 318 to 421, and the volume's record says nothing of them.</p>
  <p>The source description's <gi>biblStruct</gi> is <hi>common</hi>, since most files transcribe an edition; its
    entry type and a biblatex key are <hi>full</hi>.</p>

  <div xml:id="editions-parts">
    <head>A publication's parts</head>
    <p>A <gi>biblStruct</gi> has up to three parts, each with its title, whose <att>level</att> says which part it
      belongs to:</p>
    <list>
      <item><gi>analytic</gi>: a text with its own title in a larger publication, a council in a volume of councils,
        an article in a journal; its title is level <val>a</val>, and it names its own author or editor where it has
        one;</item>
      <item><gi>monogr</gi>: the publication itself, a book or a volume (its title level <val>m</val>, a second title
        typed <val>sub</val> where the title page has one) or a journal (<val>j</val>); its authors or editors; its
        <gi>imprint</gi>, with the publisher, the place and the date; and, in <gi>biblScope</gi>, the volume, part or
        issue, and the pages where the text of the <gi>analytic</gi> stands;</item>
      <item><gi>series</gi>: the series the volume belongs to, its title level <val>s</val>, its editor where it has
        one, and the volume's number in it, in <gi>biblScope</gi>.</item>
    </list>
    <p>The edition of the Monumenta Germaniae Historica is the case the parts are made for: a series with its editor,
      the society that publishes it; a titled volume, edited by a scholar; and in it the texts. The Council of Aachen
      of 816:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.AAC-0816">
      <biblStruct>
        <analytic>
          <title level="a">Concilium Aquisgranense a. 816 (mensibus Augusto et Septembri)</title>
        </analytic>
        <monogr>
          <editor>
            <persName>
              <forename>Albert</forename>
              <surname>Werminghoff</surname>
            </persName>
          </editor>
          <title level="m" type="main">Concilia aevi Karolini</title>
          <title level="m" type="sub">Pars I</title>
          <imprint>
            <publisher>impensis Bibliopolii Hahniani</publisher>
            <pubPlace>Hannover</pubPlace>
            <pubPlace>Leipzig</pubPlace>
            <date>1906</date>
          </imprint>
          <biblScope unit="part">1</biblScope>
          <biblScope unit="page">307-464</biblScope>
        </monogr>
        <series>
          <editor>
            <orgName>Societas Aperiendis Fontibus Rerum Germanicarum Medii Aevi</orgName>
          </editor>
          <title level="s">Monumenta Germaniae Historica</title>
          <title level="s">Concilia</title>
          <biblScope unit="volume">2.1</biblScope>
        </series>
        <citedRange unit="page">318-421</citedRange>
      </biblStruct>
    </egXML>
    <p>Where the texts of a volume have editors of their own, each <gi>analytic</gi> names its editor, and the
      <gi>monogr</gi> the volume's. The First Council of Constantinople, edited by Adolf Martin Ritter in Giuseppe
      Alberigo's volume of the Conciliorum Oecumenicorum Generaliumque Decreta:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.CON-0381-v5">
      <biblStruct>
        <analytic>
          <editor>
            <persName>
              <forename>Adolf Martin</forename>
              <surname>Ritter</surname>
            </persName>
          </editor>
          <title level="a">Concilium Constantinopolitanum I - 381</title>
        </analytic>
        <monogr>
          <editor>
            <persName>
              <forename>Giuseppe</forename>
              <surname>Alberigo</surname>
            </persName>
          </editor>
          <title level="m">The oecumenical councils: from Nicaea I to Nicaea II (325-787)</title>
          <idno type="ISBN">978-2-503-52363-7</idno>
          <imprint>
            <publisher>Brepols</publisher>
            <pubPlace>Turnhout</pubPlace>
            <date>2006</date>
          </imprint>
          <biblScope unit="page">35-70</biblScope>
        </monogr>
        <series>
          <title level="s">Corpus Christianorum</title>
          <title level="s">Conciliorum Oecumenicorum Generaliumque Decreta</title>
          <biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope>
        </series>
        <citedRange unit="page">64-70</citedRange>
      </biblStruct>
    </egXML>
    <p>A title of the wrong level for its part is an error, since the level says what the title is:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="title-level">
      <biblStruct>
        <monogr>
          <title level="a">Concilia exempli gratia</title><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <imprint>
            <date>1906</date>
          </imprint>
        </monogr>
      </biblStruct>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="editions-pages">
    <head>Pages</head>
    <p>A publication has two kinds of pages, and each has its element:</p>
    <list>
      <item><gi>biblScope</gi> in <gi>monogr</gi>, typed by its <att>unit</att> <val>page</val>, says where the text of
        the <gi>analytic</gi> stands in the volume: Aachen's council at pages 307 to 464 of the MGH's volume. It's part
        of the publication's description, and the import relates the text to the volume by it;</item>
      <item><gi>citedRange</gi>, after the <gi>monogr</gi> and the <gi>series</gi>, says which pages the file's text
        comes from: the canons at pages 318 to 421. They're the witness's pages, the relation's. Where the file
        transcribes the whole text of the <gi>analytic</gi>, there's none: its pages are the witness's.</item>
    </list>
    <p>Either may give its range in <att>from</att> and <att>to</att> beside its words. An older edition printed in
      columns counts columns: the letter of Pope Celestine I to the bishops of Vienne and Narbonne, in Coustant's
      edition of the popes' letters:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-D.CEL-0428-369">
      <biblStruct>
        <analytic>
          <title level="a">Epistola IV / Caelestini papae I. ad episcopos prouinciae Viennensis &amp;
            Narbonensis</title>
        </analytic>
        <monogr>
          <editor>
            <persName>
              <forename>Pierre</forename>
              <surname>Coustant</surname>
            </persName>
          </editor>
          <title level="m" type="main">Epistolae Romanorum pontificum, et quae ad eos scriptae sunt a S. Clemente I.
            usque ad Innocentium III</title>
          <title level="m" type="sub">Tom. I: ab anno Christi 67 ad annum 440</title>
          <imprint>
            <publisher>Ludovicus-Dionysius Delatour</publisher>
            <publisher>Antonius-Urbanus Coustelier</publisher>
            <pubPlace>Paris</pubPlace>
            <date>1721</date>
          </imprint>
          <biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope>
          <biblScope unit="column" from="1065" to="1072">1065-1072</biblScope>
        </monogr>
      </biblStruct>
    </egXML>
    <p>The units are a closed list, since each is a field of biblatex: <val>volume</val>, <val>part</val>,
      <val>issue</val>, <val>page</val> and <val>column</val>. A volume in a <gi>series</gi> is the volume's number in
      the series, a tome or a fascicle of it too; an issue is a journal's, a <foreign xml:lang="de">Heft</foreign>.
      Another unit fails the RelaxNG:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="scope-unit">
      <biblStruct>
        <monogr>
          <title level="m">Concilia exempli gratia</title>
          <imprint>
            <date>1906</date>
          </imprint>
        </monogr>
        <series>
          <title level="s">Monumenta exempli gratia</title>
          <biblScope unit="tome">2</biblScope><!-- breaks the rule -->
        </series>
      </biblStruct>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="cited-range-unit">
      <biblStruct>
        <monogr>
          <title level="m">Concilia exempli gratia</title>
          <imprint>
            <date>1906</date>
          </imprint>
        </monogr>
        <citedRange unit="folio">12</citedRange><!-- breaks the rule -->
      </biblStruct>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="editions-pages-specs">
      <!-- The units of a publication's scope and of the pages cited from it: biblatex's fields. -->
      <elementSpec ident="biblScope" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="unit" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="scope-unit" corresp="#tier-common">
              <valItem ident="volume">
                <desc>a volume; in a series, the volume's number in it</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="part">
                <desc>a part of a volume</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="issue">
                <desc>an issue of a journal</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="page">
                <desc>pages</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="column">
                <desc>columns, where the publication counts them</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
      <elementSpec ident="citedRange" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="unit" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="cited-range-unit" corresp="#tier-full">
              <valItem ident="volume">
                <desc>a volume</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="part">
                <desc>a part of a volume</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="issue">
                <desc>an issue of a journal</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="page">
                <desc>pages</desc>
              </valItem>
              <valItem ident="column">
                <desc>columns, where the publication counts them</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="editions-biblatex">
    <head>Entry types and biblatex</head>
    <p>The application's publications are biblatex's entries, and the import and the exports turn one into the other
      by the tables below. A publication's entry type is in <att>type</att>, by biblatex's name; where it's missing,
      the parts say which it is:</p>
    <table xml:id="biblatex-types">
      <head>The entry type, where <att>type</att> doesn't give it</head>
      <row role="label">
        <cell>The parts</cell>
        <cell>biblatex's type</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>an <gi>analytic</gi>, in a <gi>monogr</gi> whose title is level <val>j</val></cell>
        <cell><val>article</val></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>an <gi>analytic</gi>, in a <gi>monogr</gi> whose title is level <val>m</val></cell>
        <cell><val>incollection</val></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>a <gi>monogr</gi> alone, with editors and no author</cell>
        <cell><val>collection</val></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>a <gi>monogr</gi> alone, otherwise</cell>
        <cell><val>book</val></cell>
      </row>
    </table>
    <p>A thesis, a report or a page on the web looks like a book in its parts, and so says what it is by
      <att>type</att>; an export always does, so that a publication exported and imported again keeps its type. The
      types are biblatex's, a vocabulary whose seeds are biblatex's own types; another is reported, to be checked or
      added. A biblatex key, where a publication has one, is an <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>biblatex</val> in the part
      the entry describes. A made-up thesis:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <listBibl>
        <biblStruct type="thesis" xml:id="new_bibl_exemplum_thesis">
          <monogr>
            <author>
              <persName>
                <forename>Anna</forename>
                <surname>Exempla</surname>
              </persName>
            </author>
            <title level="m">Canones exempli gratia</title>
            <idno type="biblatex">exempla2020</idno>
            <imprint>
              <publisher>Universitas Exemplensis</publisher>
              <date>2020</date>
            </imprint>
          </monogr>
        </biblStruct>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="entry-type">
      <listBibl>
        <biblStruct type="volume" xml:id="new_bibl_exemplum_volume">
          <monogr>
            <title level="m">Concilia exempli gratia</title>
            <imprint>
              <date>1906</date>
            </imprint>
          </monogr>
        </biblStruct>
      </listBibl>
    </egXML>
    <p>Each element of a <gi>biblStruct</gi> is a field of the entry:</p>
    <table xml:id="biblatex-fields">
      <head>A publication's elements and biblatex's fields</head>
      <row role="label">
        <cell>The element</cell>
        <cell>biblatex's field</cell>
      </row>
      <row role="label">
        <cell cols="2">The text in the publication</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>analytic</gi>/<gi>title</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>title</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>analytic</gi>/<gi>author</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>author</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>analytic</gi>/<gi>editor</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>editora</code>, with <code>editoratype</code> <code>editor</code>: the text's own editor, beside
          the volume's</cell>
      </row>
      <row role="label">
        <cell cols="2">The publication</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>monogr</gi>/<gi>title</gi>, level <val>m</val></cell>
        <cell><code>booktitle</code> where there's an <gi>analytic</gi>, <code>title</code> otherwise; one typed
          <val>sub</val>, <code>booksubtitle</code> or <code>subtitle</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>monogr</gi>/<gi>title</gi>, level <val>j</val></cell>
        <cell><code>journaltitle</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>monogr</gi>/<gi>author</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>bookauthor</code> where there's an <gi>analytic</gi>, <code>author</code> otherwise</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>monogr</gi>/<gi>editor</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>editor</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>imprint</gi>/<gi>publisher</gi>, <gi>pubPlace</gi>, <gi>date</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>publisher</code>, <code>location</code>, <code>date</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>monogr</gi>/<gi>biblScope</gi>: <val>volume</val>, <val>part</val>, <val>issue</val></cell>
        <cell><code>volume</code>, <code>part</code>, <code>number</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>monogr</gi>/<gi>biblScope</gi>: <val>page</val>, <val>column</val></cell>
        <cell><code>pages</code>; for columns, <code>pagination</code> <code>column</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>idno</gi> typed <val>ISBN</val>, <val>url</val>, <val>biblatex</val></cell>
        <cell><code>isbn</code>, <code>url</code>, and the entry's key</cell>
      </row>
      <row role="label">
        <cell cols="2">The series</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>series</gi>/<gi>title</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>series</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>series</gi>/<gi>biblScope</gi>, <val>volume</val></cell>
        <cell><code>number</code></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>series</gi>/<gi>editor</gi></cell>
        <cell>none: the application keeps it in the series' record</cell>
      </row>
      <row role="label">
        <cell cols="2">What isn't a field</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>citedRange</gi></cell>
        <cell>none: the pages of the relation, a witness's in the publication</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><gi>note</gi></cell>
        <cell><code>note</code></cell>
      </row>
    </table>
    <specGrp xml:id="editions-biblatex-specs">
      <!-- A publication: its titles' levels by its parts, and its entry type from biblatex's. -->
      <elementSpec ident="biblStruct" module="core" mode="change" corresp="#tier-common">
        <constraintSpec ident="title-level" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-common">
          <desc>A title's level says which part of a publication it belongs to: a for the analytic, m or j for the
            monogr, s for the series.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:biblStruct/tei:*[self::tei:analytic or self::tei:monogr or self::tei:series]/tei:title">
              <sch:let name="levels" value="if (parent::tei:analytic) then 'a' else if (parent::tei:monogr) then ('m',                 'j') else 's'"/>
              <sch:assert id="title-level" test="@level = $levels">A title in <sch:value-of select="local-name(..)"/> has the level <sch:value-of select="string-join($levels, ' or ')"/>, which
                says which part of the publication it belongs to.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="entry-type" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>A publication's entry type is one of biblatex's, or a new term of the vocabulary.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:biblStruct[@type]">
              <sch:let name="terms" value="('article', 'book', 'mvbook', 'inbook', 'bookinbook', 'suppbook', 'booklet',                 'collection', 'mvcollection', 'incollection', 'suppcollection', 'dataset', 'manual', 'misc', 'online',                 'patent', 'periodical', 'suppperiodical', 'proceedings', 'mvproceedings', 'inproceedings', 'reference',                 'mvreference', 'inreference', 'report', 'software', 'thesis', 'unpublished')"/>
              <sch:assert id="entry-type" role="warning" test="@type = $terms">The entry type <sch:value-of select="@type"/> isn't one of biblatex's: check its spelling, or add it to the application's
                vocabulary.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
            <desc>the publication's entry type, by biblatex's name</desc>
            <valList type="semi" mode="replace">
              <valItem ident="article"><desc>an article in a journal</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="book"><desc>a book by its authors</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="mvbook"><desc>a book in several volumes</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="inbook"><desc>a part of a book with its own title, by the book's authors</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="bookinbook"><desc>a book printed within another</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="suppbook"><desc>what a book adds to its text: a preface, an index</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="booklet"><desc>a printed work without a publisher</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="collection"><desc>a book of texts by several authors, with its editors</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="mvcollection"><desc>a collection in several volumes</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="incollection"><desc>a text with its own title in a collection</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="suppcollection"><desc>what a collection adds to its texts</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="dataset"><desc>a set of data</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="manual"><desc>a manual</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="misc"><desc>a publication no other type fits</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="online"><desc>a resource on the web</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="patent"><desc>a patent</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="periodical"><desc>an issue of a journal, as a whole</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="suppperiodical"><desc>what a journal's issue adds to its articles</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="proceedings"><desc>the proceedings of a conference</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="mvproceedings"><desc>proceedings in several volumes</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="inproceedings"><desc>a paper in a conference's proceedings</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="reference"><desc>a work of reference: a dictionary, an encyclopaedia</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="mvreference"><desc>a work of reference in several volumes</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="inreference"><desc>an entry in a work of reference</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="report"><desc>a report by an institution</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="software"><desc>a program</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="thesis"><desc>a thesis for a degree</desc></valItem>
              <valItem ident="unpublished"><desc>a work not published</desc></valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
          <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="editions-records">
    <head>Publications as records</head>
    <p>A publication that more than one thing refers to, the edition several witnesses are in, the catalogue a
      manuscript's literature cites, is listed once in the standOff's <gi>listBibl</gi>, with an <att>xml:id</att>,
      a record's or a placeholder's (<code>new_bibl_</code> and a name), and pointed to: a witness by a
      <gi>ptr</gi> and its pages in <gi>citedRange</gi> (<ptr target="#records-witnesses"/>), a manuscript's
      literature the same way. The import makes the application's records from a <gi>biblStruct</gi>: the volume, the
      text in it and the series, related to one another; then the file's witness, in the publication at the pages of
      the <gi>citedRange</gi>. An export writes each publication it points to as a <gi>biblStruct</gi> with its entry
      type.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="editions-reconstructions">
    <head>Reconstructions</head>
    <p>A reconstruction is a text put together from others: a collection known only from what later collections took
      from it, or a text the project assembles from several editions. Its file's type is <val>reconstruction</val>
      (<ptr target="#header-categories"/>), and its source description names the publication it comes from, says in a
      <gi>note</gi> that it's a reconstruction, and lists what it was made from in a <gi>listWit</gi>: the editions,
      for one the project put together; the manuscripts, for one a scholar made from them. The collection of
      Benedictus Levita, which the project puts together from Pertz's edition and Schmitz's of the first
      Additio:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-COLL.Benedictus">
      <sourceDesc>
        <biblStruct xml:id="pertz-1837">
          <analytic>
            <title level="a">Benedicti Capitularia</title>
          </analytic>
          <monogr>
            <editor>
              <persName>
                <forename>Georg Heinrich</forename>
                <surname>Pertz</surname>
              </persName>
            </editor>
            <title level="m">Supplementa tomi I</title>
            <imprint>
              <publisher>Hahnsche Buchhandlung</publisher>
              <pubPlace>Hannover</pubPlace>
              <date>1837</date>
            </imprint>
            <biblScope unit="page">17-158</biblScope>
          </monogr>
          <series>
            <title level="s">Monumenta Germaniae Historica</title>
            <title level="s">Leges</title>
            <biblScope unit="volume">2.2</biblScope>
          </series>
          <note>A reconstruction: the collection as the project puts it together, from Pertz's edition for the whole
            text, and from Schmitz's for Additio 1, which Pertz's lacks.</note>
        </biblStruct>
        <biblStruct xml:id="schmitz-2008">
          <monogr>
            <editor>
              <persName>
                <forename>Gerhard</forename>
                <surname>Schmitz</surname>
              </persName>
            </editor>
            <title level="m">Additio 1</title>
            <idno type="url">https://www.benedictus.mgh.de/edition/aktuell/additio1.pdf</idno>
            <imprint>
              <publisher>Gerhard Schmitz</publisher>
              <date>2008</date>
            </imprint>
          </monogr>
        </biblStruct>
        <listWit>
          <witness xml:id="wit-pertz"><bibl><ptr target="#pertz-1837"/><citedRange unit="page" from="17" to="158">17–158</citedRange></bibl>: the whole text but Additio 1</witness>
          <witness xml:id="wit-schmitz"><bibl><ptr target="#schmitz-2008"/></bibl>: Additio 1</witness>
        </listWit>
      </sourceDesc>
    </egXML>
    <p>The import makes the reconstruction a version whose witness is the publication, and joins it to the witnesses
      it lists. The template <code>reconstruction.xml</code> holds the whole header.</p>
  </div>
</div>
      <div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:id="exports" n="X">
  <head>Exports</head>
  <p>An export is what the application writes: a transcription, or a selection of it, with its structure, the
    annotations its reader may see, and the records it points to. It's written in the forms this guide gives for
    every file, so that an export imported again gives the same text and the same annotations, and it adds what only
    the application knows: its ids, its permalinks, its history and its statuses. This part says what an export adds,
    for anyone reading one. The elements that only exports write are <hi>export only</hi>: the citation structure,
    and text reuse; what an encoder may write too keeps its tier.</p>
  <p>The template <code>export.xml</code> is a short export of the Council of Soissons of 744, as the application
    writes it; the examples below come from it.</p>

  <div xml:id="exports-ids">
    <head>Ids, permalinks and records</head>
    <p>Every node has its <att>xml:id</att>, the id the application gave it when it was imported or made: the
      document's code, <code>_</code>, and the node's citation at that moment, with <code>.h1</code> or
      <code>.p1</code> for a heading or a paragraph (<ptr target="#codes-and-ids"/>). A node keeps its id when it
      moves, so an id may no longer match the node's citation. The formal title gives the levels the file belongs
      to, each by its permalink (<ptr target="#header-title"/>):</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.SOI-0744-export">
      <div type="chapter" n="1" xml:id="C.SOI-0744_1">
        <head type="number" xml:id="C.SOI-0744_1.h1"><pb n="34"/>(I).</head>
        <p xml:id="C.SOI-0744_1.p1">In primitus constituimus fide catholica, quam constituerunt CCCXVIII episcopi in
          <eventName ref="https://example.org/id/event/EVENT.1">Niceno concilio</eventName>, ut denuntiaretur per
          universa regione nostra …</p>
      </div>
    </egXML>
    <p>A pointer to anything outside the file, a record, a canon, another document, is a permalink, since one code can
      name several kinds of thing (<ptr target="#citations-and-permalinks"/>). The records an export points to are
      listed in its standOff, each with its own id as its <att>xml:id</att>, the permalink's last step, and its names,
      so that the file reads on its own. A record's links to pages that aren't an authority's record are written
      back in its note of links (<ptr target="#records-external-ids"/>), and every manuscript's date and every date in
      the text names its calendar (<ptr target="#dates-calendars"/>).</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="exports-citations">
    <head>The citation structure</head>
    <p>An export says how its nodes are cited, in the encoding description's <gi>refsDecl</gi>, by TEI's
      <gi>citeStructure</gi>: one for each kind of division at each depth the document has, its <att>match</att>
      selecting the divisions and its <att>use</att> giving each one's step of the citation, by the rules of <ptr target="#citations-and-permalinks"/>. A chapter or a register item is cited by its number, in <att>n</att>
      (<code>11a</code> for one without a number after 11); a section by its number, or its position; the other
      divisions by a prefix and their position among their kind. Nested divisions nest their
      <gi>citeStructure</gi>s, and their steps are joined by <att>delim</att>, a dot. The council's preface and its
      chapters:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.SOI-0744-export">
      <refsDecl>
        <citeStructure unit="preface" match="/TEI/text/body/div[@type = 'preface']" use="concat('pr', count(preceding-sibling::div[@type = 'preface']) + 1)"/>
        <citeStructure unit="chapter" match="/TEI/text/body/div[@type = 'chapter']" use="@n"/>
      </refsDecl>
    </egXML>
    <p>DTS reads the same structure. A version's canons have their own citations; a transcription's chapters are
      joined to them by relations, not by its citation structure.</p>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="exports-annotations">
    <head>Annotations in the standOff</head>
    <p>An export writes an annotation inline, in the element of its layer, where it nests in the others; where it
      would cross one, it writes it in the standOff's <gi>listAnnotation</gi>, as an <gi>annotation</gi>. An encoder
      may do the same, by anchors (<ptr target="#marked-anchors"/>). A stand-off annotation has:</p>
    <list>
      <item><hi>its target</hi>, in <att>target</att>: a span of a node's text, by TEI's <code>string-range()</code>,
        the node's id, where the span begins and how long it is (<code>#string-range(C.SOI-0744_pr1.p1,26,137)</code>),
        counted in characters of the node's text as the application holds it, its words without the markup and each
        run of spaces as one, from 0; or a node, an anchor or the range between two, by their ids. An annotation
        over several spans or nodes has several targets;</item>
      <item><hi>its author</hi>, in a <gi>respStmt</gi> naming them by permalink, and its licence, in a
        <gi>licence</gi>;</item>
      <item><hi>its body</hi>: a <gi>note</gi> holding the element of its layer, with the attributes it has inline and
        the span's words, which the import checks the target against; for a note's layers, a source note, a parallel
        or an editorial remark, the <gi>note</gi> itself, typed by its layer. One form for each layer, inline or not,
        so that the import reads both alike. TEI's <att>motivation</att> isn't used: the body says the layer.</item>
    </list>
    <p>The council's date, from its year to the king's, crosses the name of Childeric, which runs on past it, so it
      stands in the standOff:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.SOI-0744-export">
      <p xml:id="C.SOI-0744_pr1.p1"><pb n="33"/>In Dei nomine trinitatis. Anno septingentesimo quadragesimo quarto ab
        incarnatione Christi sub die VI. Nonas Martii et luna XIIII. in anno secundo <persName ref="https://example.org/id/person/PERS.31">Childerici regis Francorum</persName> ego <persName ref="https://example.org/id/person/PERS.32">Pippinus, dux et princeps Francorum</persName>. …</p>
      <listAnnotation>
        <annotation xml:id="annotation-date" target="#string-range(C.SOI-0744_pr1.p1,26,137)">
          <respStmt>
            <resp>dated</resp>
            <persName ref="https://example.org/id/person/PERS.12">An editor</persName>
          </respStmt>
          <licence target="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">Creative Commons Attribution 4.0</licence>
          <note><date when="0744-03-06" calendar="#julian">Anno septingentesimo quadragesimo quarto ab incarnatione
              Christi sub die VI. Nonas Martii et luna XIIII. in anno secundo Childerici regis</date></note>
        </annotation>
      </listAnnotation>
    </egXML>
    <p>A target points into the file or to a permalink, and a body is one <gi>note</gi>, which holds the layer's
      element alone, or is typed by a note's layer:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="annotation-target">
      <listAnnotation>
        <annotation xml:id="annotation-lost" target="#string-range(nowhere,0,10)"><!-- breaks the rule -->
          <note type="editorial">A remark.</note>
        </annotation>
      </listAnnotation>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="annotation-body">
      <p xml:id="annotated">Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      <listAnnotation>
        <annotation xml:id="annotation-words" target="#string-range(annotated,0,7)">
          <note>A remark, and <persName ref="#new_person_Primus">Primus</persName>.</note><!-- breaks the rule -->
        </annotation>
      </listAnnotation>
    </egXML>
    <p>The relations an export writes, its nodes' observations and, on request, the judgements on its canons, are in
      its standOff's <gi>listRelation</gi>, in their one form (<ptr target="#relations"/>).</p>
    <specGrp xml:id="exports-annotations-specs">
      <!-- A stand-off annotation: its targets in the file or elsewhere, and its body the layer's element in a note. -->
      <elementSpec ident="annotation" module="linking" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="annotation-target" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An annotation's targets are ids in the file, ranges between them, spans of their text, or
            permalinks.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:annotation">
              <sch:let name="ids" value="//@xml:id"/>
              <sch:assert id="annotation-target" test="every $t in tokenize(normalize-space(@target), ' ') satisfies                 (matches($t, '^https?://\S+$') or (matches($t, '^#[^()]+$') and substring($t, 2) = $ids) or                 (matches($t, '^#range\([^,()]+,[^,()]+\)$') and replace($t, '^#range\(([^,()]+),.*$', '$1') = $ids and                 replace($t, '^#range\([^,()]+,([^,()]+)\)$', '$1') = $ids) or (matches($t,                 '^#string-range\([^,()]+,\d+,\d+\)$') and replace($t, '^#string-range\(([^,()]+),.*$', '$1') =                 $ids))">An annotation's target (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(@target)"/>) is an id in the
                file, a range between two, a span of one's text by string-range(), or a permalink.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <constraintSpec ident="annotation-body" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-full">
          <desc>An annotation's body is one note: holding the element of its layer alone, or typed by a note's
            layer.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:annotation">
              <sch:assert id="annotation-body" test="count(tei:note) = 1 and not(tei:ptr or tei:ref) and                 (tei:note/@type = ('source', 'parallel', 'editorial') or (count(tei:note/*) = 1 and                 not(tei:note/text()[normalize-space()])))">An annotation's body is one note, which holds the element of
                its layer alone, or is typed by a note's layer (source, parallel, editorial).</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="motivation" mode="delete"/>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="exports-reuse">
    <head>Text reuse</head>
    <p>Text reuse the project has accepted is a relation, "is a text of", with its evidence (<ptr target="#relations-levels"/>). The raw hits of a run of the reuse detection go into an export only on
      request, filtered as the export is: one <gi>linkGrp</gi> typed <val>reuse</val> for each run, pointing to the
      run by <att>resp</att>, and one <gi>link</gi> for each hit, its <att>xml:id</att> the hit's, with two targets:
      the passage in the file, and the passage in the other document, its permalink followed by the same pointer.
      Scores and lengths go in the CSV export of the same selection, under the same ids. A made-up hit:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples">
      <p xml:id="C.SOI-0744_2.p1">Propterea nos una cum consensu episcoporum sive sacerdotum seu servorum Dei …</p>
      <linkGrp type="reuse" resp="https://example.org/id/run/3">
        <link xml:id="hit-41" target="#string-range(C.SOI-0744_2.p1,0,40) https://example.org/id/transcription/C.ASC-0756#string-range(C.ASC-0756_1.p1,0,40)"/>
      </linkGrp>
    </egXML>
    <p>A hit joins two passages:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="reuse-link">
      <p xml:id="reused">Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      <linkGrp type="reuse" resp="https://example.org/id/run/1">
        <link xml:id="hit-1" target="#string-range(reused,0,7)"/><!-- breaks the rule -->
      </linkGrp>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="link-group-type">
      <p xml:id="grouped">Placuit ut episcopi in ciuitatibus suis maneant.</p>
      <linkGrp type="parallels"><!-- breaks the rule -->
        <link xml:id="hit-2" target="#string-range(grouped,0,7) https://example.org/id/node/C.NIC-0325_15.p1"/>
      </linkGrp>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="exports-reuse-specs">
      <!-- Text reuse: a run's hits, each joining two passages. -->
      <elementSpec ident="linkGrp" module="linking" mode="change" corresp="#tier-export-only">
        <constraintSpec ident="reuse-link" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-export-only">
          <desc>A hit of text reuse joins two passages.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:linkGrp[@type = 'reuse']/tei:link">
              <sch:assert id="reuse-link" test="count(tokenize(normalize-space(@target), ' ')) = 2">A hit of text
                reuse has two targets: the passage in the file, and the passage in the other document.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-export-only">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="link-group-type" corresp="#tier-export-only">
              <valItem ident="reuse">
                <desc>the hits of a run of the reuse detection</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="exports-history">
    <head>Statuses and history</head>
    <p>The text's curation is in the encoding description's <gi>correction</gi>, as in any file (<ptr target="#header-curation"/>); its completeness isn't stated, since the gaps give it. Its history is in the
      <gi>revisionDesc</gi>, the latest first:</p>
    <list>
      <item><hi>the releases</hi>: each a <gi>change</gi> typed <val>release</val>, its name in <att>n</att>
        (<code>2027.1</code>), its date in <att>when</att>, and as its words a summary of the changes since the last
        release;</item>
      <item><hi>the change sets</hi>, on request: each a <gi>change</gi> without a type, its date, its author's
        permalink in <att>who</att>, the nodes it touched in <att>target</att>, and its message as its words. An
        earlier text of a node is pointed to by its permalink and version (<code>…@t3</code>), not copied.</item>
    </list>
    <p>The history a file had before its import stays as it was, read-only (<ptr target="#header-revisions"/>). The
      council's first release, and a change set before it:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.SOI-0744-export">
      <revisionDesc>
        <change type="release" n="2027.1" when="2027-06-30">The project's first release: the council checked against
          its edition, its persons, places and events identified.</change>
        <change when="2027-05-02" who="https://example.org/id/person/PERS.12" target="#C.SOI-0744_2.p1">A reading
          corrected against the edition.</change>
      </revisionDesc>
    </egXML>
    <p>A release has its name and its date, and a change is a release or a change set:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="release-named">
      <revisionDesc>
        <change type="release">A release.</change><!-- breaks the rule -->
      </revisionDesc>
    </egXML>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" valid="false" n="change-type">
      <revisionDesc>
        <change type="correction" when="2027-05-02">A reading corrected.</change><!-- breaks the rule -->
      </revisionDesc>
    </egXML>
    <specGrp xml:id="exports-history-specs">
      <!-- The history: releases, each named and dated, and change sets. -->
      <elementSpec ident="change" module="header" mode="change" corresp="#tier-full">
        <constraintSpec ident="release-named" scheme="schematron" corresp="#tier-export-only">
          <desc>A release has its name and its date.</desc>
          <constraint>
            <sch:rule context="tei:change[@type = 'release']">
              <sch:assert id="release-named" test="@n and @when">A release names itself in @n and gives its date in
                @when.</sch:assert>
            </sch:rule>
          </constraint>
        </constraintSpec>
        <attList>
          <attDef ident="type" mode="change" corresp="#tier-export-only">
            <valList type="closed" mode="replace" xml:id="change-type" corresp="#tier-export-only">
              <valItem ident="release">
                <desc>a release of the application's data</desc>
              </valItem>
            </valList>
          </attDef>
        </attList>
      </elementSpec>
    </specGrp>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="exports-vocabularies">
    <head>The vocabularies</head>
    <p>An export declares the terms of the application's vocabularies it uses, in the encoding description's
      <gi>classDecl</gi>: a <gi>taxonomy</gi> for each vocabulary, and in it a <gi>category</gi> for each term, its
      <att>xml:id</att> the term's name, its labels in <gi>catDesc</gi>, one for each language, its permalink in
      <att>sameAs</att>, since the category is that term, and its equivalents elsewhere in <att>corresp</att>. An
      element that uses a term names it, as an attribute's value or by a pointer, as the guide's parts say. The
      council's relations use one term:</p>
    <egXML xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/Examples" source="#source-C.SOI-0744-export">
      <classDecl>
        <taxonomy xml:id="relation-types">
          <category xml:id="isTextOf" sameAs="https://example.org/id/term/isTextOf">
            <catDesc xml:lang="en">is a text of</catDesc>
          </category>
        </taxonomy>
      </classDecl>
    </egXML>
  </div>

  <div xml:id="exports-checks">
    <head>Both checks</head>
    <p>An export passes both checks, the RelaxNG and the Schematron, without an error, and declares the schema it was
      checked against by <code>&lt;?xml-model?&gt;</code>, as the templates do (<ptr target="#intro-checks"/>). The
      application's own tests check its exports so, against the release of this package it installs; what an export
      must hold beyond what every file may, they check too. The template <code>export.xml</code> passes both checks
      without a warning.</p>
  </div>
</div>

      <div xml:id="specification">
        <head>The specification</head>
        <p>The schema takes the modules of TEI below, and from each only the elements listed: an element outside
          them fails the RelaxNG. Each part's specifications follow, in the parts' order. An element the
          specifications change carries its tier there; the others take theirs from this table.</p>
        <table xml:id="element-tiers">
          <head>The elements taken from TEI unchanged, by tier</head>
          <row role="label">
            <cell>Tier</cell>
            <cell>Elements</cell>
          </row>
          <row corresp="#tier-required">
            <cell>required</cell>
            <cell>
              <gi>fileDesc</gi> <gi>publicationStmt</gi> <gi>sourceDesc</gi> <gi>TEI</gi> <gi>text</gi>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row corresp="#tier-common">
            <cell>common</cell>
            <cell>
              <gi>address</gi> <gi>analytic</gi>
              <gi>distributor</gi> <gi>email</gi> <gi>forename</gi> <gi>imprint</gi> <gi>listPerson</gi>
              <gi>monogr</gi> <gi>p</gi> <gi>postCode</gi> <gi>publisher</gi> <gi>pubPlace</gi>
              <gi>series</gi> <gi>standOff</gi> <gi>street</gi> <gi>surname</gi>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row corresp="#tier-full">
            <cell>full</cell>
            <cell>
              <gi>abbr</gi> <gi>abstract</gi> <gi>acquisition</gi> <gi>add</gi> <gi>additional</gi> <gi>additions</gi>
              <gi>addName</gi> <gi>addrLine</gi> <gi>adminInfo</gi> <gi>affiliation</gi> <gi>anchor</gi>
              <gi>authority</gi> <gi>availability</gi> <gi>bindingDesc</gi> <gi>birth</gi>
              <gi>bloc</gi> <gi>calendarDesc</gi> <gi>catDesc</gi> <gi>category</gi> <gi>choice</gi>
              <gi>cit</gi> <gi>classDecl</gi> <gi>collation</gi> <gi>colophon</gi>
              <gi>condition</gi> <gi>corr</gi> <gi>correction</gi> <gi>creation</gi> <gi>death</gi> <gi>decoDesc</gi>
              <gi>del</gi> <gi>depth</gi> <gi>desc</gi> <gi>district</gi> <gi>edition</gi> <gi>editionStmt</gi>
              <gi>encodingDesc</gi> <gi>expan</gi> <gi>explicit</gi> <gi>extent</gi> <gi>f</gi> <gi>finalRubric</gi>
              <gi>foliation</gi> <gi>foreign</gi> <gi>fs</gi> <gi>funder</gi> <gi>genName</gi> <gi>geo</gi>
              <gi>geogName</gi> <gi>graphic</gi> <gi>height</gi> <gi>hi</gi> <gi>history</gi> <gi>incipit</gi>
              <gi>interpretation</gi> <gi>item</gi> <gi>keywords</gi> <gi>label</gi> <gi>language</gi>
              <gi>langUsage</gi> <gi>layout</gi> <gi>layoutDesc</gi> <gi>lb</gi> <gi>licence</gi>
              <gi>listAnnotation</gi> <gi>listBibl</gi> <gi>listChange</gi> <gi>listEvent</gi> <gi>listOrg</gi>
              <gi>listPlace</gi> <gi>listPrefixDef</gi> <gi>listWit</gi> <gi>location</gi> <gi>locus</gi>
              <gi>locusGrp</gi> <gi>measure</gi> <gi>msContents</gi> <gi>msIdentifier</gi> <gi>msName</gi>
              <gi>name</gi> <gi>nameLink</gi> <gi>normalization</gi> <gi>notesStmt</gi> <gi>num</gi>
              <gi>occupation</gi> <gi>org</gi> <gi>orig</gi> <gi>origin</gi> <gi>physDesc</gi> <gi>place</gi>
              <gi>principal</gi> <gi>profileDesc</gi> <gi>projectDesc</gi> <gi>provenance</gi> <gi>punctuation</gi>
              <gi>q</gi> <gi>quotation</gi> <gi>recordHist</gi> <gi>refsDecl</gi> <gi>reg</gi> <gi>resp</gi>
              <gi>respStmt</gi> <gi>revisionDesc</gi> <gi>roleName</gi> <gi>rubric</gi> <gi>samplingDecl</gi>
              <gi>seriesStmt</gi> <gi>sic</gi> <gi>source</gi> <gi>sponsor</gi> <gi>stdVals</gi> <gi>subst</gi>
              <gi>summary</gi> <gi>supplied</gi> <gi>support</gi> <gi>surrogates</gi> <gi>taxonomy</gi> <gi>term</gi>
              <gi>textClass</gi> <gi>textLang</gi> <gi>unclear</gi> <gi>width</gi> <gi>witness</gi>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row corresp="#tier-export-only">
            <cell>export only</cell>
            <cell>
              <gi>citeData</gi> <gi>citeStructure</gi> <gi>link</gi>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </table>
        <schemaSpec ident="solemne" start="TEI" docLang="en" targetLang="en" xml:id="element-outside-the-schema" corresp="#tier-required">

          <!-- The modules, and the elements taken from each. -->
          <moduleRef key="tei"/>
          <moduleRef key="core" include="abbr add addrLine address analytic author bibl biblScope biblStruct choice cit citedRange corr               date del desc editor email expan foreign gap graphic head imprint item label lb list listBibl measure               monogr name note num orig p pb postCode ptr publisher pubPlace q quote ref reg resp respStmt rs series sic               street term textLang title unclear hi"/>
          <moduleRef key="header" include="abstract authority availability calendar calendarDesc catDesc category change citeData               citeStructure classDecl correction creation distributor edition editionStmt editorialDecl encodingDesc               extent fileDesc funder handNote idno interpretation keywords langUsage language licence listChange listPrefixDef normalization               notesStmt prefixDef principal profileDesc projectDesc publicationStmt punctuation quotation refsDecl               revisionDesc               samplingDecl segmentation seriesStmt sourceDesc sponsor stdVals taxonomy teiHeader textClass               titleStmt"/>
          <moduleRef key="textstructure" include="TEI body div text"/>
          <moduleRef key="namesdates" include="addName affiliation birth bloc country death district event eventName floruit forename genName               geo geogName listEvent listOrg listPerson listPlace listRelation location nameLink occupation org               orgName persName person place placeName region relation roleName settlement surname"/>
          <moduleRef key="msdescription" include="acquisition additional additions adminInfo altIdentifier binding bindingDesc collation collection               colophon condition decoDesc decoNote depth dimensions explicit finalRubric foliation handDesc height               history incipit institution layout layoutDesc locus locusGrp msContents msDesc msIdentifier msItem msName               msPart objectDesc origDate origin origPlace physDesc provenance recordHist repository rubric source               summary support supportDesc surrogates width"/>
          <moduleRef key="linking" include="anchor annotation link linkGrp listAnnotation seg standOff"/>
          <moduleRef key="transcr" include="subst supplied"/>
          <moduleRef key="textcrit" include="listWit witness"/>
          <moduleRef key="iso-fs" include="fs f"/>

          <!-- Each part's specifications, in the parts' order. -->
          <specGrpRef target="#header-title-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#header-pointers-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#header-encoding-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#header-external-ids-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#text-divisions-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#text-headings-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#text-page-breaks-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#text-gaps-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#marked-names-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#marked-works-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#marked-statements-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#marked-pointers-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#marked-notes-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#marked-other-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#records-persons-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#records-institutions-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#records-events-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#records-works-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#records-external-ids-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#relations-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#relations-types-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#dates-form-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#dates-calendars-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#dates-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-description-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-units-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-contents-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-physical-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-hands-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-decoration-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-binding-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#manuscripts-history-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#editions-pages-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#editions-biblatex-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#exports-annotations-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#exports-reuse-specs"/>
          <specGrpRef target="#exports-history-specs"/>

        </schemaSpec>
      </div>
    </body>
    <back>
      <!-- solemne-tei docs generates appendices A, B, C and F in place of these placeholders: A and B from the
           specifications, C from the package's code tables and the vocabularies' seeds, F from the changelog. -->
      <divGen xml:id="appendix-elements" type="elements">
        <head>The elements and attributes</head>
      </divGen>
      <divGen xml:id="appendix-rules" type="rules">
        <head>The rules</head>
      </divGen>
      <divGen xml:id="appendix-tables" type="tables">
        <head>The code tables and the vocabularies</head>
      </divGen>
      <div xml:id="appendix-older-forms">
        <head>Older encodings</head>
        <p>The project's files were encoded before this guide, after the old Solemne guide, and some of their forms
          have changed. The corrections bring the project's own files to the guide's forms; this table is for anyone
          reading a file in an older form. Each row gives the older form, what it became, and where the guide
          explains it.</p>
        <table xml:id="older-forms-table">
          <row role="label">
            <cell>The older form</cell>
            <cell>What it became</cell>
            <cell>Where</cell>
          </row>
          <row role="label">
            <cell cols="3">The header</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>The formal title's <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>collCode</val>, <val>collCat</val> and
              <val>collType</val></cell>
            <cell>an untyped <gi>idno</gi> for the code, <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>category</val> and
              <val>type</val></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#header-title"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>The category <val>DP</val></cell>
            <cell><val>D</val></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#header-categories"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>A date joined to a code by a dot: <code>P.THB.0700</code></cell>
            <cell>joined by a hyphen: <code>P.THB-0700</code></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#header-codes"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><att>key</att> on <gi>title</gi>, <gi>author</gi>, <gi>origPlace</gi> and <gi>country</gi>;
              <att>corresp</att> on <gi>provenance</gi>; persons by short keys (<code>who="#GS"</code>)</cell>
            <cell><att>ref</att>, pointing to a record by a placeholder, an id in the file or a permalink; in
              <gi>provenance</gi>, on the name inside it</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#header-responsibility"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Clavis's keys as <att>xml:id</att>s</cell>
            <cell>in <att>sameAs</att>, with the <code>clavis</code> prefix declared in <gi>listPrefixDef</gi></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#header-external-ids"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>The root's <att>xml:id</att> as the document's address (<code>collection_1234</code>); the
              document as one <gi>div</gi> typed <val>collection</val></cell>
            <cell>the document's code in the formal title; the <gi>body</gi> is the document</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#documents-and-nodes"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row role="label">
            <cell cols="3">The text's structure</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>div</gi> typed <val>atomicUnit</val></cell>
            <cell><val>unit</val>, where several stand side by side; a lone one dissolves into its parent</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-units"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>div</gi> typed <val>opener</val></cell>
            <cell>dissolved: its headings are the headings of the division it opens</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-headings"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>div</gi> typed <val>chapterGroup</val>, and untyped divisions</cell>
            <cell><val>chapter</val>: a chapter may hold chapters</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-divisions"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>div</gi> typed <val>part</val>, at the top</cell>
            <cell><val>section</val></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-divisions"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>The old guide's division types: <val>canon</val>, <val>norm</val>, <val>authGrp</val>,
              <val>authStmt</val>, <val>authStmtGrp</val>, <val>decretal</val>, <val>registerCanon</val>,
              <val>romanLaw</val>, <val>act</val></cell>
            <cell>the closed list of division types</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-divisions"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>A file that is only a register, its items in the body</cell>
            <cell>one <gi>div</gi> typed <val>register</val> around its items</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-registers"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>head</gi> typed <val>num</val></cell>
            <cell><val>number</val>, the division's number as a value in <att>n</att></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-numbers"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>head</gi> typed <val>inscriptio</val>; the old guide's <val>denom</val>,
              <val>highlight</val> and <val>paragr</val></cell>
            <cell><val>inscription</val> where the heading begins with its source, <val>rubric</val> where it begins
              with its subject</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-headings"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Misspelt types (<val>chpater</val>, <val>inscripto</val>, <val>atoimcUnit</val>,
              <val>souce</val>)</cell>
            <cell>the closed lists' values: a misspelt type fails the RelaxNG</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#text-divisions"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>gap</gi> with <att>reason</att> <val>missing</val></cell>
            <cell><val>lacuna</val></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#transcription-gaps"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row role="label">
            <cell cols="3">What is marked in the text</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><gi>note</gi> typed <val>corresp</val>; untyped notes; the old guide's <val>filiation</val>,
              <val>physDesc</val>, <val>warning</val>, <val>comment</val>, <val>memo</val> and <val>desc</val></cell>
            <cell><val>parallel</val>; <val>editorial</val> or <val>source</val>, by what the note does</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#marked-notes"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>A source note's codes in its words</cell>
            <cell>in its <att>source</att></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#marked-notes"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Codes in older styles: <code>CNIC.325.2</code>; <code>CanApost_N</code>, <code>Apost_N</code>,
              <code>SEA_N</code>; <code>DCEL.422.369</code>, whose year isn't the file's; pointers to the
              standOff's entries (<code>quote source="#CNIC"</code>)</cell>
            <cell>codes and citations as the coding rules make them: <code>C.NIC-0325_2</code>;
              <code>COLL.CanApost_N</code>, <code>COLL.SEA_N</code>; <code>D.CEL-0428-369</code>, by the table of
              files</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#marked-pointers"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Placeholders without their <code>#</code>, or capitalised</cell>
            <cell><code>#new_person_…</code>, in lower case before the encoder's name</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#records-ids"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>One <gi>quote</gi> around several paragraphs</cell>
            <cell>fragments, one in each paragraph, joined by <att>next</att> and <att>prev</att></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#marked-fragments"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row role="label">
            <cell cols="3">Records, relations and dates</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>An authority's records as links, in a <gi>note</gi> typed <val>links</val>; its bare numbers, in a
              <gi>note</gi> typed <val>authorities</val></cell>
            <cell><gi>idno</gi> typed by the authority (<code>&lt;idno type="mirabile"&gt;19840&lt;/idno&gt;</code>)</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#records-external-ids"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Relations after SAWS and the project's own terms, their ends pointing to the standOff's
              entries</cell>
            <cell>the model's relation types, each with its permalink; SAWS's under "is a text of"</cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#relations-types"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><att>calendar</att> <val>Gregorian</val>, undeclared</cell>
            <cell><code>#gregorian</code>, pointing to a <gi>calendar</gi> declared in <gi>calendarDesc</gi></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#dates-calendars"/></cell>
          </row>
          <row role="label">
            <cell cols="3">Manuscripts</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>The Bodleian's description: the whole manuscript's physical description and contents;
              <gi>num</gi>; <gi>filiation</gi>; <gi>msItem</gi>/<att>class</att>; an untyped
              <gi>altIdentifier</gi> as a unit's identifier</cell>
            <cell>the guide's description: physical description and contents on the units; <gi>num</gi>'s words
              kept as words; relations between the texts; the unit's own <gi>idno</gi></cell>
            <cell><ptr target="#manuscripts-bodleian"/>, and the table in <ptr target="#appendix-mappings"/></cell>
          </row>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div xml:id="appendix-mappings">
        <head>The import formats</head>
        <p>Others' TEI comes in through its own schema, then a converter into the guide's forms (<ptr target="#manuscripts-bodleian"/>). For each format, its table says what the conversion does with each of
          its elements and attributes: keeps it, moves it, makes a record or a relation of it, or drops it, and
          why.</p>
        <table xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xml:id="bodleian-mapping">
  <head>From the Bodleian's description to ours: what the conversion does with each element and attribute</head>
  <row role="label">
    <cell>The Bodleian's</cell>
    <cell>Ours</cell>
    <cell>The conversion</cell>
  </row>
  <row role="label">
    <cell cols="3">The description and the manuscript's identifier</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msDesc</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, with one unit at least</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it. Its <att>xml:id</att> goes: the import gives the manuscript's record its own id.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msDesc</gi>/<att>xml:lang</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it: the language of the description's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msIdentifier</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>country</gi>, <gi>region</gi>, <gi>settlement</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, each with <att>ref</att> to the place's record</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them; <att>key</att> (<val>place_1000070</val>) becomes <att>ref</att> to a placeholder of the place's record, which the import makes.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>region</gi>/<att>type</att></cell>
    <cell>TEI's</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>institution</gi>, <gi>repository</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, with <att>ref</att> to the institution's record</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them, and makes the library's record.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>collection</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same: the fonds, with <att>ref</att></cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>idno</gi> typed <val>shelfmark</val></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, as it's written.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>idno</gi> typed by an authority (<val>ieArk</val>, <val>crArk</val>)</cell>
    <cell>the same, in <gi>msIdentifier</gi></cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>altIdentifier</gi></cell>
    <cell>typed <val>former</val>, <val>siglum</val> or <val>code</val></cell>
    <cell>Keeps a former shelfmark. The Summary Catalogue's number, typed <val>internal</val>, becomes an <gi>idno</gi> typed <val>SCN</val> in <gi>msIdentifier</gi>. A full shelfmark is dropped: the identifier's parts say it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>altIdentifier</gi>/<att>type</att></cell>
    <cell><val>former</val>, <val>siglum</val>, <val>code</val></cell>
    <cell>Keeps <val>former</val>; the others as the row above says.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msName</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it: the manuscript's names.</cell>
  </row>
  <row role="label">
    <cell cols="3">Units</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msPart</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same; one at least, none inside another</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it. A description without units gets one, holding the manuscript's contents, physical description and history; a unit inside a unit becomes a unit of the manuscript.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msPart</gi>/<att>n</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msPart</gi>/<gi>msIdentifier</gi></cell>
    <cell>the unit's own identifier, an <gi>idno</gi></cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; an <gi>altIdentifier</gi> holding the full shelfmark and the unit's leaves becomes an <gi>idno</gi> with the unit's letter or leaves.</cell>
  </row>
  <row role="label">
    <cell cols="3">Contents</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msContents</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, on a unit</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; on the manuscript, it moves into the unit.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>summary</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>textLang</gi></cell>
    <cell>TEI's</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, with its languages.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msItem</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same; items inside items</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msItem</gi>/<att>n</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msItem</gi>/<att>class</att></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops it: an item's category is its work's.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>locus</gi>, <gi>locusGrp</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them; <gi>hi</gi> inside a <gi>locus</gi> keeps only its words.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>author</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, with <att>ref</att> to the person's record</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; <att>key</att> (<val>person_…</val>) becomes <att>ref</att> to a placeholder, and the import makes the person's record.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>title</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, with <att>ref</att> to the work's record</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; <att>key</att> (<val>work_…</val>) becomes <att>ref</att>, and the import makes the work's record. <att>level</att> stays on a title in a bibliography.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>rubric</gi>, <gi>incipit</gi>, <gi>explicit</gi>, <gi>finalRubric</gi>, <gi>colophon</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them, with their words; an empty one is dropped.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>filiation</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops it: a copy's place among the others is a relation between the texts.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>note</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; its type, where it has one of the list's.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>bibl</gi> in an item</cell>
    <cell>the literature's forms</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, in the forms of editions and literature.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msItemStruct</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Becomes an <gi>msItem</gi>.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>msFrag</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops it: a fragment kept elsewhere has a description of its own.</cell>
  </row>
  <row role="label">
    <cell cols="3">The physical description</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>physDesc</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, on a unit; on the manuscript, the binding alone</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it on a unit. On the manuscript, the binding stays and the rest moves into the units that lack it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>p</gi> in <gi>physDesc</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>objectDesc</gi>/<att>form</att></cell>
    <cell>the same, from the vocabulary of forms</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>supportDesc</gi>/<att>material</att></cell>
    <cell>the same, from the vocabulary of materials</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>support</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>extent</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, its words and dimensions.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>num</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out in a description</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tag, keeps its words: the leaves are written as words.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>dimensions</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, its unit required</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, with its type and unit.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>dimensions</gi>/<att>type</att>, <att>unit</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>height</gi>, <gi>width</gi>, <gi>depth</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them; an empty one is dropped.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>dim</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tag, keeps its words in the extent's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>foliation</gi></cell>
    <cell>TEI's</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; its date goes into its prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>collation</gi></cell>
    <cell>TEI's</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>collation</gi>/<att>mainStructures</att></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops it, into the collation's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>catchwords</gi>, <gi>signatures</gi>, <gi>secFol</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tags, keeps their words in the collation's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>condition</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>layoutDesc</gi>, <gi>layout</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>layout</gi>/<att>columns</att>, <att>ruledLines</att>, <att>writtenLines</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them; a count of zero is dropped.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>layout</gi>/<att>topLine</att>, <att>rulingMedium</att></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops them, into the layout's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>handDesc</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>handDesc</gi>/<att>hands</att></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops it: the hands are counted from their notes.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>handNote</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, with an id</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, with its <att>script</att>, <att>execution</att> and <att>scope</att>.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>handNote</gi>/<att>scribe</att>, <att>scribeRef</att></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Makes the relation <val>scribe</val> to the person's record, named in the hand's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>handNote</gi>/<att>scriptRef</att>, <att>medium</att></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops them, into the hand's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>scriptDesc</gi>, <gi>scriptNote</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tags, keeps their words in the hands' prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>typeDesc</gi>, <gi>typeNote</gi>, <gi>musicNotation</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tags, keeps their words in the unit's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>decoDesc</gi>, <gi>decoNote</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, its type from the vocabulary of decoration</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>additions</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>bindingDesc</gi>, <gi>binding</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, on the manuscript</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them on the manuscript, with <att>contemporary</att> and the binding's dates; a unit's earlier binding becomes a note on the unit.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>binding</gi>/<att>notBefore</att>, <att>notAfter</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>sealDesc</gi>, <gi>seal</gi>, <gi>accMat</gi>, <gi>watermark</gi>, <gi>heraldry</gi>, <gi>stamp</gi>, <gi>material</gi>, <gi>objectType</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tags, keeps their words in the prose of the part they stand in.</cell>
  </row>
  <row role="label">
    <cell cols="3">History</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>history</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, on a unit; on the manuscript for the whole book</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>origin</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, and makes the relation <val>origin</val>.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>origDate</gi></cell>
    <cell>the one form of date</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, its words and bounds.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>origDate</gi>/<att>calendar</att></cell>
    <cell>the same, pointing to a declared calendar</cell>
    <cell>Becomes <val>#gregorian</val> for the Bodleian's <val>#Gregorian</val>, declared in the file, and <val>#julian</val> for its <val>#Julian</val>.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>origDate</gi>/<att>notBefore</att>, <att>notAfter</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>origPlace</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, with <att>ref</att> to the place's record</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; <att>key</att> becomes <att>ref</att>.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>provenance</gi>, <gi>acquisition</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, the owner named inside</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them, with their dates, and makes the relation <val>ownedBy</val> to the owner's record.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>provenance</gi>/<att>corresp</att>, <att>key</att></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Becomes <att>ref</att> on the owner's name inside.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>provenance</gi>/<att>notBefore</att>, <att>notAfter</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>custodialHist</gi>, <gi>custEvent</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tags, keeps their words in the condition's or the binding's prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row role="label">
    <cell cols="3">Additional</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>additional</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>adminInfo</gi>, <gi>recordHist</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, the record's history alone</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>source</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, holding the description's source</cell>
    <cell>Becomes a <gi>bibl</gi> typed <val>source</val>: the Bodleian's catalogue, the description's id and link there, the date retrieved, and its licence.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>bibl</gi>/<att>type</att>, <att>facs</att></cell>
    <cell>the source's form</cell>
    <cell>The catalogue's pages become the source's pages.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>availability</gi> in <gi>adminInfo</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out</cell>
    <cell>Drops it: the licence goes with the source.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>surrogates</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it; a IIIF manifest becomes a <gi>ref</gi> typed <val>iiif</val>.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>listBibl</gi></cell>
    <cell>the literature's forms</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, in the forms of editions and literature.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>biblScope</gi>, <gi>pubPlace</gi>, <gi>date</gi> in a bibliography</cell>
    <cell>the literature's forms</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>biblScope</gi>/<att>unit</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row role="label">
    <cell cols="3">Inside the prose</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>persName</gi>, <gi>placeName</gi>, <gi>orgName</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same, with <att>ref</att></cell>
    <cell>Keeps them; <att>key</att> becomes <att>ref</att>, and the import makes the records.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>title</gi>/<att>key</att>, <gi>author</gi>/<att>key</att>, <gi>country</gi>/<att>key</att>, <gi>origPlace</gi>/<att>key</att></cell>
    <cell><att>ref</att></cell>
    <cell>Becomes <att>ref</att> to a placeholder of the record.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>title</gi>/<att>level</att></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>hi</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>date</gi> in the prose</cell>
    <cell>the one form of date</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>foreign</gi>, <gi>ref</gi></cell>
    <cell>the same</cell>
    <cell>Keeps them.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>am</gi>, <gi>ex</gi>, <gi>supplied</gi>, <gi>damage</gi>, <gi>fw</gi>, <gi>figure</gi>, <gi>formula</gi>, <gi>lg</gi>, <gi>l</gi>, <gi>measure</gi>, <gi>quote</gi>, <gi>seg</gi></cell>
    <cell>left out in a description</cell>
    <cell>Drops the tags, keeps their words: quoting the manuscript is what transcriptions do.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><gi>idno</gi>/<att>type</att>, <gi>note</gi>/<att>type</att></cell>
    <cell>the closed lists</cell>
    <cell>Keeps a type of the lists; another becomes the nearest, or is said in the prose.</cell>
  </row>
  <row>
    <cell><att>xml:id</att></cell>
    <cell>the encoder's</cell>
    <cell>Keeps it, for pointers within the file.</cell>
  </row>
</table>
      </div>
      <divGen xml:id="appendix-changes" type="changes">
        <head>The changes</head>
      </divGen>
      <div xml:id="example-sources">
        <head>The sources of the examples</head>
        <p>The guide's examples quote short excerpts of the project's corpus, cut where the point is made and revised to
          the forms this guide decides; marks that don't concern the point may be left out. Each example names its
          file here, by
          <att>source</att>: the file's code, the text, and the edition or the manuscript it was transcribed from. An
          example without a source is made up, where the corpus has nothing to show, and so are the
          counter-examples.</p>
        <listBibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.AAC-0816"><idno>C.AAC-0816</idno>: <title>Council of Aachen, 816</title>, ed.
            <editor>Albert Werminghoff</editor>, <title>Concilia aevi Karolini</title> I, MGH Concilia 2.1 (Hannover
            and Leipzig, <date>1906</date>), pp. 307–464.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.AAC-0836"><idno>C.AAC-0836</idno>: <title>Council of Aachen, 836</title>, ed.
            <editor>Albert Werminghoff</editor>, <title>Concilia aevi Karolini</title> II, MGH Concilia 2.2 (Hannover
            and Leipzig, <date>1908</date>), pp. 704–767.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.ASC-0756"><idno>C.ASC-0756</idno>: <title>Council of Aschheim, 756 or
              755–760</title>, ed. <editor>Albert Werminghoff</editor>, <title>Concilia aevi Karolini</title> I, MGH
            Concilia 2.1 (Hannover and Leipzig, <date>1906</date>), pp. 56–58.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.CAR-0397"><idno>C.CAR-0397</idno>: <title>Council of Carthage, 397</title>, ed.
            <editor>Charles Munier</editor>, <title>Concilia Africae a. 345 – a. 525</title>, CCSL 149 (Turnhout,
            <date>1974</date>), pp. 326–341: the file's header and the records of its standOff, the project's
            own.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.MAI-0813"><idno>C.MAI-0813</idno>: <title>Council of Mainz, 813</title>, ed.
            <editor>Albert Werminghoff</editor>, <title>Concilia aevi Karolini</title> I, MGH Concilia 2.1 (Hannover
            and Leipzig, <date>1906</date>), pp. 258–273.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.ROM-0826"><idno>C.ROM-0826</idno>: <title>Council of Rome, 826</title>, ed.
            <editor>Albert Werminghoff</editor>, <title>Concilia aevi Karolini</title> II, MGH Concilia 2.2 (Hannover
            and Leipzig, <date>1908</date>), pp. 552–583.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.SOI-0744"><idno>C.SOI-0744</idno>: <title>Council of Soissons, 744</title>, ed.
            <editor>Albert Werminghoff</editor>, <title>Concilia aevi Karolini</title> I, MGH Concilia 2.1 (Hannover
            and Leipzig, <date>1906</date>), pp. 33–36.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.SOI-0744-export"><idno>C.SOI-0744</idno>, exported: <title>Council of Soissons,
              744</title>, from the edition of <editor>Albert Werminghoff</editor>, as the template
            <code>export.xml</code> gives it, as the application would export it; the ids, the records' numbers, the
            release and the hit of text reuse are made up.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.VLA-0549"><idno>C.VLA-0549</idno>: <title>Council of Valencia, 549</title>, ed.
            <editor>José Vives</editor>, <title>Concilios visigóticos e hispano-romanos</title>, España Cristiana 1
            (Barcelona and Madrid, <date>1963</date>), pp. 61–64.</bibl>
          <bibl xml:id="source-C.CON-0381-v5"><idno>C.CON-0381-v5</idno>: <title>First Council of Constantinople,
              381</title>, ed. <editor>Adolf Martin Ritter</editor>, in <title>The oecumenical councils: from Nicaea I
              to Nicaea II (325-787)</title>, ed. <editor>Giuseppe Alberigo</editor>, Corpus Christianorum,
            Conciliorum Oecumenicorum Generaliumque Decreta 1 (Turnhout, <date>2006</date>), pp. 35–70: the file's
